window.UNITS=[{"id":"u1","title":"Economic Thinking","day":1,"summary":"Learn scarcity, opportunity cost, marginal thinking, the production possibilities frontier, positive versus normative statements, the circular flow, and how managers apply economic reasoning.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Scarcity: the reason economics exists","body":"<p><strong>Scarcity</strong> means resources are limited but human wants are unlimited. Resources (also called <strong>factors of production</strong>) are <strong>land</strong> (natural resources), <strong>labor</strong>, <strong>capital</strong> (machines, buildings, software), and <strong>entrepreneurship</strong> (the risk-taking that combines the other three).</p><p>Because you cannot have everything, you must <strong>choose</strong>. Economics is the study of how people, firms, and governments make choices when resources are scarce.</p><p><strong>Business example:</strong> your department has a $200,000 budget and 10 employees. You cannot launch every project on the wish list. Even a billion-dollar company faces scarcity, because management time, engineers, factory capacity, and cash are all limited. Scarcity is <em>not</em> the same as a shortage: a shortage is a market problem at one particular price, while scarcity is a permanent condition of every society.</p>"},{"h":"2. Opportunity cost and sunk costs","body":"<p><strong>Opportunity cost</strong> is the value of the <em>next-best alternative you give up</em> when you make a choice. It is not the sum of everything you gave up, only the single best thing.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> you can fund Project X (return $120,000), Project Y ($90,000), or Project Z ($100,000). You pick X. Your opportunity cost is $100,000 (Z), not $90,000 and not $190,000.</p><p>Opportunity costs include <strong>explicit costs</strong> (cash you pay out) and <strong>implicit costs</strong> (the value of resources you already own and use, such as an owner's time). A shop owner who quits a $70,000 job to run her own store has a $70,000 implicit cost every year.</p><p>A <strong>sunk cost</strong> is money already spent that cannot be recovered whatever you decide now. Rational decisions <strong>ignore sunk costs</strong> because they are the same under every option. Only <strong>future</strong> costs and benefits that <em>differ</em> between options matter. Continuing a failing project \"because we already spent so much\" is the sunk-cost fallacy.</p>"},{"h":"3. Marginal analysis and incentives","body":"<p>Most good decisions are not all-or-nothing; they are \"a little more or a little less.\" <strong>Marginal</strong> means <em>additional</em>. <strong>Marginal benefit (MB)</strong> is the extra benefit from one more unit; <strong>marginal cost (MC)</strong> is the extra cost of one more unit.</p><p><strong>Rule:</strong> do more of an activity as long as <code>MB &gt; MC</code> (or at least <code>MB &gt;= MC</code>); stop when MB falls below MC. The best level is where the two are about equal. Benefits usually shrink as you do more (the 1st ad reaches new customers; the 50th reaches the same people), and costs often rise, so a stopping point exists.</p><p><strong>Incentives</strong> are rewards and penalties that change behavior. People respond to incentives, including in ways managers did not intend. Pay call-center agents per call handled and calls get shorter, but customers may be less satisfied. Good managers design incentives, then ask \"what will people <em>actually</em> do?\"</p>"},{"h":"4. The production possibilities frontier (PPF)","body":"<p>The <strong>production possibilities frontier</strong> shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy (or a firm) can produce using all its resources efficiently with current technology.</p><ul><li><strong>On the curve:</strong> productively efficient. You can only make more of one good by making less of the other. That tradeoff <em>is</em> opportunity cost.</li><li><strong>Inside the curve:</strong> attainable but inefficient; resources are idle or wasted (a plant running at 70% capacity, high unemployment).</li><li><strong>Outside the curve:</strong> unattainable with current resources and technology.</li></ul><p>The PPF is usually <strong>bowed outward (concave)</strong> because of <strong>increasing opportunity cost</strong>: resources are not equally good at making both goods, so the first units moved are cheap and later units are expensive. A straight-line PPF means constant opportunity cost.</p><p><strong>Growth:</strong> more resources or better technology <strong>shifts the PPF outward</strong>. Technology that helps only one good <em>pivots</em> the curve. Putting idle resources to work is a <em>movement</em> from inside the curve to the curve, not a shift. <strong>Allocative efficiency</strong> means producing the mix that society values most (where MB equals MC), which is a different idea from simply being on the curve.</p>"},{"h":"5. Micro vs macro, positive vs normative","body":"<p><strong>Microeconomics</strong> studies individual decision-makers: households, firms, and single markets (pricing a product, hiring decisions, how a tax changes the price of coffee). <strong>Macroeconomics</strong> studies the whole economy: GDP, inflation, unemployment, interest rates, and growth.</p><p>A <strong>positive statement</strong> describes what <em>is</em> or <em>will be</em>. It can be tested with data, and it can still be wrong. Example: \"Raising the minimum wage to $15 will reduce teen employment by 3%.\" A <strong>normative statement</strong> expresses a value judgment about what <em>ought</em> to be. It contains words like \"should,\" \"fair,\" or \"better.\" Example: \"The government should raise the minimum wage.\"</p><p>Economists use positive analysis to predict effects; people then apply their values to decide whether the effects are desirable. As a manager, separate \"what will happen\" (evidence) from \"what we should do\" (judgment).</p>"},{"h":"6. Circular flow and economic systems","body":"<p>The <strong>circular flow model</strong> shows how households and firms exchange through two markets.</p><ul><li><strong>Product (goods and services) markets:</strong> firms sell, households buy. Money flows from households to firms as consumer spending (revenue to firms).</li><li><strong>Factor (resource) markets:</strong> households sell labor, land, capital, and entrepreneurship; firms buy them. Money flows from firms to households as wages, rent, interest, and profit (income).</li></ul><p>In the expanded model, <strong>savings, taxes, and imports</strong> are <em>leakages</em> out of the flow and <strong>investment, government spending, and exports</strong> are <em>injections</em> into it.</p><p>An <strong>economic system</strong> answers: what to produce, how, and for whom. In a <strong>market economy</strong> private owners and prices coordinate decisions through voluntary exchange. In a <strong>command economy</strong> a central authority plans and allocates. Most real economies are <strong>mixed</strong>: mostly markets with government rules, taxes, and public goods.</p>"},{"h":"7. How a manager uses economic reasoning","body":"<p>Economic thinking becomes a checklist for everyday decisions.</p><ol><li><strong>Name the tradeoff.</strong> What is the next-best use of this money, time, or capacity?</li><li><strong>Think at the margin.</strong> Compare the <em>extra</em> benefit to the <em>extra</em> cost of the next unit, hire, ad, or shift.</li><li><strong>Ignore sunk costs.</strong> Only future, differing costs and benefits count.</li><li><strong>Count implicit costs.</strong> Owned resources are not free.</li><li><strong>Predict reactions.</strong> Customers, employees, and competitors respond to incentives.</li><li><strong>Separate facts from values.</strong> Test positive claims with data before debating normative ones.</li></ol><p>Example: a plant is at average cost $12 per unit and a customer offers $10 for a rush order that adds only $6 of extra cost, with spare capacity. Average cost says \"decline\"; marginal thinking says \"accept, you gain $4 per unit.\" That is the power of marginal analysis.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Scarcity means resources are limited while human wants are unlimited, so every person, company, and country must make choices.","The four factors of production are land, labor, capital, and entrepreneurship.","Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative you give up, not the total of everything you give up.","Implicit costs are the opportunity costs of resources you already own, such as an owner's own time, and they are real costs even though no cash leaves your pocket.","A sunk cost is money already spent that cannot be recovered, and a rational manager ignores it when deciding what to do next.","Marginal analysis compares marginal benefit with marginal cost. Keep doing more of something as long as the extra benefit is at least as large as the extra cost.","Incentives are rewards and penalties. People respond to them, sometimes in ways the manager did not intend.","The production possibilities frontier shows the maximum combinations of two goods that can be produced with all resources used efficiently. Points on the curve are efficient, points inside are wasteful, and points outside are unattainable.","The frontier is usually bowed outward because of increasing opportunity cost. More resources or better technology shift the whole frontier outward, which is economic growth.","Microeconomics studies individual households, firms, and markets. Macroeconomics studies the whole economy, including output, inflation, and unemployment.","A positive statement can be tested with facts and describes what is. A normative statement is a value judgment about what ought to be, usually with words like should.","In the circular flow, households sell resources in factor markets and buy goods in product markets. Market economies use prices and private ownership, command economies use central planning, and most real economies are mixed."],"examples":[{"q":"A firm can produce laptops and phones (both in thousands) at these efficient combinations: A (0 laptops, 120 phones), B (20, 100), C (40, 70), D (60, 30), E (70, 0). What is the opportunity cost of one extra laptop when moving from B to C, and from C to D? What does the pattern show?","steps":["Opportunity cost per laptop = phones given up divided by laptops gained.","B to C: phones fall from 100 to 70, which is 30 fewer. Laptops rise from 20 to 40, which is 20 more. 30 divided by 20 = 1.5 phones per laptop.","C to D: phones fall from 70 to 30, which is 40 fewer. Laptops rise by 20. 40 divided by 20 = 2.0 phones per laptop.","The cost per laptop is rising (1.0 from A to B, 1.5, then 2.0, then 3.0 from D to E)."],"answer":"1.5 phones per laptop (B to C) and 2.0 phones per laptop (C to D). Rising opportunity cost is why the PPF is bowed outward."},{"q":"A delivery company considers adding vans. The marginal benefit (extra monthly profit before van costs) of the 1st, 2nd, 3rd, and 4th van is $900, $700, $500, and $300. Each van costs $450 per month to run. How many vans should it add?","steps":["Compare MB with MC for each van: 1st: 900 vs 450 (add). 2nd: 700 vs 450 (add). 3rd: 500 vs 450 (add, gain $50).","4th: 300 vs 450 (do not add, it loses $150).","Stop at the last unit where MB is at least MC."],"answer":"3 vans. Total net gain = (900-450) + (700-450) + (500-450) = $750 per month."},{"q":"Your company paid a non-refundable $200,000 for a software license (already spent). Option 1: keep using it, with $50,000 of added setup cost and $80,000 of benefits. Option 2: switch to new software with $60,000 of added cost and $130,000 of benefits. Which should you choose?","steps":["The $200,000 is a sunk cost. It is the same under both options, so ignore it.","Option 1 net = 80,000 - 50,000 = $30,000.","Option 2 net = 130,000 - 60,000 = $70,000.","Option 2 is better by 70,000 - 30,000 = $40,000."],"answer":"Switch to the new software (net $70,000 versus $30,000). The sunk $200,000 should not influence the choice."}],"videos":[{"title":"Scarcity, opportunity cost, and the PPF","query":"scarcity opportunity cost production possibilities frontier explained economics"},{"title":"Marginal analysis and sunk costs","query":"marginal benefit marginal cost sunk cost fallacy economics business"},{"title":"Positive vs normative statements and the circular flow","query":"positive vs normative economics circular flow model explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Scarcity","b":"Limited resources but unlimited wants, forcing choices"},{"f":"Four factors of production","b":"Land, labor, capital, entrepreneurship"},{"f":"Opportunity cost","b":"Value of the next-best alternative given up"},{"f":"Explicit cost","b":"Out-of-pocket cash payment for a resource"},{"f":"Implicit cost","b":"Opportunity cost of owned resources, such as owner's time"},{"f":"Sunk cost","b":"Already spent and unrecoverable; ignore in decisions"},{"f":"Marginal","b":"The additional or extra unit"},{"f":"Marginal benefit","b":"Extra benefit from one more unit"},{"f":"Marginal cost","b":"Extra cost of producing or doing one more unit"},{"f":"Marginal decision rule","b":"Do more while MB is at least MC; stop when MB falls below MC"},{"f":"Incentive","b":"Reward or penalty that changes behavior"},{"f":"PPF","b":"Curve of maximum combinations of two goods with efficient use of resources"},{"f":"Point inside the PPF","b":"Attainable but inefficient; idle or wasted resources"},{"f":"Point outside the PPF","b":"Unattainable with current resources and technology"},{"f":"Why is the PPF bowed outward?","b":"Increasing opportunity cost; resources are not equally suited to both goods"},{"f":"What shifts the PPF outward?","b":"More resources or better technology (economic growth)"},{"f":"Microeconomics vs macroeconomics","b":"Individual households, firms, markets vs whole-economy output, prices, jobs"},{"f":"Positive statement","b":"Testable description of what is; can be false"},{"f":"Normative statement","b":"Value judgment about what ought to be; uses 'should'"},{"f":"Product market vs factor market","b":"Product: firms sell goods to households. Factor: households sell resources to firms"},{"f":"Command vs market economy","b":"Central planning vs prices, private ownership, and voluntary exchange"},{"f":"Leakages and injections","b":"Leakages: savings, taxes, imports. Injections: investment, government spending, exports"}],"questions":[{"q":"Which statement best defines scarcity?","o":["Only low-income countries lack enough goods and services","A product sells out at the current market price","Prices of essential goods rise faster than wages","Resources are limited while human wants are unlimited"],"a":3,"e":"Scarcity is a universal condition: limited resources versus unlimited wants. Running out of a product at one price is a shortage, a market symptom that can disappear if the price changes, so it is the tempting but wrong choice.","d":1},{"q":"Opportunity cost is best described as","o":["the sum of every alternative that was rejected","the value of the next-best alternative given up","the cost of an option minus its benefits","the total dollars spent on the chosen option"],"a":1,"e":"Opportunity cost counts only the single best forgone alternative. Summing all rejected options overstates it, and the dollars spent are just the explicit cost.","d":1},{"q":"Which of the following is a positive statement?","o":["A $15 minimum wage is fairer to low-wage workers across the country","Raising the minimum wage to $15 will reduce teen employment by 3 percent","Employers ought to pay all workers a living wage in every state","The government should raise the minimum wage to $15 for all full-time workers"],"a":1,"e":"A positive statement can be tested with data, even if it turns out wrong. The other three contain value judgments (should, fairer, ought), which makes them normative.","d":1},{"q":"A bank's CFO studies how a rise in the national unemployment rate will affect overall loan demand across the whole economy. This is mainly","o":["macroeconomics, because it examines economy-wide variables","microeconomics, because loans are a single financial product","microeconomics, because banks are individual firms","normative economics, because it concerns a forecast"],"a":0,"e":"Macroeconomics looks at aggregates such as unemployment, inflation, and total output. The analysis is about the whole economy, even though a single bank is asking. A forecast is positive, not normative.","d":1},{"q":"In the circular flow model, households receive wages, rent, interest, and profit from firms in the","o":["product (goods) market","government transfer market","factor (resource) market","financial futures market"],"a":2,"e":"Households sell labor, land, capital, and entrepreneurship in factor markets and receive income. In the product market they instead spend money to buy goods and services.","d":1},{"q":"In a command economy, the main decisions about what and how much to produce are made by","o":["foreign trading partners","consumers through market prices","competing private firms","a central planning authority"],"a":3,"e":"Command economies rely on central planning and allocation. Market prices and competing private firms describe a market economy.","d":1},{"q":"On a production possibilities frontier, a combination of goods located outside the curve is","o":["unattainable with current resources and technology","attainable only if market prices fall far enough","attainable, but with idle resources, so inefficient","efficient and attainable with present resources"],"a":0,"e":"Points outside the PPF require more resources or better technology than the economy has. Attainable but inefficient points lie inside the curve.","d":1},{"q":"A company announces a $500 bonus to any employee whose team has zero reported safety incidents. Economic reasoning predicts","o":["behavior will not change because employees' base pay is unaffected","employees will work fewer hours because of the bonus plan","safety will improve with certainty and no side effects","reported incidents will fall, though some may simply go unreported"],"a":3,"e":"People respond to incentives, and the reward is tied to reported incidents, which may also encourage under-reporting. Assuming no behavior change ignores incentives, and assuming certain improvement ignores unintended effects.","d":1},{"q":"A bakery owner gave up a $70,000 per year job to run her own shop. The shop earns $85,000 in revenue and has $40,000 in cash costs. Her annual implicit cost of running the shop is","o":["$15,000, revenue minus both costs","$70,000, the salary she gave up","$40,000, the cash costs","$110,000, cash costs plus the salary"],"a":1,"e":"Implicit cost is the opportunity cost of her own time: the $70,000 forgone. The $40,000 is an explicit cost. Adding them gives total economic cost (a different quantity), and $15,000 is not a cost at all.","d":2},{"q":"A manager paid a non-refundable $15,000 deposit for a trade show booth. Now a key client asks for a meeting on the same dates. Which approach is rational?","o":["Compare future benefits and costs of each option; ignore the deposit","Attend the client meeting only if it earns at least $15,000 more","Attend the trade show, since otherwise the deposit is wasted","Split the time evenly so that both commitments get some of their value"],"a":0,"e":"The deposit is sunk: it is gone whichever way you decide. Only future differing benefits and costs matter. Using the deposit as a hurdle or reason to attend is the sunk-cost fallacy.","d":2},{"q":"The marginal benefit of the 5th customer-service hire is $18,000 per year and the marginal cost is $22,000 per year. The manager should","o":["hire, because total benefits from all five hires are large","hire, because the 5th hire still has a positive benefit","not make the 5th hire, because MC exceeds MB","hire, because the average benefit per hire is high"],"a":2,"e":"Marginal analysis compares the extra benefit with the extra cost of this one hire. Since 18,000 is less than 22,000, the hire reduces net benefit by $4,000. Total or average benefits are irrelevant to the marginal choice.","d":2},{"q":"An IT team could add technicians. Extra annual benefit of the 1st, 2nd, 3rd, and 4th technician is $120,000, $95,000, $70,000, and $50,000. Each costs $65,000 per year. How many should be hired?","o":["3","1","2","4"],"a":0,"e":"Hire while MB is greater than or equal to MC: 120 and 95 and 70 all exceed 65 (hire three). The 4th adds only 50 for a cost of 65, losing 15. Hiring four ignores that last comparison.","d":2},{"q":"A plant is operating at 70 percent of capacity with some idle machines. Compared with its production possibilities frontier, the plant is","o":["on the frontier, producing efficiently","inside the frontier, producing inefficiently","outside the frontier, using new technology","at a point that cannot exist"],"a":1,"e":"Idle capacity means resources are not fully used, so output lies inside the PPF. Attainable but inefficient is exactly what an inside point means.","d":2},{"q":"A technology breakthrough doubles the maximum output of phones but does not change how many laptops can be made. The firm's PPF will","o":["not change, because only one good is affected","shift outward in a parallel way on both axes","pivot outward on the phone axis only","shift inward on the laptop axis"],"a":2,"e":"Technology that improves just one good rotates the frontier outward along that good's axis. A parallel shift would require improvements in both goods; the laptop intercept stays the same.","d":2},{"q":"The bowed-out (concave) shape of a typical PPF is explained mainly by","o":["increasing opportunity costs as resources are shifted between goods","constant opportunity costs whenever resources move between the two goods","falling prices of both goods as total output increases","the unlimited wants of consumers for both goods"],"a":0,"e":"Because resources are better suited to one good or the other, each additional unit of one good costs more units of the other. Constant costs would give a straight line.","d":2},{"q":"Which of these is a normative statement?","o":["Steel tariffs reduced steel imports last year","Car makers' costs rise when input prices rise","A tariff on imported steel is unfair to domestic car makers","A tariff on imported steel raises the price paid by car makers"],"a":2,"e":"Calling the tariff unfair is a value judgment. The other statements describe cause and effect or past facts that data could confirm or refute.","d":2},{"q":"In the expanded circular flow model, which pair are both leakages?","o":["Government spending and imports","Investment and exports","Savings and exports","Savings and taxes"],"a":3,"e":"Savings, taxes, and imports take money out of the spending flow (leakages). Investment, government spending, and exports add to it (injections). Each wrong pair mixes one leakage with an injection or has two injections.","d":2},{"q":"A call center pays agents per call handled. Average call time drops, but customer satisfaction scores fall. This best illustrates","o":["an unintended consequence of an incentive","a normative disagreement about call quality","the sunk-cost fallacy in a staffing decision","increasing opportunity cost along the PPF"],"a":0,"e":"The pay design rewarded speed, so agents shortened calls, a predictable response to incentives that hurt quality. Sunk costs and PPF tradeoffs are different ideas.","d":2},{"q":"Which feature most clearly characterizes a market economy?","o":["A central planning board sets output targets for each industry","Consumers are barred from choosing among competing products","The government owns and allocates all productive resources","Prices guide private owners' decisions through voluntary exchange"],"a":3,"e":"Market economies rely on prices, private property, and voluntary exchange to answer what, how, and for whom. The other options describe features of command planning.","d":2},{"q":"A large corporation with billions in cash still must choose which projects to fund. This shows that","o":["scarcity disappears once a firm becomes profitable","large firms face no opportunity costs on their projects","scarcity is a problem faced mainly by households","scarcity applies to every decision-maker, rich or poor"],"a":3,"e":"Wants for investment, talent, and management attention exceed any firm's resources, so every organization faces scarcity and opportunity cost. Wealth only changes the size of the tradeoffs.","d":2},{"q":"Which question is a microeconomic question?","o":["What caused the nation's total output to fall during the last recession?","How will a 10 percent price cut change sales of one company's laptop model?","Why did national inflation rise to 4 percent last year?","How do central bank interest rate changes affect national employment?"],"a":1,"e":"Microeconomics analyzes individual firms, households, and markets, such as one product's sales response to its price. Inflation, national output, and national employment are economy-wide, so they are macroeconomic.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's efficient output combinations (tablets, servers) are (0, 90), (30, 80), (60, 60), and (90, 30). Moving from (60, 60) to (90, 30), the opportunity cost of one additional tablet is","o":["0.67 server","2.0 servers","1.0 server","30 servers"],"a":2,"e":"Servers fall by 30 while tablets rise by 30, so 30 divided by 30 equals 1.0 server per tablet. 30 is the total given up, not the cost per unit, and 0.67 is the cost on the earlier segment (20 divided by 30), showing opportunity cost has increased.","d":3},{"q":"A plant spent $150,000 retooling a line (sunk). If it keeps the line, extra cost is $35,000 and benefit $60,000. If it switches to a new line, extra cost is $90,000 and benefit $130,000. The best choice and its advantage are","o":["switch, $40,000 better than keeping","keep the line, because $150,000 was already invested","switch, $15,000 better than keeping","keep the line, $115,000 better than switching"],"a":2,"e":"Ignore the sunk $150,000. Keep: 60,000 - 35,000 = $25,000. Switch: 130,000 - 90,000 = $40,000. Difference = $15,000 in favor of switching. The $40,000 answer confuses the switch net with the advantage over keeping.","d":3},{"q":"A company has spent $2,000,000 developing an app. Launching now would cost an additional $300,000 and bring in expected revenue of $450,000. The total project would lose money overall. Should it launch?","o":["Yes, because the extra revenue exceeds the extra cost by $150,000","No, because total revenue is below total spending of $2,300,000","Yes, but only if revenue also covers the $2,000,000","No, because the company should recover the $2,000,000 first"],"a":0,"e":"The $2,000,000 is sunk. The decision depends on the incremental $450,000 versus $300,000, a gain of $150,000. Not launching does not recover the sunk money, it just forgoes the $150,000.","d":3},{"q":"Project X returns $120,000, Project Y returns $90,000, and Project Z returns $100,000. A manager chooses X. The opportunity cost of that choice is","o":["$120,000","$100,000","$90,000","$190,000"],"a":1,"e":"Opportunity cost is the value of the next-best alternative, which is Z at $100,000. $190,000 adds both rejected options, and $120,000 is the benefit of the chosen project.","d":3},{"q":"A firm is producing on its PPF but making a mix of goods that customers value less than another attainable mix. The firm is","o":["neither productively nor allocatively efficient","productively efficient but not allocatively efficient","allocatively efficient but not productively efficient","both productively and allocatively efficient"],"a":1,"e":"Being on the curve means no resources are wasted (productive efficiency). Allocative efficiency additionally requires the mix to match what society values, where marginal benefit equals marginal cost.","d":3},{"q":"Which event would NOT shift an economy's production possibilities frontier outward?","o":["Idle workers are hired, moving output toward the frontier","A new technology raises output per worker in all industries","The labor force grows through immigration","The capital stock increases through new investment"],"a":0,"e":"Hiring idle workers moves the economy from inside the curve toward the curve; it is a movement, not a shift. More labor, more capital, or better technology expand what is possible and shift the frontier.","d":3},{"q":"Which of the following is true of a positive statement?","o":["It expresses what ought to happen in the economy","It can be tested against evidence, and it can still be wrong","It must be true, as it is based on facts about the economy","It cannot be tested, since it contains no value judgments"],"a":1,"e":"Positive means testable, not necessarily true. A false prediction is still positive. Value judgments about what ought to be are normative.","d":3},{"q":"A firm's plant has an average total cost of $12 per unit, including fixed costs, and idle capacity. A customer offers $10 per unit for a one-time rush order that adds only $6 per unit of extra cost. The firm should","o":["reject, because the price is below average cost","accept only if the price covers the $12 average cost","reject, because it would set a low-price precedent for all sales","accept, because each unit adds $4 of profit"],"a":3,"e":"Fixed costs are incurred whether or not the order is accepted. Comparing the $10 marginal benefit with the $6 marginal cost shows a gain of $4 per unit. Average cost is the wrong benchmark for an extra unit.","d":3},{"q":"In the circular flow, when a household spends $50,000 on goods and services in the product market, that same money is best described by the firm as","o":["a payment it receives in the factor market","a leakage that leaves the circular flow","revenue, used to pay for resources in factor markets","profit, which the firm keeps in full after the sale"],"a":2,"e":"Household spending is firm revenue; firms pay it out to households as wages, rent, and interest for resources in the factor market. Only the part left after costs is profit, and leakages refer to savings, taxes, and imports.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Scarcity","Limited resources, unlimited wants"],["Opportunity cost","Next-best alternative forgone"],["Sunk cost","Spent already; irrelevant now"],["Marginal cost","Extra cost of one more unit"],["Implicit cost","Value of owned resources used"],["PPF","Maximum output combinations of two goods"],["Normative","Value judgment about what should be"],["Factor market","Households sell labor and resources to firms"]],"traps":["Trap: Opportunity cost is everything you give up. Truth: It is only the single next-best alternative.","Trap: Money already spent should influence whether to continue. Truth: Sunk costs are ignored; only future differing costs and benefits matter.","Trap: Average cost tells you whether to accept one more order. Truth: Compare marginal benefit with marginal cost; fixed costs do not change.","Trap: A positive statement is a true statement. Truth: Positive means testable; it can be false.","Trap: Hiring idle workers shifts the PPF outward. Truth: That is a movement toward the curve; a shift needs more resources or better technology."],"mnemonic":"SOMPI: Scarcity forces choices, Opportunity cost is the next-best, Marginal means MB versus MC, Positive is testable, Incentives drive behavior.","cram":["Scarcity: limited resources, unlimited wants, so choices are unavoidable.","Opportunity cost equals the single next-best alternative forgone.","Ignore sunk costs; compare only future, differing costs and benefits.","Do more while marginal benefit is at least marginal cost.","On PPF: efficient. Inside: wasteful. Outside: unattainable.","Bowed PPF reflects increasing opportunity cost.","Positive is testable; normative has 'should' and value judgments.","Leakages: savings, taxes, imports. Injections: investment, government spending, exports."],"ytv":[{"title":"Production Possibilities Curve Review","channel":"Jacob Clifford","id":"O6XL__2CDPU","why":"Short, clear walk-through of PPC points, shifts, and opportunity cost."},{"title":"Production Possibilities Frontier (PPF) Explained | Scarcity, Opportunity Cost & Efficiency","channel":"INOMICS","id":"ycUgsW37fMo","why":"Ties scarcity, opportunity cost, and efficiency together using the PPF."}]},{"id":"u2","title":"Supply, Demand & Market Equilibrium","day":1,"summary":"Master the laws of demand and supply, shifters versus movements, equilibrium and linear solving, price ceilings and floors, consumer and producer surplus, deadweight loss, and tax incidence.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Demand and the law of demand","body":"<p><strong>Demand</strong> is the relationship between the price of a good and the quantity consumers are <em>willing and able to buy</em>, holding everything else constant (<em>ceteris paribus</em>). The <strong>law of demand</strong> says that when price rises, quantity demanded falls, and when price falls, quantity demanded rises. The demand curve slopes <strong>downward</strong>.</p><p>Why? At a higher price some buyers switch to substitutes (the <em>substitution effect</em>) and everyone's purchasing power feels smaller (the <em>income effect</em>). Also, extra units give less additional satisfaction (diminishing marginal utility), so buyers pay less for more.</p><p><strong>Market demand</strong> is the horizontal sum of all individual buyers' quantities at each price.</p><p><strong>Business example:</strong> a software firm lists its subscription at $50 and sells 1,000 seats per month. At $40 it sells 1,300. That is a <strong>movement along</strong> the same demand curve caused by a price change. The two terms to keep apart: <strong>quantity demanded</strong> (a single point; changes only when the good's own price changes) and <strong>demand</strong> (the entire curve; changes when something other than price changes).</p>"},{"h":"2. Supply and the law of supply","body":"<p><strong>Supply</strong> is the relationship between the price of a good and the quantity producers are willing and able to sell. The <strong>law of supply</strong>: when price rises, quantity supplied rises. The supply curve slopes <strong>upward</strong> because higher prices cover the rising marginal cost of producing more and attract more sellers.</p><p>Again distinguish <strong>quantity supplied</strong> (movement along the curve, caused only by the good's own price) from <strong>supply</strong> (the whole curve shifting).</p><p><strong>Business example:</strong> a farm will plant more acres of corn if the price of corn rises. The higher price makes it profitable to use less productive land and pay overtime to workers, whose cost is higher at the margin.</p><p>Remember the mirror image: demand slopes down (buyers want a lower price), supply slopes up (sellers want a higher price). Where they cross is the market's resting point.</p>"},{"h":"3. Shifters: determinants of demand and supply","body":"<p>A change in anything <em>except the good's own price</em> shifts the curve. <strong>Right shift = increase; left shift = decrease.</strong></p><p><strong>Demand shifters:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Income:</strong> for a <em>normal good</em> higher income raises demand; for an <em>inferior good</em> (instant noodles, used cars) higher income lowers demand.</li><li><strong>Prices of related goods:</strong> a rise in the price of a <em>substitute</em> raises demand for this good; a rise in the price of a <em>complement</em> lowers it.</li><li><strong>Tastes and preferences</strong> (trends, advertising).</li><li><strong>Expectations</strong> (expect a higher price later, buy more now).</li><li><strong>Number of buyers</strong> (population, new markets).</li></ul><p><strong>Supply shifters:</strong> <strong>input prices</strong> (wages, materials), <strong>technology</strong> (better lowers costs, supply increases), <strong>number of sellers</strong>, <strong>expectations</strong> (expect a higher price later, hold stock now), <strong>taxes and subsidies</strong> (a per-unit tax decreases supply, a subsidy increases it), and prices of other goods the firm could make.</p><p><strong>Simultaneous shifts:</strong> when both curves shift, one of price or quantity is certain and the other is <em>indeterminate</em> without knowing the sizes. Demand up and supply up: quantity certainly rises, price unclear. Demand up and supply down: price certainly rises, quantity unclear. Demand down and supply down: quantity certainly falls, price unclear.</p>"},{"h":"4. Equilibrium, shortages, surpluses, and solving the math","body":"<p><strong>Equilibrium</strong> is the price where <strong>quantity demanded equals quantity supplied</strong>. At that price there is no pressure to change.</p><ul><li><strong>Price above equilibrium:</strong> quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded, a <strong>surplus</strong> (excess supply). Sellers cut prices.</li><li><strong>Price below equilibrium:</strong> quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, a <strong>shortage</strong> (excess demand). Buyers bid prices up.</li></ul><p><strong>Solving a linear market.</strong> Suppose <code>Qd = 120 - 2P</code> and <code>Qs = 20 + 3P</code>. Set them equal: <code>120 - 2P = 20 + 3P</code>, so <code>100 = 5P</code>, <code>P = 20</code>. Plug back in: <code>Q = 120 - 40 = 80</code> (and <code>20 + 60 = 80</code>, which checks).</p><p>At <code>P = 15</code>: <code>Qd = 90</code>, <code>Qs = 65</code>, shortage of 25. At <code>P = 25</code>: <code>Qd = 70</code>, <code>Qs = 95</code>, surplus of 25.</p><p><strong>Shifting the curve:</strong> if demand increases by 25 units at every price, <code>Qd = 145 - 2P</code>; then <code>145 - 2P = 20 + 3P</code> gives <code>P = 25</code>, <code>Q = 95</code>. Both price and quantity rise. Always shift the curve, then re-solve.</p>"},{"h":"5. Price ceilings and price floors","body":"<p>A <strong>price ceiling</strong> is a legal <em>maximum</em> price. It matters only if set <strong>below</strong> equilibrium (it is <em>binding</em>). Result: quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, a <strong>shortage</strong>; the quantity actually traded is the smaller quantity supplied. Side effects: waiting lists, black markets, lower quality, and non-price rationing. <strong>Rent control</strong> is the classic example: tenants in controlled units benefit, but fewer apartments are offered and maintenance suffers.</p><p>A <strong>price floor</strong> is a legal <em>minimum</em> price. It matters only if set <strong>above</strong> equilibrium. Result: quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded, a <strong>surplus</strong>; the quantity traded is the smaller quantity demanded. A <strong>minimum wage</strong> above the market wage creates a surplus of labor (unemployment among those willing to work at that wage), though workers who keep jobs earn more.</p><p>A ceiling set <em>above</em> equilibrium, or a floor set <em>below</em> it, is <strong>non-binding</strong> and changes nothing.</p><p><strong>Numeric illustration</strong> (market with <code>Qd = 100 - 4P</code>, <code>Qs = -20 + 4P</code>, equilibrium <code>P = 15</code>, <code>Q = 40</code>): a ceiling of $10 gives <code>Qd = 60</code>, <code>Qs = 20</code>, shortage 40, trades only 20. A floor of $18 gives <code>Qd = 28</code>, <code>Qs = 52</code>, surplus 24, trades only 28.</p>"},{"h":"6. Consumer surplus, producer surplus, and deadweight loss","body":"<p><strong>Consumer surplus (CS)</strong> is the difference between what buyers are <em>willing to pay</em> and what they <em>actually pay</em>. On a graph it is the triangle <em>under the demand curve and above the price</em>. <strong>Producer surplus (PS)</strong> is the difference between the price sellers receive and the minimum they would accept (their marginal cost): the triangle <em>above the supply curve and below the price</em>. <strong>Total surplus = CS + PS</strong>, the gain from trade.</p><p>In the market <code>Qd = 100 - 4P</code>, <code>Qs = -20 + 4P</code>: equilibrium is $15 and 40 units. Demand hits zero at $25 and supply starts at $5. <code>CS = 1/2 x 40 x (25 - 15) = 200</code>, <code>PS = 1/2 x 40 x (15 - 5) = 200</code>, total surplus $400.</p><p>The competitive equilibrium maximizes total surplus. Any policy that cuts trading below that quantity creates <strong>deadweight loss (DWL)</strong>: gains from trade that disappear and go to no one. For the $10 ceiling, only 20 units trade. At 20 units, buyers would pay $20 (read off demand) and sellers would accept $10 (read off supply). <code>DWL = 1/2 x (40 - 20) x (20 - 10) = $100</code>. Triangle formula: half times the lost quantity times the gap between the buyer's and seller's price.</p>"},{"h":"7. Taxes and who really pays (incidence)","body":"<p>Who writes the check to the government does not decide who bears the burden. A per-unit tax drives a <strong>wedge</strong> between the price buyers pay and the price sellers keep. The wedge equals the tax. Buyers and sellers split the burden; this is <strong>tax incidence</strong>.</p><p>Take <code>Qd = 120 - 2P</code>, <code>Qs = 20 + 3P</code> (equilibrium $20, 80 units) and a <strong>$5 per-unit tax on sellers</strong>. Sellers now keep <code>P - 5</code>, so <code>Qs = 20 + 3(P - 5) = 5 + 3P</code>. Solve <code>120 - 2P = 5 + 3P</code>: <code>P = 23</code> (the price buyers pay), <code>Q = 74</code>, and sellers keep <code>23 - 5 = 18</code>.</p><ul><li>Buyers pay $3 more (23 vs 20): 60% of the burden.</li><li>Sellers keep $2 less (18 vs 20): 40% of the burden.</li><li>Tax revenue = 5 x 74 = $370. Deadweight loss = 1/2 x 5 x (80 - 74) = $15 because 6 fewer units trade.</li></ul><p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> the side of the market that is <em>less responsive</em> (less able to walk away; more inelastic) bears more of the tax. Taxing sellers or buyers gives the same result. Putting the tax on the other side of the market just shifts the demand curve down instead of the supply curve up. The next unit explains responsiveness (elasticity) in detail.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["The law of demand says that when the price of a good rises and everything else is equal, the quantity demanded falls. The demand curve slopes downward.","The law of supply says that when price rises, the quantity supplied rises, so the supply curve slopes upward.","A change in the good's own price causes a movement along the curve, called a change in quantity demanded or quantity supplied. A change in anything else shifts the whole curve.","Demand shifters are income, prices of substitutes and complements, tastes, expectations, and the number of buyers. A normal good gains demand when income rises, an inferior good loses demand.","Supply shifters are input prices, technology, the number of sellers, expectations, and taxes or subsidies. Better technology increases supply, and a per-unit tax decreases supply.","Equilibrium is the price at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. Above it there is a surplus, below it there is a shortage.","To solve a linear market, set quantity demanded equal to quantity supplied, solve for price, then plug the price back in to find the quantity.","When both curves shift at once, one of price or quantity is certain and the other is indeterminate until you know which shift is larger.","A price ceiling is a legal maximum that causes a shortage when it is set below equilibrium. A price floor is a legal minimum that causes a surplus when it is set above equilibrium. Rent control and the minimum wage are examples.","A price control that does not cross the equilibrium price is non-binding and has no effect.","Consumer surplus is willingness to pay minus price paid. Producer surplus is price received minus the lowest price the seller would accept. Deadweight loss is the lost total surplus when trading falls below the efficient quantity.","In tax incidence, the legal payer does not matter. The side of the market that is less responsive to price bears more of the tax burden."],"examples":[{"q":"A market has demand Qd = 120 - 2P and supply Qs = 20 + 3P. Find the equilibrium price and quantity, and the shortage or surplus at a price of $15.","steps":["Set Qd = Qs: 120 - 2P = 20 + 3P.","Collect terms: 100 = 5P, so P = 20.","Quantity: Q = 120 - 2(20) = 80. Check with supply: 20 + 3(20) = 80.","At P = 15: Qd = 120 - 30 = 90 and Qs = 20 + 45 = 65.","Qd is greater than Qs, so shortage = 90 - 65 = 25."],"answer":"Equilibrium P = $20, Q = 80. At $15 there is a shortage of 25 units."},{"q":"In a market with Qd = 100 - 4P and Qs = -20 + 4P, the government sets a price ceiling of $10. Find the equilibrium, the shortage, the quantity traded, and the deadweight loss.","steps":["Equilibrium: 100 - 4P = -20 + 4P gives 120 = 8P, so P = 15 and Q = 40.","At $10: Qd = 100 - 40 = 60 and Qs = -20 + 40 = 20. Shortage = 60 - 20 = 40.","Only the short side trades: Q = 20 (the quantity supplied).","Read the prices at Q = 20. Demand: P = (100 - 20)/4 = $20. Supply: P = (20 + 20)/4 = $10.","DWL = 1/2 x (40 - 20) x (20 - 10) = 1/2 x 20 x 10 = $100."],"answer":"Equilibrium $15 and 40 units; ceiling creates a shortage of 40, only 20 units trade, and DWL = $100."},{"q":"Using Qd = 120 - 2P and Qs = 20 + 3P, a $5 per-unit tax is placed on sellers. Find the price buyers pay, the price sellers keep, the share of the tax borne by buyers, and the tax revenue.","steps":["Sellers receive P - 5, so Qs = 20 + 3(P - 5) = 5 + 3P.","Set equal to demand: 120 - 2P = 5 + 3P, so 115 = 5P and P = 23. Q = 120 - 46 = 74.","Sellers keep 23 - 5 = $18.","Buyers' extra cost = 23 - 20 = $3, so share = 3/5 = 60 percent. Sellers bear 2/5 = 40 percent.","Revenue = 5 x 74 = $370."],"answer":"Buyers pay $23, sellers keep $18, buyers bear 60 percent of the tax, and revenue is $370."}],"videos":[{"title":"Supply and demand basics","query":"supply and demand explained shifts vs movement along curve economics"},{"title":"Price ceilings and floors","query":"price ceiling price floor rent control minimum wage shortage surplus explained"},{"title":"Consumer surplus, producer surplus, deadweight loss","query":"consumer surplus producer surplus deadweight loss tax incidence explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Law of demand","b":"Higher price means lower quantity demanded, all else equal"},{"f":"Law of supply","b":"Higher price means higher quantity supplied, all else equal"},{"f":"Change in quantity demanded","b":"Movement along the curve; only the good's own price causes it"},{"f":"Change in demand","b":"Shift of the whole curve; caused by non-price factors"},{"f":"Normal good","b":"Demand rises when income rises"},{"f":"Inferior good","b":"Demand falls when income rises"},{"f":"Substitutes","b":"Price of one rises, demand for the other rises"},{"f":"Complements","b":"Price of one rises, demand for the other falls"},{"f":"Demand shifters","b":"Income, related prices, tastes, expectations, number of buyers"},{"f":"Supply shifters","b":"Input prices, technology, number of sellers, expectations, taxes/subsidies"},{"f":"Equilibrium","b":"Price where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied"},{"f":"Surplus (excess supply)","b":"Price above equilibrium; quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded"},{"f":"Shortage (excess demand)","b":"Price below equilibrium; quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied"},{"f":"Demand up, supply up","b":"Quantity rises for sure; price is indeterminate"},{"f":"Demand up, supply down","b":"Price rises for sure; quantity is indeterminate"},{"f":"Price ceiling","b":"Legal maximum price; binding below equilibrium, causes shortage"},{"f":"Price floor","b":"Legal minimum price; binding above equilibrium, causes surplus"},{"f":"Consumer surplus","b":"Willingness to pay minus price actually paid"},{"f":"Producer surplus","b":"Price received minus minimum acceptable price"},{"f":"Deadweight loss","b":"Lost total surplus when trade falls below the efficient quantity"},{"f":"Tax wedge","b":"Gap between price buyers pay and price sellers keep equals the tax"},{"f":"Who bears more of a tax?","b":"The less price-responsive (more inelastic) side of the market"}],"questions":[{"q":"According to the law of demand, when the price of a good rises and all else is unchanged, the","o":["quantity demanded rises","demand curve shifts to the left","demand curve shifts to the right","quantity demanded falls"],"a":3,"e":"The law of demand describes a movement along a downward-sloping curve: higher price, lower quantity demanded. A price change alone never shifts the curve, which is the trap in the shift options.","d":1},{"q":"Which event causes a movement along a demand curve rather than a shift?","o":["A change in consumers' income","A change in the good's own price","A change in the price of a substitute","A change in consumers' tastes"],"a":1,"e":"Only the good's own price moves buyers along the curve. Income, related prices, and tastes are demand shifters that move the entire curve.","d":1},{"q":"Incomes rise. For a normal good, the demand curve will","o":["shift to the left","shift to the right","stay in place, with only a move along the curve","become steeper but not shift"],"a":1,"e":"A normal good by definition gains demand when income rises, so the curve shifts right. The leftward shift describes an inferior good.","d":1},{"q":"Market equilibrium occurs at the price where","o":["consumer surplus equals producer surplus","quantity demanded equals quantity supplied","price equals marginal revenue","quantity supplied is at its maximum"],"a":1,"e":"Equilibrium is defined by Qd = Qs. At that price neither buyers nor sellers want to change their behavior. Equal surpluses are not required.","d":1},{"q":"If the price in a market is above the equilibrium price, there will be","o":["a surplus, and the price tends to fall","a shortage, and the price tends to rise","a shortage, and the price tends to fall","a surplus, and the price tends to rise"],"a":0,"e":"At a high price, sellers want to sell more than buyers want to buy, so excess supply pushes the price down. A shortage happens at prices below equilibrium.","d":1},{"q":"A binding price ceiling causes","o":["a shortage","an increase in quantity supplied","a surplus","an increase in producer surplus"],"a":0,"e":"A ceiling set below equilibrium holds the price down, so quantity demanded rises and quantity supplied falls, leaving a shortage. Surpluses come from binding floors.","d":1},{"q":"A binding price floor such as a minimum wage above the market wage causes","o":["a surplus of labor, which appears as unemployment","a shortage of workers willing to work at the legal wage","no change in employment because wages are fixed by law","a decrease in the quantity of labor supplied at that wage"],"a":0,"e":"In the labor market firms are the buyers. A wage above equilibrium raises the quantity of labor supplied and cuts the quantity demanded, creating a surplus of labor (unemployment).","d":1},{"q":"Consumer surplus is the","o":["total amount that consumers spend in the market","difference between willingness to pay and the price actually paid","difference between the price and the marginal cost of production","difference between the price paid and the cost to the seller"],"a":1,"e":"Consumer surplus measures the buyer's net gain from a purchase: willingness to pay minus price. The price minus cost gap is producer surplus.","d":1},{"q":"The price of coffee rises sharply. What is the most likely effect on the market for tea, a substitute?","o":["Quantity demanded of tea decreases along the same curve","Demand for tea decreases (shifts left)","Demand for tea increases (shifts right)","Supply of tea increases"],"a":2,"e":"A higher price of a substitute pushes buyers toward tea, shifting its demand curve right. The price of tea itself has not changed, so this is a shift, not a movement.","d":2},{"q":"The price of printers rises. What happens in the market for ink cartridges, a complement?","o":["Quantity supplied of cartridges rises along the supply curve","Demand for cartridges increases (shifts right)","Demand for cartridges decreases (shifts left)","Supply of cartridges decreases"],"a":2,"e":"Complements are used together; fewer printers bought means fewer cartridges demanded at every price. Substitutes would show the opposite pattern.","d":2},{"q":"The cost of aluminum, a key input for bicycle makers, increases. In the bicycle market the","o":["supply curve shifts right, lowering the equilibrium price","demand curve shifts right, raising the equilibrium price","supply curve shifts left, raising the equilibrium price","demand curve shifts left, lowering the equilibrium price"],"a":2,"e":"A higher input price raises marginal cost, so sellers offer less at each price: supply decreases (shifts left) and the price rises. Demand is not directly affected by input costs.","d":2},{"q":"A new technology lowers the cost of producing solar panels. The most likely result is","o":["a lower equilibrium price and a higher equilibrium quantity","a higher equilibrium price and a lower equilibrium quantity","a lower equilibrium price and a lower equilibrium quantity","a higher equilibrium price and a higher equilibrium quantity"],"a":0,"e":"Better technology increases supply (right shift). Along a downward-sloping demand curve, that means a lower price and a higher quantity.","d":2},{"q":"In a market with Qd = 120 - 2P and Qs = 20 + 3P, the equilibrium price is","o":["$24","$12","$20","$16"],"a":2,"e":"Set 120 - 2P = 20 + 3P, so 100 = 5P and P = 20. The quantity is 80 units. Dividing 120 by 5 (24) forgets to subtract the supply intercept.","d":2},{"q":"In the same market (Qd = 120 - 2P, Qs = 20 + 3P), at a price of $15 there is","o":["a surplus of 65 units","a shortage of 25 units","a surplus of 25 units","a shortage of 90 units"],"a":1,"e":"At $15, Qd = 90 and Qs = 65. Demand exceeds supply by 25, a shortage. The numbers 90 and 65 are the individual quantities, not the gap.","d":2},{"q":"Demand for a product increases and, at the same time, supply increases. What can be said about equilibrium price and quantity?","o":["Price rises; the change in quantity is indeterminate","Both price and quantity fall","Both price and quantity rise","Quantity rises; the change in price is indeterminate"],"a":3,"e":"Both shifts push quantity up. Demand pushes price up while supply pushes it down, so the net effect on price depends on which shift is bigger.","d":2},{"q":"Demand for a product decreases while supply decreases. Which outcome is certain?","o":["Equilibrium quantity rises","Equilibrium quantity falls","Equilibrium price rises","Equilibrium price falls"],"a":1,"e":"Both shifts reduce quantity. Lower demand lowers price while lower supply raises it, so price is indeterminate.","d":2},{"q":"A city imposes a binding rent control on apartments. Which outcome is most likely over time?","o":["A shortage of rental units and declining quality","A surplus of vacant apartments","Lower demand for apartments","A rise in the quantity of apartments supplied"],"a":0,"e":"A rent ceiling below equilibrium increases quantity demanded and reduces quantity supplied. Landlords have less incentive to maintain units, so quality tends to decline.","d":2},{"q":"Shoppers expect the price of a smartphone to rise next month. Today's market will show","o":["a decrease in current demand","an increase in quantity supplied today","no change, because the price has not yet changed","an increase in current demand (shift right)"],"a":3,"e":"Expectations of a higher future price pull purchases forward, shifting today's demand curve to the right.","d":2},{"q":"The price of wheat rises, and farmers produce more wheat. In supply-and-demand terms this is","o":["a decrease in supply, a leftward shift of the whole curve","an increase in demand for wheat from farmers and buyers","an increase in quantity supplied along the supply curve","an increase in supply, a rightward shift of the whole curve"],"a":2,"e":"A change in the good's own price causes a change in quantity supplied along a fixed supply curve. Supply itself shifts only when other determinants change.","d":2},{"q":"Deadweight loss is best described as","o":["tax revenue collected by the government on each unit that is traded","the surplus that buyers keep when they pay a lower price","profits that sellers lose when the price of the good falls","total surplus lost when trade falls below the efficient quantity"],"a":3,"e":"Deadweight loss is a net efficiency loss: gains from trade that no one captures. Tax revenue is transferred, not lost.","d":2},{"q":"During a recession incomes fall. What happens to the demand for an inferior good such as store-brand instant noodles?","o":["It increases (shifts right)","Quantity demanded falls along the same curve","It stays the same, since only price shifts demand","It decreases (shifts left)"],"a":0,"e":"Inferior goods are bought more as income falls, because consumers trade down from pricier options, so demand shifts right. A decrease would describe a normal good.","d":2},{"q":"A market has Qd = 100 - 4P and Qs = -20 + 4P. The government sets a price ceiling of $10. The deadweight loss is","o":["$50","$200","$400","$100"],"a":3,"e":"At $10, Qd = 60 and Qs = 20, so only 20 units trade. The buyer's price at 20 units is $20 and the seller's is $10. DWL = 1/2 x (40 - 20) x (20 - 10) = $100. $200 forgets the one-half; $400 multiplies the shortage of 40 by the $10 gap.","d":3},{"q":"In the same market (Qd = 100 - 4P, Qs = -20 + 4P), a price floor of $18 is imposed. The result is","o":["a surplus of 52 units, with 28 units sold","a surplus of 24 units, with 52 units sold","a surplus of 24 units, with 28 units sold","a shortage of 24 units, with 52 units sold"],"a":2,"e":"At $18, Qd = 28 and Qs = 52. Supply exceeds demand by 24 (a surplus). Only the short side of the market, buyers, determines the 28 units actually sold.","d":3},{"q":"In the market Qd = 100 - 4P and Qs = -20 + 4P, total surplus at the free-market equilibrium is","o":["$600","$400","$200","$800"],"a":1,"e":"Equilibrium is P = 15, Q = 40. Demand hits zero at $25 and supply begins at $5. CS = 1/2 x 40 x 10 = 200 and PS = 1/2 x 40 x 10 = 200, so total is 400. 200 counts only one side, and 800 omits the one-half.","d":3},{"q":"A $10 per-unit tax is placed on sellers in a market with Qd = 120 - 2P and Qs = 20 + 3P (original equilibrium $20). The price buyers pay becomes","o":["$16","$20","$26","$30"],"a":2,"e":"Sellers keep P - 10, so Qs = 20 + 3(P - 10) = -10 + 3P. Setting 120 - 2P = -10 + 3P gives P = 26. Buyers pay $6 more, not the full $10. Sellers keep 26 - 10 = $16, and $20 is the old price.","d":3},{"q":"Demand for electric scooters increases sharply while an input shortage decreases supply. Which statement is definitely true?","o":["The equilibrium quantity falls","The equilibrium price falls","The equilibrium quantity rises","The equilibrium price rises"],"a":3,"e":"Higher demand raises price and lower supply also raises price, so price certainly rises. The effect on quantity depends on which shift is larger.","d":3},{"q":"In the market Qd = 100 - 4P and Qs = -20 + 4P, a price ceiling of $20 is imposed. The effect is","o":["no effect, since $20 is above the $15 equilibrium price","a rise in the market price to the $20 cap","a surplus of 20 units, because $20 exceeds equilibrium","a shortage, because the price is capped at $20"],"a":0,"e":"A ceiling only binds when it is set below equilibrium. At $20 the market price of $15 is already below the cap, so nothing changes. Caps do not push price up.","d":3},{"q":"A per-unit tax is imposed on sellers of a good. Which describes what happens on the graph?","o":["The demand curve shifts up by the full amount of the tax paid by buyers","The demand curve shifts left and the quantity supplied rises","Both curves shift right by the amount of the tax","Supply shifts up by the tax; buyers move along demand and buy less"],"a":3,"e":"A tax on sellers raises their marginal cost by the tax, shifting supply up/left. Buyers respond to the higher price by moving along their (unchanged) demand curve.","d":3},{"q":"A tax is placed on a product that buyers consider a necessity with few substitutes, while sellers can easily switch to producing other goods. Who bears most of the tax?","o":["Buyers, because they are less responsive to price","Sellers, because the tax is legally collected from them","Neither, because the tax is paid to the government","Buyers and sellers equally, regardless of responsiveness"],"a":0,"e":"The less price-responsive side bears more of the burden because it cannot easily walk away. Legal incidence and economic incidence are different things.","d":3},{"q":"An arena sets concert ticket prices well below the market-clearing level, and tickets sell out in minutes. Resellers then charge much more online. This is best explained as","o":["a leftward shift in demand for concerts once the tickets sell out","a shortage at the capped price; the resale price signals equilibrium","a surplus of tickets at the capped price that resellers absorb","a decrease in supply of concerts because of the resale market"],"a":1,"e":"A below-equilibrium price means Qd exceeds Qs, so tickets are rationed by speed or connections and a secondary market emerges at the higher price. No surplus exists when tickets sell out.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Law of demand","Price up, quantity demanded down"],["Equilibrium","Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied"],["Shortage","Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied"],["Price ceiling","Legal maximum price"],["Price floor","Legal minimum price"],["Consumer surplus","Willingness to pay minus price paid"],["Deadweight loss","Gains from trade lost to inefficiency"],["Complement","Used together; price up lowers other's demand"]],"traps":["Trap: A price change shifts the demand curve. Truth: The good's own price only moves you along the curve; other factors shift it.","Trap: Any price ceiling creates a shortage. Truth: Only a binding ceiling set below equilibrium does; above it, nothing happens.","Trap: When both curves shift, you can always predict price and quantity. Truth: One of the two is indeterminate without knowing relative sizes.","Trap: Whoever legally pays the tax bears it. Truth: The less price-responsive side bears more of the burden.","Trap: Under a binding price control the larger quantity is traded. Truth: The smaller quantity (short side) is what actually trades."],"mnemonic":"Slide for own price, shift for the rest (TIREN: Tastes, Income, Related goods, Expectations, Number of buyers); a Ceiling causes a shortage, a Floor floods the market with surplus.","cram":["Own price: movement along curve. Anything else: shift of the curve.","Equilibrium: set Qd equal to Qs, solve price, then quantity.","Surplus above equilibrium price; shortage below equilibrium price.","Binding ceiling is below equilibrium; binding floor is above.","Both curves shift: one of price or quantity is indeterminate.","Deadweight loss equals one-half times lost quantity times price gap.","Tax wedge equals the tax; the less elastic side pays more.","Total surplus equals consumer surplus plus producer surplus."],"ytv":[{"title":"Supply and Demand: Crash Course Economics #4","channel":"CrashCourse","id":"g9aDizJpd_s","why":"Friendly overview of demand, supply, equilibrium, and shifts."},{"title":"Price Ceilings: Deadweight Loss","channel":"Marginal Revolution University","id":"YbLAASzzo6A","why":"Shows how a binding ceiling creates a shortage and deadweight loss."}]},{"id":"u3","title":"Elasticity & Pricing","day":2,"summary":"Compute price elasticity of demand with the midpoint method, apply the total revenue test to pricing decisions, and use cross-price, income, and supply elasticity, including who bears a tax.","lesson":[{"h":"1. What elasticity measures, and the midpoint formula","body":"<p><strong>Elasticity</strong> measures how much one variable responds to a change in another. The most important version is the <strong>price elasticity of demand (PED)</strong>: how strongly buyers cut (or raise) their quantity when price changes. Managers care because it predicts what a price change does to sales and revenue.</p><p><code>PED = (% change in quantity demanded) / (% change in price)</code></p><p>Because demand slopes down, the result is negative, so we report the <strong>absolute value</strong>. To avoid getting a different answer depending on direction, use the <strong>midpoint (arc) formula</strong>, which divides each change by the <em>average</em> of the old and new values:</p><p><code>%ΔQ = (Q2 - Q1) / ((Q1 + Q2)/2)</code> and <code>%ΔP = (P2 - P1) / ((P1 + P2)/2)</code></p><p><strong>Example:</strong> price rises from $8 to $12 and quantity falls from 600 to 300. Average Q = 450, so %ΔQ = -300/450 = -66.7%. Average P = $10, so %ΔP = 4/10 = +40%. PED = 66.7/40 = <strong>1.67</strong>. (The simple starting-point method would give exactly 1.0, a different answer. The exam uses the midpoint method.)</p>"},{"h":"2. Categories of elasticity and what drives them","body":"<ul><li><strong>Elastic:</strong> |PED| greater than 1. Quantity responds <em>more than proportionally</em> to price.</li><li><strong>Inelastic:</strong> |PED| less than 1. Quantity responds <em>less than proportionally</em>.</li><li><strong>Unit elastic:</strong> |PED| equals 1.</li><li><strong>Perfectly inelastic:</strong> PED equals 0 (vertical demand curve; quantity does not respond at all).</li><li><strong>Perfectly elastic:</strong> PED is infinite (horizontal demand curve; any price increase loses every customer).</li></ul><p><strong>Determinants of price elasticity of demand:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Availability of substitutes:</strong> more and closer substitutes mean more elastic. This is the biggest factor.</li><li><strong>Necessity vs luxury:</strong> necessities (insulin, electricity) are inelastic; luxuries (cruises) are elastic.</li><li><strong>Share of budget:</strong> big-ticket items are more elastic; cheap items (salt, paper clips) are inelastic.</li><li><strong>Time horizon:</strong> demand is more elastic in the long run, since buyers have time to adjust (gasoline).</li><li><strong>How narrowly the market is defined:</strong> demand for \"gasoline\" is inelastic; demand for \"one brand's gasoline\" is elastic.</li></ul>"},{"h":"3. The total revenue test and pricing decisions","body":"<p><strong>Total revenue (TR) = price x quantity.</strong> A price change has two opposite effects on TR: a higher price earns more per unit, but fewer units sell. Elasticity tells you which effect wins.</p><ul><li><strong>Elastic demand:</strong> price and TR move in <em>opposite</em> directions. Cut price, TR rises. Raise price, TR falls.</li><li><strong>Inelastic demand:</strong> price and TR move in the <em>same</em> direction. Raise price, TR rises. Cut price, TR falls.</li><li><strong>Unit elastic:</strong> TR does not change; it is at its maximum.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> a software firm cuts its monthly price from $50 to $40 and seats rise from 1,000 to 1,300. Midpoint PED = (300/1150) / (10/45) = 26.09% / 22.22% = <strong>1.17</strong>, elastic. TR rises from $50,000 to $52,000, as the test predicts.</p><p><strong>Managerial rules:</strong> if demand is elastic, discounts can raise revenue; if inelastic, a price increase raises revenue (and cuts costs, since you sell fewer units), so a profit-maximizing firm with positive marginal cost avoids operating where demand is inelastic. Elasticity is not constant along a straight-line demand curve: it is <strong>elastic at high prices, unit elastic at the midpoint, and inelastic at low prices</strong>. For <code>Q = 200 - 4P</code>, TR is maximized at P = $25 (Q = 100, TR = $2,500), where PED = 1. At $30 TR is $2,400 and at $20 it is $2,400 too.</p>"},{"h":"4. Cross-price elasticity","body":"<p><strong>Cross-price elasticity of demand</strong> measures how the quantity demanded of good X responds to a change in the price of a <em>different</em> good Y.</p><p><code>Exy = (% change in quantity of X) / (% change in price of Y)</code></p><ul><li><strong>Positive:</strong> the goods are <strong>substitutes</strong>. Y gets pricier, buyers switch to X. The larger the number, the closer the substitutes.</li><li><strong>Negative:</strong> the goods are <strong>complements</strong>. Y gets pricier, buyers also buy less X.</li><li><strong>Near zero:</strong> the goods are unrelated.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> Brand A's price rises from $4 to $6 (midpoint %ΔP = 2/5 = 40%) and sales of Brand B climb from 500 to 600 (%ΔQ = 100/550 = 18.18%). Exy = 18.18/40 = <strong>+0.45</strong>: substitutes, but not very close ones. Note the sign matters here; do not take the absolute value.</p><p><strong>Use:</strong> firms use cross-price elasticity to anticipate how a competitor's price move affects their sales, and to bundle or price complements (a razor sold cheaply to sell more blades).</p>"},{"h":"5. Income elasticity of demand","body":"<p><strong>Income elasticity of demand</strong> measures how quantity demanded responds to a change in consumer income.</p><p><code>Ey = (% change in quantity demanded) / (% change in income)</code></p><ul><li><strong>Positive:</strong> <strong>normal good</strong>. Between 0 and 1 is a normal <em>necessity</em> (groceries, utilities): demand grows slower than income.</li><li><strong>Greater than 1:</strong> a <strong>luxury</strong> (a normal good where demand grows faster than income): restaurants, travel, premium brands.</li><li><strong>Negative:</strong> an <strong>inferior good</strong>. Demand falls as income rises: instant noodles, bus rides, generic brands.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> income rises from $40,000 to $48,000 (midpoint %ΔI = 8,000/44,000 = 18.18%) and spending on organic groceries rises from 2,000 to 3,000 units (%ΔQ = 1,000/2,500 = 40%). Ey = 40/18.18 = <strong>2.2</strong>, a luxury.</p><p><strong>Managerial use:</strong> a company selling luxury goods will suffer in recessions and boom in expansions; makers of inferior goods may do better when incomes fall. Plan product mix and inventory around expected income trends.</p>"},{"h":"6. Price elasticity of supply","body":"<p><strong>Price elasticity of supply (PES)</strong> measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to price: <code>PES = (% change in quantity supplied) / (% change in price)</code>. It is positive. Elastic if above 1, inelastic if below 1.</p><p>Supply is more elastic when firms have <strong>spare capacity</strong>, can <strong>store inventory</strong>, can easily get more inputs, or have <strong>more time</strong> to adjust. Supply is inelastic when capacity is fixed (beachfront land, a sold-out stadium) or inputs are scarce. <strong>Supply is almost always more elastic in the long run</strong> than in the short run.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> price rises 10% and quantity supplied rises 15%. PES = 15/10 = 1.5, elastic.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> when demand surges, an industry with inelastic supply sees a large price rise and a small increase in quantity (think concert tickets or hotel rooms during an event); with elastic supply, quantity does most of the adjusting and the price barely moves.</p>"},{"h":"7. Elasticity and who bears a tax","body":"<p>The tax burden falls mostly on whichever side of the market is <em>less elastic</em>, because that side has fewer ways to avoid the tax.</p><ul><li><strong>Demand inelastic, supply elastic:</strong> buyers bear most of the tax (cigarettes, gasoline).</li><li><strong>Demand elastic, supply inelastic:</strong> sellers bear most.</li></ul><p>A handy approximation for the buyers' share of a tax: <code>buyers' share = Es / (Es + |Ed|)</code>, and sellers' share is the rest. If |Ed| = 0.5 and Es = 1.5, buyers bear 1.5/2.0 = 75%.</p><p>The market from Unit 2 (<code>Qd = 120 - 2P</code>, <code>Qs = 20 + 3P</code>) has, at the $20 equilibrium, |Ed| = 2 x 20/80 = 0.5 and Es = 3 x 20/80 = 0.75, so buyers' share = 0.75/1.25 = 60%, exactly what we found by solving the tax problem.</p><p>A more elastic market also loses more trade from a tax, so the deadweight loss is larger. Governments therefore tax inelastic goods when they want revenue with little distortion.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Price elasticity of demand measures how responsive the quantity demanded is to a change in price. It equals the percent change in quantity divided by the percent change in price, and we report its absolute value.","The midpoint formula divides each change by the average of the old and new values, so the answer is the same whichever direction the price moves.","If elasticity is greater than one, demand is elastic. If it is less than one, demand is inelastic. If it equals one, demand is unit elastic.","The strongest determinant of elasticity is the availability of close substitutes. Necessities, small budget items, and short time horizons make demand more inelastic.","The total revenue test says that with elastic demand, price and revenue move in opposite directions, and with inelastic demand they move in the same direction.","Along a straight line demand curve, demand is elastic at high prices, unit elastic at the midpoint, and inelastic at low prices. Revenue is highest where demand is unit elastic.","A firm that wants more revenue should cut price when demand is elastic and raise price when demand is inelastic.","Cross price elasticity is positive for substitutes, negative for complements, and near zero for unrelated goods. Keep the sign when you calculate it.","Income elasticity is positive for normal goods, greater than one for luxuries, and negative for inferior goods.","Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to price. Supply is more elastic with spare capacity, storable inventory, and more time.","In a tax, the burden falls mostly on the side of the market that is less elastic, meaning less able to change its behavior."],"examples":[{"q":"A café raises the price of a lunch combo from $8 to $12, and daily sales fall from 600 to 300. Compute price elasticity of demand with the midpoint method and state what happened to total revenue.","steps":["Average quantity = (600 + 300)/2 = 450. %ΔQ = (300 - 600)/450 = -66.67%.","Average price = (8 + 12)/2 = 10. %ΔP = (12 - 8)/10 = +40%.","PED = |-66.67 / 40| = 1.67, which is greater than 1, so demand is elastic.","TR before = 8 x 600 = $4,800. TR after = 12 x 300 = $3,600."],"answer":"PED = 1.67 (elastic). Revenue fell from $4,800 to $3,600, consistent with the total revenue test: price up, revenue down."},{"q":"A software firm cuts its monthly price from $50 to $40, and seats sold rise from 1,000 to 1,300. Find the elasticity and decide whether the cut was a good idea for revenue.","steps":["%ΔQ = 300 / 1,150 = 26.09%.","%ΔP = -10 / 45 = -22.22%.","PED = 26.09 / 22.22 = 1.17, elastic.","TR before = 50 x 1,000 = $50,000. TR after = 40 x 1,300 = $52,000, up $2,000."],"answer":"PED = 1.17 (elastic), so the price cut raised revenue by $2,000 as the total revenue test predicts."},{"q":"When Brand A's price rises from $2 to $3, sales of competing Brand B rise from 200 to 260. Separately, when household income rises from $50,000 to $60,000, restaurant meals purchased rise from 40 to 50 per year. Compute the cross-price and income elasticities and classify each.","steps":["Cross-price: %ΔQ(B) = 60 / 230 = 26.09%. %ΔP(A) = 1 / 2.5 = 40%. Exy = 26.09 / 40 = +0.65.","Positive sign means substitutes.","Income: %ΔQ = 10 / 45 = 22.22%. %ΔI = 10,000 / 55,000 = 18.18%. Ey = 22.22 / 18.18 = 1.22.","Ey is positive and greater than 1: a luxury (normal) good."],"answer":"Cross-price elasticity = +0.65 (substitutes). Income elasticity = 1.22 (luxury normal good)."}],"videos":[{"title":"Price elasticity of demand and the midpoint method","query":"price elasticity of demand midpoint method explained"},{"title":"Total revenue test and pricing","query":"total revenue test elastic inelastic pricing decisions economics"},{"title":"Cross-price and income elasticity","query":"cross price elasticity income elasticity normal inferior luxury goods explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Price elasticity of demand","b":"Percent change in quantity demanded divided by percent change in price"},{"f":"Midpoint formula","b":"Change divided by the average of old and new values"},{"f":"Elastic demand","b":"|PED| greater than 1; quantity very responsive to price"},{"f":"Inelastic demand","b":"|PED| less than 1; quantity not very responsive"},{"f":"Unit elastic demand","b":"|PED| equals 1; revenue is at its maximum"},{"f":"Perfectly inelastic","b":"PED equals 0; vertical demand curve"},{"f":"Perfectly elastic","b":"PED is infinite; horizontal demand curve"},{"f":"Biggest determinant of PED","b":"Availability of close substitutes"},{"f":"Other determinants of PED","b":"Necessity vs luxury, budget share, time horizon, market definition"},{"f":"Total revenue","b":"Price times quantity"},{"f":"TR test, elastic demand","b":"Price and TR move in opposite directions"},{"f":"TR test, inelastic demand","b":"Price and TR move in the same direction"},{"f":"Linear demand curve elasticity","b":"Elastic at high prices, unit at midpoint, inelastic at low prices"},{"f":"Cross-price elasticity","b":"% change in quantity of X divided by % change in price of Y"},{"f":"Cross-price positive","b":"Substitutes"},{"f":"Cross-price negative","b":"Complements"},{"f":"Income elasticity","b":"% change in quantity demanded divided by % change in income"},{"f":"Income elasticity above 1","b":"Luxury normal good"},{"f":"Income elasticity negative","b":"Inferior good"},{"f":"Price elasticity of supply","b":"% change in quantity supplied divided by % change in price"},{"f":"Supply is more elastic when","b":"There is spare capacity, storable inventory, or more time"},{"f":"Who bears more of a tax?","b":"The less elastic side; buyers' share = Es / (Es + |Ed|)"}],"questions":[{"q":"Price elasticity of demand measures","o":["how demand responds to a change in consumer income","how responsive quantity demanded is to a change in price","how responsive price is to a change in quantity supplied","the slope of the supply curve at equilibrium"],"a":1,"e":"PED is the percent change in quantity demanded divided by the percent change in price. Responses to income are income elasticity, and supply slope is a different concept.","d":1},{"q":"If the price elasticity of demand for a product has an absolute value greater than 1, demand is","o":["unit elastic","perfectly inelastic","elastic","inelastic"],"a":2,"e":"Greater than 1 means quantity changes by a larger percentage than price, which defines elastic demand.","d":1},{"q":"Which factor generally makes the demand for a product MORE elastic?","o":["Being a necessity of daily life","A very short time to adjust","A very small share of the consumer's budget","The availability of many close substitutes"],"a":3,"e":"Close substitutes let buyers switch easily when price rises. Necessities, small budget shares, and short adjustment times all make demand less elastic.","d":1},{"q":"A firm faces inelastic demand for its product. If it raises its price, total revenue will","o":["stay the same","fall to zero","decrease","increase"],"a":3,"e":"With inelastic demand the percentage drop in quantity is smaller than the percentage rise in price, so revenue rises.","d":1},{"q":"A positive cross-price elasticity of demand between two goods means they are","o":["substitutes","complements","inferior goods","unrelated goods"],"a":0,"e":"If a higher price of Y increases the quantity demanded of X, buyers are switching, so the goods are substitutes. Complements have negative cross-price elasticity.","d":1},{"q":"A good with a negative income elasticity of demand is classified as","o":["a complement","a normal necessity","a luxury good","an inferior good"],"a":3,"e":"Negative income elasticity means demand falls when income rises, the definition of an inferior good. Luxuries have income elasticity greater than 1.","d":1},{"q":"Total revenue is calculated as","o":["price divided by quantity","quantity multiplied by marginal cost","price multiplied by quantity sold","price minus average cost"],"a":2,"e":"Total revenue equals P times Q. Costs are not part of revenue.","d":1},{"q":"Price elasticity of supply is generally greater in the long run than in the short run because","o":["producers face fewer substitutes in the long run","producers have time to adjust capacity and inputs","consumers are less responsive to price as time passes","the supply curve always becomes vertical over time"],"a":1,"e":"With more time, firms can build plants, hire, and change inputs, so quantity supplied responds more strongly to price. Consumer responsiveness belongs to demand elasticity.","d":1},{"q":"Price rises from $8 to $12 and quantity demanded falls from 600 to 300. Using the midpoint method, price elasticity of demand is approximately","o":["1.00","1.25","1.67","0.60"],"a":2,"e":"%ΔQ = -300/450 = -66.7% and %ΔP = 4/10 = 40%, so PED = 66.7/40 = 1.67. The starting-point method gives 1.00 and is not the midpoint method; 0.60 inverts the ratio.","d":2},{"q":"A study finds the price elasticity of demand for a firm's product is 0.4. If the firm raises its price by 10 percent, quantity demanded will fall by about","o":["25 percent","4 percent","10 percent","0.4 percent"],"a":1,"e":"Percent change in quantity = elasticity x percent change in price = 0.4 x 10 = 4 percent. Dividing instead of multiplying gives 25, which is the common slip.","d":2},{"q":"A theater's demand is inelastic and managers want to increase total revenue. They should","o":["cut prices to fill more seats","raise ticket prices","cut ticket prices","keep prices the same"],"a":1,"e":"When demand is inelastic, revenue moves with price. Raising price therefore raises revenue (and also lowers the number of seats served). Cutting price would reduce revenue.","d":2},{"q":"An airline finds demand on a leisure route is elastic. To increase revenue on that route it should","o":["lower fares","keep fares unchanged","raise fares","raise fares and cut the number of flights"],"a":0,"e":"With elastic demand the gain in volume from a lower fare outweighs the lower price per ticket, so revenue rises. Raising fares reduces revenue.","d":2},{"q":"A firm cuts price from $50 to $40 and sales rise from 1,000 to 1,300. The midpoint price elasticity of demand is approximately","o":["1.00","0.85","1.17","1.50"],"a":2,"e":"%ΔQ = 300/1150 = 26.09% and %ΔP = 10/45 = 22.22%, so PED = 1.17 (elastic). 1.50 comes from using starting values, and 0.85 is the inverse.","d":2},{"q":"When Brand A's price rises from $4 to $6, sales of Brand B rise from 500 to 600. The cross-price elasticity (midpoint) is approximately","o":["+0.45, indicating substitutes","+2.2, indicating close complements","-0.45, indicating complements","+0.45, indicating inferior goods"],"a":0,"e":"%ΔQ(B) = 100/550 = 18.2% and %ΔP(A) = 2/5 = 40%, so Exy = +0.45. A positive sign means substitutes; inferior goods relate to income, not related prices.","d":2},{"q":"Household income rises from $40,000 to $48,000 and spending on organic groceries rises from 2,000 to 3,000 units. Income elasticity (midpoint) is about","o":["2.2, an inferior good","0.45, an inferior good","2.2, a luxury good","0.45, a normal necessity"],"a":2,"e":"%ΔQ = 1000/2500 = 40% and %ΔI = 8000/44000 = 18.18%, so Ey = 2.2, positive and above 1, a luxury. A negative sign would be needed for inferior.","d":2},{"q":"If hot dogs become more expensive and the quantity of hot dog buns demanded falls, the cross-price elasticity between them is","o":["negative, because they are complements","zero, because buns are unrelated to hot dogs","negative, because buns are inferior goods","positive, because they are substitutes"],"a":0,"e":"A price increase for one good that lowers the quantity of the other reflects goods that are used together: negative cross-price elasticity.","d":2},{"q":"Why is demand for gasoline more elastic over several years than over a few weeks?","o":["Gasoline consumption does not depend on price over time","Over time buyers can switch to efficient cars or transit","Gasoline becomes a luxury good as time passes","Gasoline loses its substitutes as time passes"],"a":1,"e":"A longer time horizon gives buyers more ways to adjust, raising elasticity. Substitutes become more available, not less.","d":2},{"q":"Which good is likely to have the MOST inelastic demand?","o":["A new luxury sports car from a well-known brand","A week-long cruise vacation booked in the peak season","Table salt, which is cheap and has no close substitute","A specific brand of breakfast cereal at the grocery store"],"a":2,"e":"Salt takes a tiny share of the budget and has few substitutes. Brand-specific goods, cruises, and luxury cars have close substitutes or are easy to postpone.","d":2},{"q":"When the price of a good rises 10 percent, quantity supplied rises 15 percent. The price elasticity of supply is","o":["0.67, inelastic","1.5, elastic","0.67, elastic","1.5, inelastic"],"a":1,"e":"PES = 15/10 = 1.5. Greater than 1 is elastic. Inverting gives 0.67, which would mean inelastic.","d":2},{"q":"A pharmaceutical firm sells a life-saving drug with no substitutes, and demand is nearly vertical. A price increase will mostly","o":["leave total revenue unchanged by the increase","create a large surplus of the drug in the market","lower total revenue because many buyers leave","raise total revenue because quantity barely changes"],"a":3,"e":"Near-perfectly inelastic demand means quantity barely falls, so revenue rises almost in proportion to price. The product is still scarce, so no surplus arises.","d":2},{"q":"Along a straight-line downward-sloping demand curve, elasticity","o":["is elastic at high prices and inelastic at low prices","is constant and equal to the slope of the line","is inelastic at high prices and elastic at low prices","is always equal to one at every point on it"],"a":0,"e":"The slope is constant but elasticity depends on the ratio of price to quantity: large at high prices and small at low prices. Unit elasticity occurs only at the midpoint.","d":2},{"q":"Demand is Q = 200 - 4P. At which price is total revenue maximized?","o":["$25","$20","$50","$30"],"a":0,"e":"TR = P(200 - 4P) = 200P - 4P², maximized at P = 25, where Q = 100, TR = $2,500, and PED = 4 x 25/100 = 1. At $20 or $30 TR is $2,400, and at $50 quantity is zero.","d":3},{"q":"A manager raises price by 10 percent, and quantity sold falls by 15 percent. Total revenue will change by about","o":["an increase of 6.5 percent, since demand is inelastic","a decrease of 6.5 percent, since demand is elastic","a decrease of 5 percent, since demand is unit elastic","an increase of 10 percent, since price rose"],"a":1,"e":"New TR is 1.10 x 0.85 = 0.935 of the old, a 6.5 percent fall. Quantity fell by a larger percentage than price rose, so demand is elastic.","d":3},{"q":"When tennis racket prices rise 8 percent, the quantity of tennis balls demanded falls 6 percent. Cross-price elasticity of demand is","o":["-1.33, indicating complements","-0.75, indicating substitutes","+0.75, indicating substitutes","-0.75, indicating complements"],"a":3,"e":"Exy = -6/8 = -0.75 (quantity of balls over price of rackets). Negative means complements. -1.33 inverts the ratio and the sign cannot flip to positive.","d":3},{"q":"Income rises 8 percent and the quantity demanded of generic cereal falls 4 percent. This indicates","o":["a normal good with income elasticity of 0.5","an inferior good with income elasticity of -2","a luxury good with income elasticity of 2","an inferior good with income elasticity of -0.5"],"a":3,"e":"Ey = -4/8 = -0.5. A negative sign marks an inferior good. The value -2 comes from inverting the ratio.","d":3},{"q":"A theater cuts its ticket price from $20 to $16 and attendance rises from 400 to 500. Over this range the midpoint elasticity is about","o":["1.0 (unit elastic), and revenue is unchanged","1.25 (elastic), and revenue rises","1.0 (unit elastic), and revenue rises by 20%","0.8 (inelastic), and revenue falls"],"a":0,"e":"%ΔQ = 100/450 = 22.2% and %ΔP = 4/18 = 22.2%, so elasticity is 1.0. TR is $8,000 before (20 x 400) and after (16 x 500), unchanged.","d":3},{"q":"The price elasticity of demand for a product is 0.5 and the price elasticity of supply is 1.5. If the tax burden is split in proportion to these elasticities, buyers bear about","o":["25 percent of the tax","33 percent of the tax","50 percent of the tax","75 percent of the tax"],"a":3,"e":"Buyers' share = Es / (Es + |Ed|) = 1.5 / 2.0 = 75 percent. The less elastic side (demand) bears more. 25 percent is the sellers' share.","d":3},{"q":"The government taxes a product with inelastic demand and elastic supply. Compared with the legal incidence (a tax collected from sellers), the economic burden falls","o":["mostly on buyers, who cannot easily cut back","equally on buyers and sellers","mostly on sellers because supply is elastic","entirely on sellers, who are legally responsible"],"a":0,"e":"Economic incidence depends on elasticities, not on who sends the money. The less elastic side (buyers) bears more of the tax.","d":3},{"q":"A hotel is fully booked during a conference and cannot add rooms in the short run, while demand for rooms surges. Compared with a market that has spare capacity, the price rise will be","o":["the same, because demand determines price","larger, because supply is inelastic","larger, because supply is elastic","smaller, because supply is inelastic"],"a":1,"e":"When supply is inelastic, a demand increase produces a large price increase and a small quantity increase. With spare capacity (elastic supply), quantity adjusts and the price rises less.","d":3},{"q":"Suppose a profit-maximizing firm with positive marginal costs finds that its demand is inelastic at its current price. The best response is to","o":["lower the price to sell more units and gain share","hold the price, because inelastic demand is ideal","raise the price, raising revenue and lowering costs","lower the price in order to raise revenue"],"a":2,"e":"In the inelastic range a higher price raises revenue and the firm sells fewer units, which cuts total cost. Profit rises. Lowering price would reduce revenue and raise costs.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Elastic","Elasticity greater than 1"],["Inelastic","Elasticity less than 1"],["Midpoint method","Change over average of endpoints"],["Total revenue","Price times quantity"],["Positive cross elasticity","Substitutes"],["Negative cross elasticity","Complements"],["Inferior good","Negative income elasticity"],["Luxury good","Income elasticity above 1"]],"traps":["Trap: Elastic demand means a steep curve. Truth: Elasticity depends on the percent responses, not slope; it varies along a straight line.","Trap: Raise price when demand is elastic to raise revenue. Truth: Elastic demand means price and revenue move opposite; cut price to gain revenue.","Trap: Use the absolute value for cross-price and income elasticity. Truth: Keep the sign; it tells substitutes vs complements and normal vs inferior.","Trap: The side that legally pays the tax bears it. Truth: The less elastic side bears more of the burden.","Trap: Use starting values for percent changes on the exam. Truth: Use the midpoint formula, dividing by the average of old and new values."],"mnemonic":"Elastic = Easy to leave (price and revenue go opposite ways); Inelastic = Stuck with it (price and revenue go the same way).","cram":["PED equals percent change in quantity over percent change in price, using midpoints.","Greater than 1 elastic, less than 1 inelastic, equals 1 unit elastic.","Elastic: cut price to raise revenue. Inelastic: raise price to raise revenue.","Revenue peaks where demand is unit elastic.","Cross-price positive means substitutes; negative means complements.","Income elasticity: above 1 luxury, between 0 and 1 necessity, negative inferior.","Supply is more elastic with spare capacity, inventory, and more time.","Less elastic side bears more tax: buyers' share equals Es over Es plus |Ed|."],"ytv":[{"title":"Elasticity of Demand - Micro Topic 2.3","channel":"Jacob Clifford","id":"HHcblIxiAAk","why":"Clear explanation of elasticity categories with business examples."},{"title":"Micro 2.10 Total Revenue Test and Elasticity Review: Econ Concepts in 60 Seconds","channel":"Jacob Clifford","id":"UlRyUmbAPwg","why":"One-minute refresher on the total revenue test."},{"title":"Price Elasticity of Demand and the Total Revenue Test - HD","channel":"Jason Welker","id":"PoOZWQF-5Yo","why":"Longer worked walk-through of elasticity and revenue."}]},{"id":"u4","title":"Production, Costs & Profit","day":2,"summary":"Understand short versus long run, diminishing returns, fixed and variable costs, average and marginal cost curves, economies of scale, accounting versus economic profit, MR equals MC, shutdown, and break-even.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Short run, long run, and diminishing marginal returns","body":"<p>In economics the <strong>short run</strong> is a period in which at least one input is <em>fixed</em> (a factory building, a lease, a machine), so a firm can change output only by changing <em>variable</em> inputs such as labor and materials. In the <strong>long run</strong> <em>all</em> inputs are variable: the firm can build a new plant, enter or exit the industry. It is not defined by calendar time, only by whether any input is fixed.</p><p><strong>Marginal product (MP)</strong> of an input is the extra output from one more unit of that input. The <strong>law of diminishing marginal returns</strong> says that as you keep adding a variable input to a fixed input, the marginal product <em>eventually</em> falls. Add more workers to the same kitchen and they start getting in each other's way.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> total output with 0 to 5 workers is 0, 10, 24, 36, 44, 48. The marginal products are 10, 14, 12, 8, 4. The 2nd worker adds more than the 1st (specialization), but from the 3rd worker onward MP shrinks. Diminishing returns is why marginal cost eventually rises.</p><p>The link to costs: if each worker costs $200, <code>MC = wage / MP</code>. The 4th worker has MP 8, so MC = 200/8 = $25 per unit; the 5th has MP 4, so MC = $50. When MP falls, MC rises.</p>"},{"h":"2. The cost vocabulary","body":"<p><strong>Fixed costs (FC)</strong> do not change with output in the short run: rent, insurance, salaried managers, loan payments. <strong>Variable costs (VC)</strong> change with output: materials, hourly labor, shipping. <strong>Total cost (TC) = FC + VC.</strong></p><p>Per-unit measures:</p><ul><li><code>AFC = FC / Q</code> (always falls as Q rises: \"spreading the overhead\")</li><li><code>AVC = VC / Q</code></li><li><code>ATC = TC / Q = AFC + AVC</code></li><li><code>MC = change in TC / change in Q</code> (the cost of one more unit; fixed costs never affect it)</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> a bakery has FC = $100. Total cost at 0 through 7 batches per hour is $100, 150, 190, 220, 260, 320, 400, 500. At Q = 4: VC = 160, AFC = 25, AVC = 40, ATC = 65, and MC (3rd to 4th batch) = 260 - 220 = $40.</p><p><strong>Full table:</strong> MC for Q = 1 to 7 is 50, 40, 30, 40, 60, 80, 100. ATC is 150, 95, 73.33, 65, 64, 66.67, 71.43. AVC is 50, 45, 40, 40, 44, 50, 57.14. Keep this table in mind; later sections use it.</p>"},{"h":"3. Shapes of the cost curves and the MC rule","body":"<p>In the short run <strong>MC</strong> first falls (specialization) then rises (diminishing returns), a <strong>U or check-mark shape</strong>. <strong>AVC and ATC</strong> are U-shaped. <strong>AFC</strong> slopes down continuously. The vertical gap between ATC and AVC is AFC, so the gap shrinks as Q grows.</p><p><strong>The key relationship:</strong> the marginal pulls the average. When MC is <em>below</em> an average, the average falls. When MC is <em>above</em> the average, it rises. So <strong>MC crosses AVC and ATC at their minimum points</strong>. It is like your grade average: a score below your average pulls it down.</p><p><strong>Check with the table:</strong> MC at Q = 5 is 60, below ATC(4) = 65, so ATC falls to 64. MC at Q = 6 is 80, above ATC(5) = 64, so ATC rises to 66.67. The minimum ATC (64) occurs at Q = 5, just where MC crosses it. AVC bottoms out at 40 where MC = 40 (Q = 4).</p><p><strong>Exam shortcut:</strong> if given MC and an average, compare them. MC under the average means the average is falling. Also, ATC's minimum is always to the right of AVC's minimum because AFC keeps pulling ATC down after AVC starts to rise.</p>"},{"h":"4. Economies and diseconomies of scale (the long run)","body":"<p>In the long run the firm can change plant size. The <strong>long-run average cost (LRAC)</strong> curve shows the lowest average cost at each output level when all inputs can vary.</p><ul><li><strong>Economies of scale:</strong> LRAC falls as output rises. Causes: specialization of labor, buying inputs in bulk, spreading large fixed investments (technology, marketing), cheaper financing.</li><li><strong>Constant returns to scale:</strong> LRAC is flat.</li><li><strong>Diseconomies of scale:</strong> LRAC rises as output rises. Causes: bureaucracy, communication problems, weaker incentives, coordination costs. The company becomes too complex to manage efficiently.</li></ul><p>The output at the bottom of the LRAC curve is the <strong>minimum efficient scale</strong>.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> a contract manufacturer's total cost is $1,000 at 100 units, $1,600 at 200 units, and $3,600 at 400 units. LRAC is $10, then $8, then $9. From 100 to 200 units it enjoys economies of scale; from 200 to 400 units it suffers diseconomies. Its best scale is near 200 units.</p><p>Do not confuse the two ideas: <em>diminishing marginal returns</em> is a short-run idea with a fixed input; <em>diseconomies of scale</em> is a long-run idea with all inputs variable.</p>"},{"h":"5. Accounting profit vs economic profit","body":"<p><strong>Explicit costs</strong> are actual payments (wages, materials, rent paid). <strong>Implicit costs</strong> are the opportunity cost of resources the owner already owns and uses: the owner's own time, owned buildings, and the return on the owner's invested money.</p><ul><li><strong>Accounting profit = total revenue - explicit costs.</strong></li><li><strong>Economic profit = total revenue - explicit costs - implicit costs</strong> (also called revenue minus total opportunity cost).</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> a consulting firm earns $500,000 revenue and pays $350,000 of explicit costs, so accounting profit = $150,000. But the owner gave up a $90,000 salary, uses a building that could be rented for $30,000, and tied up capital that could earn $10,000 elsewhere. Implicit costs = $130,000. Economic profit = 150,000 - 130,000 = <strong>$20,000</strong>.</p><p>When economic profit equals zero the firm earns <strong>normal profit</strong>: just enough to keep resources in this use. Positive economic profit attracts new firms; negative economic profit drives firms out of the industry in the long run. Accounting profit is always at least as large as economic profit when implicit costs are positive.</p>"},{"h":"6. Profit maximization: MR = MC","body":"<p><strong>Marginal revenue (MR)</strong> is the extra revenue from selling one more unit. Profit is highest at the quantity where <strong>MR = MC</strong>, applying the marginal rule from Unit 1. If MR is greater than MC, producing one more unit adds more revenue than cost, so expand. If MR is less than MC, cut back.</p><p>For a <strong>price taker</strong> (a firm in a competitive market that sells at the market price regardless of how much it sells), <strong>MR = P</strong>, so the rule becomes <strong>P = MC</strong> on the rising part of the MC curve.</p><p><strong>Using the table</strong> (MC: 50, 40, 30, 40, 60, 80, 100 for Q = 1 to 7): produce a unit whenever its MC is no more than the price (if MC exactly equals P, profit is the same either way). At P = $55 the 4th unit costs 40 (produce it) and the 5th costs 60 (skip it), so <strong>Q = 4</strong>. Revenue = 55 x 4 = 220, TC = 260, profit = -$40 (a loss). At P = $85 the 6th unit costs 80 (produce) and the 7th costs 100 (skip), so Q = 6: revenue 510, TC 400, profit = <strong>+$110</strong>.</p><p>Profit per unit = P - ATC, and total profit = (P - ATC) x Q. A firm may be profitable even when its MC is far above its ATC. Notice that the best quantity does <em>not</em> come from the minimum of ATC; it comes from MR = MC.</p>"},{"h":"7. Shutdown rule and break-even","body":"<p>In the short run, fixed costs are <strong>sunk</strong> (owed whether the firm runs or not). So the firm compares operating with shutting down:</p><ul><li>If it shuts down, it loses exactly its fixed costs.</li><li>If it operates, it loses fixed costs minus whatever revenue exceeds variable costs.</li></ul><p><strong>Shutdown rule:</strong> operate if <code>P &gt;= AVC</code> (total revenue covers variable cost); shut down if <code>P &lt; AVC</code>. In the table, at P = $55 and Q = 4, AVC = 40, so the firm operates, losing $40 rather than the $100 it would lose by shutting down. At P = $35, below the minimum AVC of 40, every unit loses money before fixed costs; shut down.</p><p><strong>Three zones for price:</strong> P above ATC: economic profit. P between AVC and ATC: loss, but keep operating in the short run. P below AVC: shut down. In the long run a firm exits if P stays below ATC.</p><p><strong>Break-even</strong> (managerial version): the sales volume at which total revenue equals total cost. With a constant price and constant variable cost per unit, <code>Q* = FC / (P - AVC)</code>, where P - AVC is the <em>contribution margin</em> per unit. A firm with FC = $100,000, P = $50, and variable cost $30 per unit breaks even at 100,000/20 = <strong>5,000 units</strong>.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["In the short run at least one input is fixed. In the long run all inputs are variable. The distinction is about whether any input is fixed, not about calendar time.","The law of diminishing marginal returns says that as you add more of a variable input to a fixed input, the marginal product eventually falls, which makes marginal cost eventually rise.","Total cost equals fixed cost plus variable cost. Average total cost equals total cost divided by quantity, and it also equals average fixed cost plus average variable cost.","Marginal cost is the change in total cost from producing one more unit. Fixed costs do not affect marginal cost.","Average fixed cost always falls as output rises. Average variable cost and average total cost are U shaped.","Marginal cost crosses average variable cost and average total cost at their minimum points. When marginal cost is below an average, the average falls, and when it is above, the average rises.","Economies of scale mean long run average cost falls as output grows. Diseconomies of scale mean it rises, usually because of bureaucracy and coordination problems.","Accounting profit is revenue minus explicit costs. Economic profit also subtracts implicit costs, the opportunity cost of owned resources like the owner's time and capital.","Zero economic profit is called normal profit. It means the firm earns just enough to keep its resources in this use.","A profit maximizing firm produces the quantity where marginal revenue equals marginal cost. For a price taker, marginal revenue equals price.","The short run shutdown rule is to shut down if price is below average variable cost. If price is between average variable cost and average total cost, keep operating because you lose less than your fixed costs.","Break even quantity equals fixed cost divided by the contribution margin, which is price minus variable cost per unit."],"examples":[{"q":"A firm's fixed cost is $100 and its total cost at 3 and 4 units of output is $220 and $260. Find at Q = 4: variable cost, AFC, AVC, ATC, and the marginal cost of the 4th unit.","steps":["VC at Q = 4: TC - FC = 260 - 100 = 160.","AFC = 100 / 4 = 25. AVC = 160 / 4 = 40.","ATC = 260 / 4 = 65, which also equals AFC + AVC = 25 + 40.","MC of the 4th unit = 260 - 220 = 40."],"answer":"VC = $160, AFC = $25, AVC = $40, ATC = $65, MC = $40."},{"q":"A price-taking firm has total costs of $100, 150, 190, 220, 260, 320, 400, 500 at output 0 through 7. Market price is $55. How many units should it produce, what is its profit, and should it shut down?","steps":["Marginal costs for Q = 1 to 7: 50, 40, 30, 40, 60, 80, 100. Produce each unit whose MC is no more than the price of 55: units 1 through 4 qualify; the 5th costs 60, so Q = 4.","Revenue = 55 x 4 = 220. TC(4) = 260. Profit = 220 - 260 = -$40.","Shutdown test: AVC at Q = 4 is (260 - 100)/4 = 40. Price 55 is greater than 40, so revenue covers variable cost.","Shutting down would lose all fixed cost: -$100. Operating loses only $40."],"answer":"Produce 4 units, take a $40 loss, and keep operating, because P exceeds AVC and the loss is smaller than the $100 shutdown loss."},{"q":"A consulting firm has revenue of $500,000 and explicit costs of $350,000. The owner gave up a $90,000 salary, owns the building (rent value $30,000), and has $10,000 of forgone return on invested funds. Find accounting and economic profit. Separately, a product has FC = $100,000, price = $50, and variable cost = $30 per unit; find break-even volume.","steps":["Accounting profit = 500,000 - 350,000 = $150,000.","Implicit costs = 90,000 + 30,000 + 10,000 = $130,000.","Economic profit = 150,000 - 130,000 = $20,000.","Break-even: contribution margin = 50 - 30 = $20. Q* = 100,000 / 20 = 5,000 units."],"answer":"Accounting profit $150,000; economic profit $20,000; break-even at 5,000 units."}],"videos":[{"title":"Short-run cost curves: MC, AVC, ATC","query":"short run cost curves marginal cost average total cost average variable cost explained"},{"title":"Economies and diseconomies of scale","query":"economies of scale diseconomies of scale long run average cost explained"},{"title":"Profit maximization MR=MC and shutdown rule","query":"profit maximization MR equals MC shutdown rule perfect competition explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Short run","b":"Period when at least one input is fixed"},{"f":"Long run","b":"Period when all inputs are variable"},{"f":"Marginal product","b":"Extra output from one more unit of an input"},{"f":"Law of diminishing marginal returns","b":"Adding a variable input to a fixed one eventually lowers its marginal product"},{"f":"Fixed cost","b":"Does not change with output in the short run"},{"f":"Variable cost","b":"Changes with the level of output"},{"f":"Total cost","b":"Fixed cost plus variable cost"},{"f":"Average total cost","b":"Total cost divided by quantity; equals AFC plus AVC"},{"f":"Average variable cost","b":"Variable cost divided by quantity"},{"f":"Average fixed cost","b":"Fixed cost divided by quantity; always falls"},{"f":"Marginal cost","b":"Change in total cost from one more unit"},{"f":"MC and wage link","b":"MC equals wage divided by marginal product of labor"},{"f":"MC crosses ATC and AVC where?","b":"At their minimum points"},{"f":"MC below average","b":"The average cost is falling"},{"f":"Economies of scale","b":"Long-run average cost falls as output rises"},{"f":"Diseconomies of scale","b":"Long-run average cost rises as output rises"},{"f":"Explicit vs implicit costs","b":"Actual payments vs opportunity cost of owned resources"},{"f":"Accounting profit","b":"Revenue minus explicit costs"},{"f":"Economic profit","b":"Revenue minus explicit and implicit costs"},{"f":"Normal profit","b":"Zero economic profit"},{"f":"Profit-maximizing rule","b":"Produce where MR equals MC"},{"f":"Shutdown rule","b":"Shut down in the short run if P is below AVC"}],"questions":[{"q":"In economics, the short run is best defined as a period in which","o":["no inputs can be changed","at least one input is fixed","all inputs are variable","less than one year passes"],"a":1,"e":"The short run is defined by the presence of a fixed input, not by calendar time. When every input can vary, it is the long run.","d":1},{"q":"The law of diminishing marginal returns states that, as more of a variable input is added to a fixed input,","o":["the average cost of the fixed inputs eventually rises","total output eventually falls to zero as workers are added","the firm's output stops growing the moment a worker is added","the marginal product of the variable input eventually declines"],"a":3,"e":"Diminishing returns says that the extra output from each additional unit of the variable input eventually shrinks. Total output may still rise, and the effect begins at some point, not necessarily immediately.","d":1},{"q":"Which of the following is a fixed cost in the short run?","o":["Monthly rent on a factory lease","Raw materials for each unit made","Shipping charges per order","Hourly wages for production workers"],"a":0,"e":"Fixed costs do not vary with output, as with a lease. Materials, hourly wages, and shipping rise as output rises, so they are variable.","d":1},{"q":"Marginal cost is","o":["the average cost of the last ten units produced","total fixed cost spread over all units produced","the extra cost of producing one more unit","total cost divided by the quantity produced"],"a":2,"e":"MC is the change in total cost from one more unit. Total cost divided by quantity is average total cost.","d":1},{"q":"Average fixed cost, as output increases,","o":["falls, then rises","continually falls","stays constant","rises, then falls"],"a":1,"e":"AFC equals fixed cost divided by output; as Q grows the same fixed cost is spread over more units, so AFC always falls.","d":1},{"q":"When a firm's long-run average cost falls as it increases output, the firm is experiencing","o":["constant returns to scale","diseconomies of scale","diminishing marginal returns","economies of scale"],"a":3,"e":"Falling LRAC with larger output is the definition of economies of scale. Diminishing returns is a short-run concept; constant returns means LRAC is flat.","d":1},{"q":"Accounting profit equals total revenue minus","o":["implicit costs","variable costs only","explicit costs","explicit and implicit costs"],"a":2,"e":"Accountants subtract only actual payments (explicit costs). Economists additionally subtract implicit costs to get economic profit.","d":1},{"q":"A profit-maximizing firm chooses the output at which","o":["total revenue is at its maximum","marginal cost equals average fixed cost","average total cost is at its minimum","marginal revenue equals marginal cost"],"a":3,"e":"The marginal rule says to expand output until the extra revenue equals the extra cost. Maximum revenue and minimum ATC are different points that do not necessarily maximize profit.","d":1},{"q":"A firm has fixed costs of $40,000 and variable costs of $60,000 when it produces 2,000 units. Its average total cost is","o":["$100","$50","$20","$30"],"a":1,"e":"TC = 40,000 + 60,000 = 100,000, so ATC = 100,000 / 2,000 = $50. $30 is AVC, $20 is AFC, and $100 is just the total in thousands.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's total cost rises from $220 to $260 when it increases output from 3 to 4 units. The marginal cost of the 4th unit is","o":["$260","$65","$40","$60"],"a":2,"e":"MC = change in TC / change in Q = (260 - 220) / 1 = $40. $65 would be average total cost (260 / 4).","d":2},{"q":"Total cost at 5 units is $320 and fixed cost is $100. Average variable cost at 5 units is","o":["$20","$320","$44","$64"],"a":2,"e":"VC = 320 - 100 = 220, so AVC = 220 / 5 = $44. $64 is ATC (320 / 5) and $20 is AFC.","d":2},{"q":"Output with 3 workers is 36 units, and with 4 workers it is 44 units. The marginal product of the 4th worker is","o":["8 units","11 units","44 units","80 units"],"a":0,"e":"MP is the change in output from one more worker: 44 - 36 = 8. 11 is the average product (44 / 4).","d":2},{"q":"Each worker costs $200 and the marginal product of the 4th worker is 8 units. The marginal cost of an extra unit is","o":["$8","$1,600","$200","$25"],"a":3,"e":"MC = wage / MP = 200 / 8 = $25. Multiplying (1,600) uses the wrong operation, and $200 is the wage itself.","d":2},{"q":"At 100 units, a firm's average total cost is $50 and the marginal cost of the 101st unit is $30. As a result, average total cost will","o":["fall","rise","stay at $50","rise to $80"],"a":0,"e":"When marginal cost is below the average, the average is pulled down. MC of $30 is less than the $50 average, so ATC falls.","d":2},{"q":"Marginal cost curves cross the AVC and ATC curves at their minimum points because","o":["MC is always equal to the average at every level of output","fixed costs fall to zero at the minimum point","marginal cost never changes as the level of output changes in the short run","MC above an average raises it, and MC below an average lowers it"],"a":3,"e":"The marginal pulls the average. At the minimum, the average stops falling and starts rising, which is exactly where MC equals the average.","d":2},{"q":"Which is a common cause of diseconomies of scale?","o":["Communication problems and bureaucracy in a very large firm","Spreading fixed costs over many more units of total output","Bulk discounts on large purchases of inputs","Greater specialization of labor and management in very large plants"],"a":0,"e":"As firms get very large, coordination and management costs can offset gains. The other options are sources of economies of scale.","d":2},{"q":"A restaurant owner earns accounting profit of $100,000. She gave up an $80,000 job to run it and has no other implicit costs. Her economic profit is","o":["$180,000","$100,000","$20,000","-$20,000"],"a":2,"e":"Economic profit = accounting profit - implicit costs = 100,000 - 80,000 = $20,000. Adding the salary ($180,000) goes the wrong way.","d":2},{"q":"If a firm earns zero economic profit, then it","o":["is earning a normal profit, just covering all opportunity costs","is earning a loss relative to its next-best use of resources","has zero accounting profit","is losing money and should exit immediately"],"a":0,"e":"Zero economic profit means revenue exactly covers explicit and implicit costs: a normal profit. The accounting profit is typically positive.","d":2},{"q":"For a firm that is a price taker, marginal revenue equals","o":["zero","the market price","average fixed cost","total revenue divided by quantity minus one"],"a":1,"e":"A price taker sells every unit at the market price, so each extra unit adds exactly P to revenue.","d":2},{"q":"In the short run, a firm should shut down if the market price is below","o":["average fixed cost","average total cost","average variable cost","marginal revenue"],"a":2,"e":"If P is below AVC the firm cannot even cover variable costs, so each unit produced adds to the loss. Between AVC and ATC it should keep operating.","d":2},{"q":"The vertical distance between the ATC curve and the AVC curve at any output equals","o":["average fixed cost, which shrinks as output rises","total fixed cost, which stays constant","marginal cost, which stays constant","average fixed cost, which grows as output rises"],"a":0,"e":"ATC = AVC + AFC, so the gap is AFC = FC / Q. As Q rises the same fixed cost is spread thinner and the two curves converge. Total fixed cost is not a per-unit gap.","d":2},{"q":"A price-taking firm has total costs at output 0 through 7 of $100, 150, 190, 220, 260, 320, 400, and 500. At a market price of $55, the profit-maximizing output is","o":["3 units","4 units","2 units","5 units"],"a":1,"e":"MC for units 1 to 7 is 50, 40, 30, 40, 60, 80, 100. The 4th unit costs $40 for $55 of revenue (profit rises), but the 5th costs $60, which exceeds the price. Profit: -55 at 3 units, -40 at 4 units, -45 at 5 units.","d":3},{"q":"Using the same cost data, if the market price rises to $85, the firm's maximum profit is","o":["$85","$510","$105","$110"],"a":3,"e":"At P = 85, the 6th unit (MC 80) adds profit and the 7th (MC 100) does not, so Q = 6. Revenue = 510, TC = 400, profit = $110. $105 is the profit at only 5 units, and $510 is revenue.","d":3},{"q":"Using the same cost data with a market price of $55, the firm produces 4 units and loses $40. The best decision is to","o":["keep operating, since the loss is under the $100 fixed cost","raise output to 6 units so that revenue covers the fixed cost","shut down, because profit is negative in this period of production","shut down, because the price is below the ATC of $65 per unit"],"a":0,"e":"AVC at 4 units is 40, below the $55 price. Operating loses $40, shutting down loses $100 of fixed cost. A loss alone, or P below ATC, is not the shutdown rule.","d":3},{"q":"A product has fixed costs of $100,000, a price of $50, and a variable cost of $30 per unit. The break-even volume is","o":["1,250 units","5,000 units","2,000 units","3,333 units"],"a":1,"e":"Contribution margin = 50 - 30 = $20 per unit. Q* = 100,000 / 20 = 5,000. 2,000 divides FC by price only, 3,333 divides by variable cost, and 1,250 divides by P + VC.","d":3},{"q":"A manufacturer's total costs are $1,000 at 100 units, $1,600 at 200 units, and $3,600 at 400 units. Which statement is correct?","o":["LRAC rises throughout the range, which shows diseconomies of scale at every output","LRAC falls throughout the range, which shows economies of scale at every output","LRAC is constant at $8, which shows constant returns to scale","LRAC falls from $10 to $8, then rises to $9: economies, then diseconomies"],"a":3,"e":"LRAC = TC / Q = 10, 8, and 9. The minimum is at 200 units; beyond it, costs per unit rise.","d":3},{"q":"A consulting firm has revenue of $500,000 and explicit costs of $350,000. The owner forgoes a $90,000 salary, owns a building that could be rented for $30,000, and has $10,000 of forgone return on invested funds. Economic profit is","o":["$130,000","$280,000","$20,000","$150,000"],"a":2,"e":"Accounting profit = 150,000; implicit costs = 130,000; economic profit = 20,000. $150,000 is accounting profit and $130,000 is the implicit cost total.","d":3},{"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes diminishing marginal returns from diseconomies of scale?","o":["Diminishing returns is a long-run idea; diseconomies of scale is short-run","Diminishing returns is a short-run idea; diseconomies of scale is long-run","Both occur only in the long run when all inputs vary","Both describe a fall in the average cost of production"],"a":1,"e":"Diminishing returns needs a fixed input (short run). Diseconomies of scale describe rising long-run average cost when every input can be changed. Neither describes falling average cost.","d":3},{"q":"A restaurant's rent of $10,000 per month is non-cancelable. In a slow month revenue is $12,000 and variable costs are $9,000. The owner should","o":["stay open, because the loss is $7,000 rather than $10,000","stay open only if revenue covers the full $10,000 rent","close, because the month shows a $7,000 loss on the books","close, because revenue is below total costs of $19,000"],"a":0,"e":"Rent is unavoidable. Operating contributes 12,000 - 9,000 = $3,000 toward it, for a loss of $7,000 versus $10,000 if closed. The shutdown rule uses variable cost.","d":3},{"q":"A firm's total cost at 5 units is $320 (ATC = $64) and the marginal cost of the 6th unit is $80. Average total cost at 6 units is","o":["$64.00","$66.67","$80.00","$72.00"],"a":1,"e":"TC(6) = 320 + 80 = 400, so ATC = 400 / 6 = $66.67. It rises only slightly because MC is above ATC, but it does not jump to MC. $72 is the simple average of 64 and 80.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Short run","At least one input fixed"],["Fixed cost","Unchanging with output"],["Marginal cost","Extra cost of one more unit"],["Economies of scale","Average cost falls as output grows"],["Implicit cost","Opportunity cost of owned resources"],["Normal profit","Zero economic profit"],["MR = MC","Profit-maximizing output rule"],["Shutdown rule","Close if price is below AVC"]],"traps":["Trap: Diminishing returns and diseconomies of scale are the same. Truth: The first is short-run with a fixed input; the second is long-run.","Trap: Fixed costs affect marginal cost. Truth: MC includes only costs that change with output.","Trap: Shut down whenever you lose money. Truth: Shut down only if P is below AVC; between AVC and ATC keep operating in the short run.","Trap: Produce at minimum ATC to maximize profit. Truth: Profit is maximized where MR equals MC.","Trap: Accounting profit tells you if the owner is better off. Truth: Subtract implicit costs to get economic profit."],"mnemonic":"MC pulls the average: low MC drags averages down, high MC drags them up, so MC crosses at the minimum. Then: Make where MR=MC, Stay if P is at least AVC.","cram":["Short run has a fixed input; long run has all inputs variable.","TC equals FC plus VC; ATC equals AFC plus AVC.","MC crosses AVC and ATC at their minimum points.","MC below average pulls the average down; above pulls it up.","Economic profit equals revenue minus explicit minus implicit costs.","Maximize profit where MR equals MC; price takers set P equals MC.","Shut down in the short run only if P is below AVC.","Break-even quantity equals fixed cost divided by price minus unit variable cost."],"ytv":[{"title":"Short-Run Cost Curves (Part 2) - Micro Topic 3.2","channel":"Jacob Clifford","id":"qYKJdooEnwU","why":"Walks through MC, AVC, and ATC and how they relate."},{"title":"Marginal cost, average variable cost, and average total cost | AP Microeconomics","channel":"Khan Academy","id":"mW6Cwpfb5CI","why":"Builds the cost curves step by step with the MC crossing rule."},{"title":"Short Run Cost Curves | Think Econ","channel":"Think Econ","id":"zmAVYD0dYSo","why":"Another clear presentation of the short-run cost curve relationships."}]},{"id":"u5","title":"Market Structures","day":3,"summary":"Learn how perfect competition, monopoly, monopolistic competition and oligopoly differ in pricing power, profit, and efficiency, and how managers and regulators use concentration measures, price discrimination and antitrust law.","lesson":[{"h":"1. The big idea: how much pricing power does a firm have?","body":"<p>A <strong>market structure</strong> is a label for how competitive an industry is. Economists sort industries by four questions: How many sellers are there? Are the products identical or differentiated? How hard is it for new firms to enter? How much control does a single firm have over price?</p><ul><li><strong>Perfect competition:</strong> very many sellers, identical product, free entry. Firms are <em>price takers</em>.</li><li><strong>Monopolistic competition:</strong> many sellers, differentiated products, free entry. Firms have a little pricing power.</li><li><strong>Oligopoly:</strong> a few big sellers whose decisions affect each other. Entry is hard.</li><li><strong>Monopoly:</strong> one seller, no close substitutes, high barriers to entry. The firm is a <em>price maker</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Business example:</strong> a farmer selling a commodity like wheat has no control over price. A local water utility has plenty. A manager's job changes depending on which world the firm lives in: in perfect competition you manage costs; in monopolistic competition you manage branding; in oligopoly you manage rivals; in monopoly you manage regulators and public opinion.</p><p>The common thread is the profit-maximizing rule you learned earlier: <strong>produce the quantity where marginal revenue (MR) equals marginal cost (MC)</strong>. What changes across structures is what MR looks like.</p>"},{"h":"2. Perfect competition: P = MR = MC","body":"<p>In <strong>perfect competition</strong> each firm is so small relative to the market that it cannot change the price. The market sets the price; the firm is a <strong>price taker</strong>. The firm's demand curve is a horizontal line at the market price, so every extra unit sells for the same price. That means <code>MR = P</code>.</p><p>Profit maximization (MR = MC) therefore becomes <code>P = MR = MC</code>. If the market price is $30 and MC = 2q, the firm produces where 2q = 30, so q = 15 units. If average total cost (ATC) at 15 units is $25, profit is ($30 - $25) x 15 = <strong>$75</strong>.</p><p><strong>Short-run rule:</strong> if price covers average variable cost (AVC) but not ATC, the firm still produces, because the revenue above AVC helps pay fixed costs it owes anyway. If price falls below AVC, it should <strong>shut down</strong> in the short run. In the long run, a firm that cannot cover ATC exits.</p><p><strong>Long-run result:</strong> positive economic profit attracts entrants, supply shifts right, and price falls. Losses push firms out, supply shifts left, and price rises. The process stops when price equals minimum ATC and <strong>economic profit is zero</strong>. Zero economic profit does not mean zero accounting profit; it means owners earn exactly their next-best return (a <em>normal profit</em>).</p><p>Perfect competition is efficient: P = MC (allocative efficiency) and output is at minimum ATC (productive efficiency).</p>"},{"h":"3. Monopoly: the price maker","body":"<p>A <strong>monopoly</strong> is the only seller of a product with no close substitutes. It persists because of <strong>barriers to entry</strong>: legal barriers (patents, licenses, exclusive franchises), control of a key resource, and <strong>economies of scale</strong> so large that one firm is cheapest. A <strong>natural monopoly</strong> (water, electric distribution, rail track) is the last case: average cost keeps falling over the whole market, so two firms would each have higher costs than one.</p><p><strong>Why MR is below price:</strong> a monopoly faces the whole downward-sloping market demand curve. To sell one more unit it must cut the price on <em>every</em> unit. So MR < P, and the MR curve lies below demand (for straight-line demand, MR falls twice as fast).</p><p><strong>Worked example:</strong> demand is P = 100 - Q and MC = $20. Then MR = 100 - 2Q. Set MR = MC: 100 - 2Q = 20, so Q = 40. Read the price from <em>demand</em> (not MR): P = 100 - 40 = <strong>$60</strong>. Profit = ($60 - $20) x 40 = $1,600. A competitive industry would produce where P = MC: Q = 80 at $20.</p><p><strong>Deadweight loss (DWL):</strong> the monopoly produces 40 instead of 80 units. Units 41 to 80 are worth more to buyers than they cost to make, but they are never produced. The lost surplus is the triangle 0.5 x ($60 - $20) x (80 - 40) = <strong>$800</strong>.</p><p>Two more facts: a monopolist never operates on the inelastic part of demand (MR would be negative), and a monopoly is not guaranteed a profit, only the power to choose its price. Regulators of natural monopolies often use <strong>average-cost pricing</strong> (P = ATC) because forcing P = MC would make the firm lose money.</p>"},{"h":"4. Monopolistic competition: many firms, differentiated products","body":"<p>Think restaurants, hair salons, coffee shops, clothing brands. Many firms sell <strong>differentiated</strong> (similar but not identical) products and entry is easy. Because each brand is a little different, each firm faces a downward-sloping (but quite elastic) demand curve, so it has some pricing power. Like a monopoly, MR < P, and it produces where MR = MC.</p><p><strong>Short run:</strong> a hit new product can earn positive economic profit (P above ATC).</p><p><strong>Long run:</strong> profit attracts copycats. Entry takes customers away, so each existing firm's demand curve shifts left and becomes more elastic until it just touches the ATC curve. Result: <strong>zero economic profit, but P > MC</strong>. The firm also ends up on the falling part of its ATC curve, producing less than the minimum-ATC output. This gap is called <strong>excess capacity</strong>.</p><p><strong>Why firms still like it:</strong> advertising, branding, and quality differences are ways to make demand less elastic. Consumers get variety in return for slightly higher prices. Managers compete on <em>non-price</em> factors: design, location, service, and reputation.</p>"},{"h":"5. Oligopoly: when rivals' reactions matter","body":"<p>An <strong>oligopoly</strong> has a few large firms (airlines, wireless carriers, soft drinks, cereal). Entry is hard because of scale, brand, or technology. The key feature is <strong>interdependence</strong>: each firm's best move depends on what rivals do. That is why game theory (next unit) is so useful here.</p><p><strong>Kinked demand idea:</strong> suppose a firm believes rivals will <em>match a price cut</em> (so the firm gains little volume) but will <em>ignore a price increase</em> (so the firm loses lots of volume). Demand is then elastic above the current price and inelastic below it, creating a kink. Because of the kink there is a gap in the MR curve, and prices tend to stay <strong>sticky</strong> even if costs change a bit. (This model explains stickiness; it does not explain how the original price was set.)</p><p><strong>Cartels and collusion:</strong> a <strong>cartel</strong> is a group of firms that agree to limit output and raise price, acting like a joint monopoly. OPEC is the classic example. Cartels are unstable because each member earns more by secretly producing extra at the high cartel price (cheating), and in the United States explicit price-fixing is illegal. <strong>Tacit collusion</strong> (following a price leader without a formal agreement) is harder to prosecute.</p><p>Oligopolies can end up anywhere between near-competitive and near-monopoly results. Typical features: non-price competition (advertising, loyalty programs), price leadership, and strategic moves such as capacity expansion.</p>"},{"h":"6. Measuring concentration and price discrimination","body":"<p><strong>Concentration ratio (CR4):</strong> the combined market share of the four largest firms. <strong>Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI):</strong> square each firm's percentage market share, then add them up. For shares of 40, 30, 20, and 10: HHI = 1,600 + 900 + 400 + 100 = <strong>3,000</strong>. (CR4 = 100%.) A pure monopoly has HHI = 10,000; a market of thousands of tiny firms is near 0. Squaring means big firms count much more.</p><p><strong>Merger shortcut:</strong> if two firms with shares a and b merge, HHI rises by <code>2 x a x b</code>. Merging a 20% firm and a 10% firm adds 2 x 20 x 10 = <strong>400</strong> points. Regulators look at both the post-merger HHI and the change. Thresholds have been revised over time (older guidelines flagged about 2,500 as highly concentrated; the 2023 guidelines lowered the bar to about 1,800), so focus on the calculation and the logic.</p><p><strong>Price discrimination</strong> means charging different customers different prices for the same good when the cost difference does not explain it. It requires three things: (1) <strong>market power</strong> (a downward-sloping demand curve), (2) the ability to <strong>separate customers</strong> by willingness to pay or elasticity, and (3) <strong>no resale</strong> (arbitrage) between groups. Examples: student and senior discounts, airline fares by booking date, coupons, business vs. consumer software licenses.</p><p><strong>Numbers:</strong> segment A has P = 100 - Q and segment B has P = 60 - Q, MC = $10. Charging each separately gives A: Q = 45 at $55 and B: Q = 25 at $35, profit = $2,025 + $625 = $2,650. One uniform price gives P = $45 and Q = 70, profit = $2,450. Discrimination adds $200 and charges the <em>less elastic</em> group more.</p>"},{"h":"7. Antitrust law and mergers","body":"<p>Antitrust law tries to preserve competition. Size alone is not illegal; <em>harmful conduct</em> is.</p><ul><li><strong>Sherman Act (1890):</strong> Section 1 bans contracts, combinations, and conspiracies in restraint of trade (price fixing, bid rigging, market division). Section 2 bans monopolization and attempts to monopolize (using unfair tactics to gain or keep a monopoly). Gaining dominance with a better product is not a violation.</li><li><strong>Clayton Act (1914):</strong> targets practices that may <em>substantially lessen competition</em>: certain mergers and acquisitions, tying and exclusive dealing, and interlocking directorates. Later amendments added premerger notification (Hart-Scott-Rodino Act, 1976) and addressed price discrimination (Robinson-Patman Act, 1936).</li><li><strong>Federal Trade Commission Act (1914):</strong> created the FTC and bans unfair methods of competition. The DOJ Antitrust Division and the FTC enforce antitrust law.</li></ul><p><strong>Mergers</strong> come in three flavors: <em>horizontal</em> (competitors combine, most scrutinized), <em>vertical</em> (supplier and customer combine), and <em>conglomerate</em> (unrelated businesses). Regulators weigh higher market power against efficiency gains such as lower costs. They may approve a merger with conditions, such as selling off stores.</p><p><strong>Manager takeaway:</strong> never discuss prices, bids, or territories with a competitor. Talking is enough to create legal risk, even if no deal is struck.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["A market structure describes how competitive an industry is, based on the number of sellers, product differences, ease of entry, and control over price.","In perfect competition, firms are price takers, so price equals marginal revenue equals marginal cost, and long run economic profit is zero.","Zero economic profit does not mean the owners earn nothing. It means they earn exactly their next best return, called normal profit.","A monopoly is protected by barriers to entry such as patents, licenses, control of key resources, and economies of scale. A natural monopoly is one where a single firm has the lowest cost.","For a monopoly, marginal revenue is less than price, because selling more requires cutting the price on every unit. The monopolist sets marginal revenue equal to marginal cost and then reads the price off the demand curve.","Compared with perfect competition, a monopoly produces less, charges more, and creates a deadweight loss, which is surplus that disappears because some worthwhile units are never made.","Monopolistic competition has many firms selling differentiated products with easy entry. In the long run, profit is zero, but price stays above marginal cost, leaving excess capacity.","Oligopoly means a few interdependent firms. Kinked demand explains sticky prices, and cartels try to act like a monopoly but are unstable because members have an incentive to cheat.","The Herfindahl Hirschman Index squares each firm's percentage share and adds them. When two firms merge, the index rises by two times the product of their shares.","Price discrimination needs market power, a way to separate customers by willingness to pay, and no resale. The firm charges more to the less price sensitive group.","The Sherman Act bans price fixing and monopolization. The Clayton Act targets mergers and practices that may substantially lessen competition. The Federal Trade Commission and the Justice Department enforce them.","For the exam, always start by asking how many sellers there are, whether the products are identical, and how easy it is to enter, and then apply the rule that marginal revenue equals marginal cost."],"examples":[{"q":"A software firm is the only seller of a niche inventory tool. Demand is P = 100 - Q (Q in thousands of licenses) and marginal cost is a constant $20. Find the profit-maximizing quantity and price, the profit (ignore fixed costs), and the deadweight loss versus perfect competition.","steps":["Total revenue is P x Q = 100Q - Q^2, so marginal revenue is MR = 100 - 2Q.","Set MR = MC: 100 - 2Q = 20, so Q = 40.","Find price from the DEMAND curve: P = 100 - 40 = 60.","Profit = (P - MC) x Q = (60 - 20) x 40 = 1,600.","Competitive output would set P = MC: 100 - Q = 20, so Q = 80.","DWL = 0.5 x (60 - 20) x (80 - 40) = 0.5 x 40 x 40 = 800."],"answer":"Q = 40, P = $60, profit = $1,600, deadweight loss = $800."},{"q":"A market has five firms with shares of 30%, 25%, 20%, 15%, and 10%. (a) Find CR4 and HHI. (b) The 20% firm and the 10% firm propose to merge. Find the new HHI.","steps":["CR4 = 30 + 25 + 20 + 15 = 90 percent.","HHI before = 900 + 625 + 400 + 225 + 100 = 2,250.","Merger change = 2 x 20 x 10 = 400.","New HHI = 2,250 + 400 = 2,650.","Check directly: shares become 30, 25, 30, 15 so HHI = 900 + 625 + 900 + 225 = 2,650."],"answer":"CR4 = 90%, HHI = 2,250 before and 2,650 after the merger (a 400-point increase)."},{"q":"A wheat farm in a perfectly competitive market takes the price of $30 per unit. Marginal cost is MC = 2q. At the profit-maximizing output, average total cost is $25. Find output and economic profit, then say what you expect in the long run.","steps":["Price taker: MR = P = 30.","Set P = MC: 30 = 2q, so q = 15.","Revenue = 30 x 15 = 450. Total cost = 25 x 15 = 375.","Economic profit = 450 - 375 = 75 (or (30 - 25) x 15).","Positive profit attracts entry, which shifts market supply right and lowers the price.","Entry continues until price falls to minimum ATC and economic profit is zero."],"answer":"q = 15 units, profit = $75 now, and zero economic profit in the long run as new farms enter."}],"videos":[{"title":"Monopoly explained: marginal revenue, price, and deadweight loss","query":"monopoly microeconomics marginal revenue deadweight loss graph explained"},{"title":"Perfect competition vs monopolistic competition long run","query":"perfect competition vs monopolistic competition long run zero economic profit"},{"title":"Oligopoly, kinked demand curve, and cartels","query":"oligopoly kinked demand curve cartel collusion explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"What is a price taker?","b":"A firm that cannot influence the market price, so it sells every unit at the going price (perfect competition)."},{"f":"Profit-maximizing rule for any firm","b":"Produce the quantity where marginal revenue equals marginal cost (MR = MC)."},{"f":"Perfectly competitive firm: MR vs price","b":"MR equals P, because each extra unit sells at the same market price. So P = MR = MC at the optimum."},{"f":"Long-run economic profit in perfect competition","b":"Zero. Entry and exit push price to minimum ATC."},{"f":"Normal profit","b":"The return owners could earn in their next-best use. Zero economic profit means normal profit is earned."},{"f":"When does a competitive firm shut down in the short run?","b":"When price is below average variable cost (P < AVC)."},{"f":"Barriers to entry (examples)","b":"Patents, government licenses, control of a key resource, large economies of scale, network effects, brand loyalty."},{"f":"Why is MR below price for a monopoly?","b":"To sell more, it must lower the price on all units, not just the extra unit."},{"f":"How does a monopolist set price?","b":"Find Q where MR = MC, then read the price on the demand curve at that Q."},{"f":"Deadweight loss of monopoly","b":"Surplus lost because output is below the efficient level where P = MC."},{"f":"Natural monopoly","b":"An industry where one firm can supply the whole market at lower average cost than two or more firms (huge fixed costs)."},{"f":"Average-cost pricing","b":"Regulating a natural monopoly to charge P = ATC so it breaks even; P = MC would cause a loss."},{"f":"Monopolistic competition features","b":"Many sellers, differentiated products, easy entry, some pricing power."},{"f":"Monopolistic competition in the long run","b":"Zero economic profit, P = ATC but P > MC, and excess capacity."},{"f":"Oligopoly key feature","b":"Interdependence: a firm's best choice depends on the reactions of a few rivals."},{"f":"Kinked demand curve idea","b":"Rivals match price cuts but not price increases, so demand is elastic above and inelastic below the current price; prices are sticky."},{"f":"Cartel","b":"A group of firms that agree to restrict output and raise price; unstable because members gain by cheating."},{"f":"Concentration ratio CR4","b":"The combined market share of the four largest firms."},{"f":"HHI","b":"Herfindahl-Hirschman Index: sum of squared percentage shares. Monopoly = 10,000."},{"f":"Change in HHI from merging two firms","b":"2 x (share of firm A) x (share of firm B)."},{"f":"Three conditions for price discrimination","b":"Market power, ability to separate customers by willingness to pay, and no resale between groups."},{"f":"Sherman Act vs Clayton Act","b":"Sherman (1890): bans restraint of trade and monopolization. Clayton (1914): bans mergers and practices that may substantially lessen competition."}],"questions":[{"q":"Which description fits perfect competition?","o":["Many sellers of identical products with free entry and exit","One seller protected by high barriers to entry","A few large firms whose decisions directly affect each other","Many sellers, each with a heavily branded and unique product line"],"a":0,"e":"Perfect competition means many small sellers, identical products, and free entry and exit. A single seller is monopoly, a few interdependent firms is oligopoly, and differentiated products with many sellers is monopolistic competition.","d":1},{"q":"A wheat farmer in a perfectly competitive market faces a demand curve for her own output that is:","o":["downward sloping like the market demand curve","vertical at the market quantity","horizontal at the market price","upward sloping at higher prices"],"a":2,"e":"A price taker can sell any amount at the market price, so her demand curve is horizontal (perfectly elastic). Downward-sloping demand belongs to firms with pricing power, such as monopolies.","d":1},{"q":"For a monopoly, marginal revenue is:","o":["equal to price","less than price","greater than price","always equal to marginal cost"],"a":1,"e":"A monopoly must cut price on all units to sell one more, so MR < P. MR = P is true only for price takers. MR equals MC only at the optimal quantity, not always.","d":1},{"q":"In the long run, a perfectly competitive firm earns:","o":["large positive economic profit","negative economic profit","zero economic profit","profit equal to the monopoly level"],"a":2,"e":"Positive profit attracts entry and losses cause exit, so long-run economic profit is zero. It may seem that efficient firms keep profits, but entrants copy them and compete the profit away.","d":1},{"q":"Which is a barrier to entry that gives a firm legal protection from competitors?","o":["A large number of buyers","Identical products across sellers","A patent on its product","Low start-up costs"],"a":2,"e":"A patent gives temporary exclusive rights, which blocks entry. Many buyers, identical products, and low start-up costs all make entry easier, not harder.","d":1},{"q":"A neighborhood with dozens of coffee shops, each with its own style, menu, and loyal customers, is best described as:","o":["perfect competition with identical sellers","monopolistic competition","monopoly with one dominant seller","oligopoly with a few big rivals"],"a":1,"e":"Many sellers with differentiated products and easy entry is monopolistic competition. It is not perfect competition because products are not identical, and it is not oligopoly because there are many firms.","d":1},{"q":"The Sherman Act of 1890 mainly prohibits:","o":["any firm from becoming large","price fixing and attempts to monopolize","mergers between firms in different countries","all advertising by dominant firms"],"a":1,"e":"The Sherman Act bans restraint of trade (like price fixing) and monopolization. It does not punish size by itself, and it does not ban advertising or foreign mergers as such.","d":1},{"q":"A cartel is a group of firms that:","o":["agree to restrict output and raise prices","compete on price to win customers","merge into one company permanently","are regulated by a government commission"],"a":0,"e":"Cartels coordinate to act like a joint monopoly. Competing on price is the opposite. A merger is a single firm, and regulation of a natural monopoly is a different topic.","d":1},{"q":"A monopolist faces demand P = 100 - Q and constant marginal cost of $20. What price maximizes its profit?","o":["$60","$20","$40","$80"],"a":0,"e":"MR = 100 - 2Q = 20 gives Q = 40. Price is read from demand: 100 - 40 = $60. $20 is the competitive price (P = MC). $40 is the quantity, not the price. $80 is the competitive quantity.","d":2},{"q":"Using the same monopoly (P = 100 - Q, MC = $20), what is the deadweight loss compared with perfect competition?","o":["$1,600","$800","$400","$2,400"],"a":1,"e":"Monopoly Q = 40, competitive Q = 80, price gap = 60 - 20 = 40. DWL = 0.5 x 40 x 40 = $800. $1,600 is the monopoly profit rectangle, not the triangle. $400 is only half the correct triangle, and $2,400 has no basis.","d":2},{"q":"Four firms in a market have shares of 40%, 30%, 20%, and 10%. The HHI is:","o":["3,000","100","1,400","1,000"],"a":0,"e":"HHI = 1,600 + 900 + 400 + 100 = 3,000. 100 is just the sum of shares (or CR4). 1,400 drops the largest firm's squared share. 1,000 has no basis.","d":2},{"q":"In the long run, a firm in monopolistic competition ends up with:","o":["positive economic profit protected by its brand","price equal to marginal cost at minimum ATC","zero economic profit and price above marginal cost","a price below average total cost, so ongoing losses persist"],"a":2,"e":"Entry erodes profit until P = ATC, but differentiation keeps demand downward sloping so P > MC. Brand loyalty may help in the short run only. P = MC is perfect competition.","d":2},{"q":"An airline manager believes rivals will match any fare cut but ignore any fare increase. According to the kinked demand model, the airline will most likely:","o":["cut fares often to win market share","raise fares promptly whenever costs rise even slightly","leave the market and avoid rivals","keep fares stable despite small cost changes"],"a":3,"e":"If cuts are matched, cutting wins little; if increases are ignored, raising loses many customers. So both moves look unattractive and prices stay sticky.","d":2},{"q":"Which set of conditions must hold for a firm to successfully price discriminate?","o":["Many competitors, identical products, and free entry","Constant marginal cost, zero fixed costs, and high advertising","Market power, separable groups, and no resale between them","Government approval, patents, and price controls"],"a":2,"e":"A price taker cannot set different prices, groups must be separable by willingness to pay, and low-price buyers must not resell to high-price buyers. Competitive conditions make discrimination impossible.","d":2},{"q":"A movie theater charges $8 for students and $13 for adults for the same seat. This is an example of:","o":["a cartel agreement","price discrimination","predatory pricing","perfect competition"],"a":1,"e":"Same product, different prices for different groups with different price sensitivity, not explained by cost, is price discrimination. It is not collusion or predatory pricing.","d":2},{"q":"A regional water distribution company has falling average cost over the whole market. A regulator sets price equal to marginal cost. The most likely problem is:","o":["the firm will earn large excess profits from following the rule","new competitors will immediately enter and take over","output will fall well below the efficient level","the firm will lose money since MC is below average cost"],"a":3,"e":"For a natural monopoly, ATC is above MC, so P = MC does not cover total cost. That is why regulators use average-cost pricing or subsidies. The efficient output is actually achieved at P = MC.","d":2},{"q":"A perfectly competitive firm takes a price of $30 and has MC = 2q. How many units should it produce?","o":["30","15","60","7.5"],"a":1,"e":"Set P = MC: 30 = 2q, so q = 15. 30 mistakenly sets q equal to price. 60 doubles instead of dividing. 7.5 divides by four.","d":2},{"q":"In that same firm (price $30, output 15 units), average total cost at 15 units is $25. Economic profit is:","o":["$450","$75","$5","$375"],"a":1,"e":"Profit = (30 - 25) x 15 = $75. $450 is total revenue. $5 is per-unit profit, not total. $375 is total cost.","d":2},{"q":"Firms in a perfectly competitive industry are earning positive economic profit. What should the industry expect?","o":["Exit, a leftward supply shift, and a rising price as firms leave","Higher prices as firms pass their higher costs on to buyers","No change, since profits are simply a lasting reward for efficiency","Entry, rising supply, and a falling price until profit is zero"],"a":3,"e":"Free entry means profit attracts new firms. Supply rises, price falls, and profit disappears. Exit happens with losses, not profits.","d":2},{"q":"The Clayton Act is most directly aimed at:","o":["conspiracies among competitors to fix prices","monopolies that arise from patents","mergers and practices that reduce competition","taxing firms with high profits"],"a":2,"e":"The Clayton Act addresses mergers, tying, exclusive dealing, and interlocking directorates. Price fixing is a Sherman Act issue. Patents are legal monopolies, not violations.","d":2},{"q":"A 20% market-share firm merges with a 10% market-share firm in an industry where no other shares change. The HHI rises by:","o":["400","500","30","300"],"a":0,"e":"The change is 2 x 20 x 10 = 400. Check: (30 squared = 900) minus (400 + 100) = 400. 500 is the sum of the old squares. 30 is the sum of shares. 300 comes from multiplying 30 by 10.","d":2},{"q":"A profit-maximizing monopolist with positive marginal cost will always produce:","o":["on the elastic portion of its demand curve","on the inelastic portion of its demand curve","where demand has unit elasticity exactly","where price equals marginal cost"],"a":0,"e":"On the inelastic part of demand, MR is negative, but MC is positive, so MR = MC is impossible. The firm raises price until demand is elastic. P = MC is the competitive outcome.","d":3},{"q":"Compared with the efficient benchmark, a long-run monopolistic competitor is best described as:","o":["charging above marginal cost and producing below minimum ATC","charging marginal cost and producing at the minimum point of ATC","earning positive profit and producing too much output","earning losses and producing exactly at the minimum ATC output"],"a":0,"e":"Long-run zero profit comes from P = ATC, but since demand slopes down the tangency is left of minimum ATC, which means excess capacity and P > MC. P = MC and minimum ATC describe perfect competition.","d":3},{"q":"In the short run, a competitive firm faces a price of $12 with AVC of $10 and ATC of $14 at its best output. What should it do?","o":["Shut down immediately because it is losing money","Raise its price to $14 to break even","Keep producing in both the short and long run","Produce now, but plan to exit in the long run"],"a":3,"e":"P > AVC, so each unit contributes $2 toward fixed costs that are owed regardless; shutting down would lose more. But P < ATC, so in the long run it must exit. A price taker cannot simply raise its price.","d":3},{"q":"Segment A has demand P = 100 - Q and segment B has P = 60 - Q, with MC = $10. A single uniform price yields profit of $2,450 (P = $45, Q = 70). Charging the best separate price in each segment adds how much profit?","o":["$0","$100","$200","$2,650"],"a":2,"e":"Separate pricing: A at $55 with Q = 45 gives $2,025; B at $35 with Q = 25 gives $625; total $2,650. Gain = 2,650 - 2,450 = $200. $2,650 is total profit, not the gain.","d":3},{"q":"Why do cartels such as an agreement among oil producers tend to break down?","o":["Members always prefer competing on price with one another instead","Each member gains by secretly selling extra at the high price","Cartel output is always higher than competitive output","Consumers refuse to buy anything from cartels at all"],"a":1,"e":"At the cartel price, P > MC, so each member gains by expanding output. If all cheat, price falls. Cartel output is lower than competitive output by design.","d":3},{"q":"A market has four equal firms of 25% each. Two of them merge. What is the HHI after the merger?","o":["2,500","1,250","5,000","3,750"],"a":3,"e":"Before: 4 x 625 = 2,500. After: 50, 25, 25 gives 2,500 + 625 + 625 = 3,750 (a 1,250 increase). 2,500 is the old HHI. 1,250 is the change. 5,000 is not a result of any correct step.","d":3},{"q":"Which action is MOST clearly a violation of the Sherman Act?","o":["A firm becomes dominant because customers prefer its better product","A firm patents its own invention and sells it","A regulated utility serves as the only provider in a town","Two rival executives agree by phone to raise prices on the same day"],"a":3,"e":"Price fixing among competitors is the classic Section 1 violation. Winning on merit, patenting, and regulated natural monopolies are legal.","d":3},{"q":"Why do regulators of a natural monopoly often choose average-cost pricing instead of marginal-cost pricing?","o":["Pricing at marginal cost would leave the firm with a loss","Average-cost pricing creates the efficient output level","Marginal cost is impossible to measure in any industry","Average-cost pricing maximizes consumer surplus"],"a":0,"e":"With falling ATC, MC is below ATC, so P = MC gives negative profit. Average-cost pricing lets the firm break even, at the cost of some deadweight loss. It does not give the efficient output.","d":3},{"q":"A trendy salad chain in a monopolistically competitive city earns above-normal profit this year. Over time, which is the most likely result?","o":["Its demand turns less elastic as more rivals enter the market, raising profit","It sets price equal to marginal cost in order to deter new entry","Its profit keeps rising further as the whole market expands","Rivals enter, its demand shifts left and flattens, profit falls to zero"],"a":3,"e":"Easy entry brings competing brands that take customers away and offer closer substitutes, so demand is lower and more elastic. Profit is competed away. More substitutes cannot make demand less elastic.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Price taker","Firm with no control over price"],["Barrier to entry","Obstacle that keeps new firms out"],["Natural monopoly","One firm serves market at lowest cost"],["HHI","Sum of squared market shares"],["Cartel","Firms colluding to restrict output"],["Price discrimination","Different buyers pay different prices"],["Deadweight loss","Surplus lost from underproduced output"],["Clayton Act","Targets mergers that lessen competition"]],"traps":["Trap: A monopolist simply charges the highest price it can. Truth: it picks the quantity where MR = MC, then reads the price from demand; a higher price would cut profit.","Trap: Zero economic profit means the firm earns nothing and will close. Truth: it still earns a normal return on owners' capital, and it stays in business.","Trap: Monopolistic competition is the same as monopoly. Truth: it has many firms, easy entry, differentiated products, and zero long-run economic profit.","Trap: For a monopoly, price equals marginal revenue. Truth: that is only for price takers; for a monopoly MR is below P.","Trap: Antitrust law makes big market share illegal. Truth: it targets harmful conduct and mergers that lessen competition, not size earned by better products."],"mnemonic":"Competition: P = MR = MC. Monopoly: MR = MC, then look UP to demand for the price. 'Match, then look up.'","cram":["Perfect competition: price taker, price equals MR equals MC, zero long-run profit.","Monopoly: marginal revenue below price; set MR equal MC, read price from demand.","Natural monopoly: falling average cost; regulators use average-cost pricing.","Monopolistic competition: differentiated products, zero long-run profit, excess capacity.","Oligopoly: interdependence; kinked demand gives sticky prices; cartels are unstable because of cheating.","HHI is the sum of squared shares; merger adds twice the share product.","Price discrimination needs market power, separable groups, no resale; charge inelastic group more.","Sherman Act: price fixing, monopolization. Clayton Act: harmful mergers, tying, interlocking directorates."],"ytv":[{"title":"Monopolies & Types of Monopolies: Microeconomics","channel":"Mr. Sinn","id":"zmwKZk4I5Zs","why":"Clear walk-through of what makes a monopoly, barriers to entry, and natural monopolies."},{"title":"Game Theory and Oligopoly: Crash Course Economics #26","channel":"CrashCourse","id":"PCcVODWm-oY","why":"Explains oligopoly interdependence, cartels, and why collusion is unstable, a bridge into the next unit."}]},{"id":"u6","title":"Game Theory & Market Failures","day":3,"summary":"Learn how rivals make strategic choices (payoff matrices, dominant strategies, Nash equilibrium, the prisoner's dilemma), and why markets fail with externalities, public goods, common resources and asymmetric information, plus the policy fixes and their limits.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Strategic interaction and payoff matrices","body":"<p><strong>Game theory</strong> studies decisions where your best choice depends on what someone else chooses. That is exactly an oligopoly: if you cut your price, your rival's reaction determines whether you gain or lose. A game has <strong>players</strong>, <strong>strategies</strong> (the choices each can make), and <strong>payoffs</strong> (profit or utility from each combination of choices).</p><p>The simplest tool is the <strong>payoff matrix</strong>. Each cell shows the payoffs as (Firm A, Firm B). Example: two competing retailers decide whether to run a big ad campaign. Payoffs are profits in $ millions.</p><ul><li>Neither advertises: (10, 10)</li><li>A advertises, B does not: (14, 4)</li><li>A does not, B advertises: (4, 14)</li><li>Both advertise: (7, 7)</li></ul><p>A <strong>dominant strategy</strong> is the best choice for a player <em>no matter what the other player does</em>. Check A: if B does not advertise, A earns 14 by advertising versus 10 by not. If B advertises, A earns 7 versus 4. So advertising is dominant for A. By symmetry it is dominant for B too.</p><p>A <strong>Nash equilibrium</strong> is an outcome where each player is making their best choice <em>given the other's choice</em>, so nobody wants to change unilaterally. Here (Advertise, Advertise) with payoffs (7, 7) is the Nash equilibrium. A dominant-strategy outcome is always a Nash equilibrium, but a game can have a Nash equilibrium without any dominant strategies, and some games have more than one.</p><p><strong>How to find a Nash equilibrium:</strong> for each cell, ask whether either player would gain by switching while the other stays put. If neither would, it is a Nash equilibrium.</p>"},{"h":"2. The prisoner's dilemma and cartel instability","body":"<p>Notice the advertising game: both firms would earn more at (10, 10) than at (7, 7), yet each firm's self-interest pushes both to (7, 7). That is a <strong>prisoner's dilemma</strong>: individually rational choices lead to a collectively worse result. The name comes from two suspects who each do better by confessing no matter what the other does, but both end up worse off than if both stayed silent.</p><p><strong>Cartels are prisoner's dilemmas.</strong> Suppose two oil producers agree to a production quota. Payoffs: both comply (10, 10); A cheats while B complies (15, 5); A complies while B cheats (5, 15); both cheat (6, 6). Cheating is dominant for each, so the Nash equilibrium is both cheat at (6, 6), not the agreed (10, 10). That is why cartels tend to collapse, and why they need monitoring and punishment.</p><p>Other prisoner's dilemmas in business: price wars, advertising arms races, and countries cutting tariffs or not. The lesson for managers: if you see a dilemma, look for ways to change the game (contracts, reputation, repeated interaction, laws).</p><p><strong>Nash equilibrium without dominant strategies:</strong> in a coordination game, two firms choose between technology standards. If both pick Standard X they earn (4, 4), if both pick Y they earn (2, 2), and if they mismatch they earn (0, 0). Both (X, X) and (Y, Y) are Nash equilibria. Theory alone does not say which one will occur, so a firm may need to signal or lead.</p>"},{"h":"3. Repeated games, credibility, and first-mover advantage","body":"<p>If firms play the prisoner's dilemma <em>once</em>, defection wins. If they play it <strong>repeatedly</strong> with no known end, cooperation can last because cheating today invites punishment tomorrow. A famous strategy is <strong>tit-for-tat</strong>: cooperate first, then copy whatever the rival did last round. It is nice, firm, and forgiving. Cooperation is sustained when players value future profits enough. If the game has a <em>known last round</em>, backward reasoning says both defect in the last round, and then the round before, and so on, so cooperation unravels in theory.</p><p><strong>Sequential games and credibility.</strong> When players move one after another, work backward from the end (<strong>backward induction</strong>). Example: an incumbent says it will fight any entrant with a price war. Payoffs (entrant, incumbent): stay out (0, 8); enter and incumbent fights (-2, 2); enter and incumbent accommodates (3, 4). Once the entrant has entered, the incumbent earns 4 by accommodating but only 2 by fighting, so the threat is <strong>not credible</strong>. The entrant enters and the incumbent accommodates: (3, 4). Threats only matter if they are believable.</p><p><strong>First-mover advantage</strong> arises when moving first lets a firm make a <strong>commitment</strong> that changes rivals' best responses. Examples: building a huge factory (a credible signal of low prices), locking customers into contracts, or grabbing a scarce location. The first mover is not always better off; in some games a second mover learns from the first.</p>"},{"h":"4. Market failure and externalities","body":"<p>Markets usually allocate resources efficiently, but a <strong>market failure</strong> occurs when the outcome is not efficient. The main causes are <strong>externalities</strong>, <strong>public goods</strong>, <strong>common resources</strong>, <strong>asymmetric information</strong>, and market power.</p><p>An <strong>externality</strong> is a cost or benefit that falls on people who are not part of the transaction. Private decisions ignore it, so the market quantity is wrong.</p><ul><li><strong>Negative externality</strong> (pollution, noise, traffic): social cost > private cost, so the market <em>overproduces</em>.</li><li><strong>Positive externality</strong> (vaccines, education, R&D): social benefit > private benefit, so the market <em>underproduces</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Numbers (negative externality).</strong> Private demand is P = 100 - Q, and private supply (marginal private cost) is P = 20 + Q. Each unit also imposes $20 of pollution damage on neighbors, so marginal social cost is P = 40 + Q. The unregulated market sets 100 - Q = 20 + Q, so Q = 40 and P = $60. The social optimum sets 100 - Q = 40 + Q, so Q = 30 and P = $70. At Q = 40, marginal social cost is $80 but the value to the marginal buyer is $60, so the market overproduces. The deadweight loss is 0.5 x (40 - 30) x ($80 - $60) = <strong>$100</strong>.</p><p><strong>Positive externality numbers.</strong> Private marginal benefit is P = 60 - Q, each unit gives outsiders $10 of benefit (so marginal social benefit is P = 70 - Q), and marginal cost is P = 20 + Q. The market produces Q = 20, but the social optimum is Q = 25. Deadweight loss = 0.5 x (25 - 20) x $10 = <strong>$25</strong>.</p>"},{"h":"5. Fixing externalities: taxes, subsidies, cap and trade, and Coase","body":"<p><strong>Pigouvian tax:</strong> a tax equal to the marginal external cost makes producers pay the true social cost. In the example above, a $20 per unit tax moves supply up to P = 40 + Q, so the market produces Q = 30. Buyers now pay $70, and sellers keep $70 - $20 = $50 per unit. Tax revenue is $20 x 30 = $600. The tax is efficient because it uses prices, and firms with cheap ways to cut pollution cut the most.</p><p><strong>Pigouvian subsidy:</strong> for positive externalities, pay a subsidy equal to the marginal external benefit ($10 in the example) so output rises from 20 to 25.</p><p><strong>Cap and trade:</strong> the government sets a total <em>quantity</em> of pollution (the cap), issues tradable permits, and lets firms buy and sell them. Example: A and B each emit 60 tons, and the cap is 100 tons total (50 permits each). Cutting costs $30 per ton for A and $50 per ton for B. If each must cut 10 tons, cost = 10 x $30 + 10 x $50 = $800. With trading, A cuts 20 tons (cost $600) and sells its 10 spare permits to B at a price between $30 and $50 (say $40). Total abatement cost falls to <strong>$600</strong>, saving $200, and the pollution target is still met. Taxes fix the <em>price</em> of pollution; cap and trade fixes the <em>quantity</em>.</p><p><strong>Coase theorem:</strong> if property rights are clearly defined and <strong>transaction costs are low</strong>, private parties can bargain to an efficient outcome regardless of who initially holds the rights. Example: a factory's smoke causes $500,000 in damage to a town, and a filter costs $200,000. The filter gets installed either way. If the town holds the right, the factory buys the filter rather than pay up to $500,000. If the factory holds the right, the town pays the factory somewhere between $200,000 and $500,000 to install it. Who gets the money changes; efficiency does not. The theorem fails with many parties, high bargaining costs, or unclear rights.</p><p>Other tools: regulations and standards (command and control), liability laws, and quotas.</p>"},{"h":"6. Public goods and common resources","body":"<p>Goods can be sorted by two questions: <strong>Is it rival?</strong> (does one person's use reduce another's?) and <strong>Is it excludable?</strong> (can non-payers be kept out?)</p><ul><li><strong>Private goods:</strong> rival and excludable (a sandwich, a laptop).</li><li><strong>Public goods:</strong> nonrival and nonexcludable (national defense, flood control, clean air, streetlights).</li><li><strong>Common resources:</strong> rival but nonexcludable (ocean fish, public pastures, a free but crowded road).</li><li><strong>Club goods:</strong> nonrival but excludable (streaming subscriptions, a gated park, uncongested toll road).</li></ul><p><strong>Free-rider problem:</strong> since non-payers cannot be excluded from a public good, everyone is tempted to let others pay. A private firm cannot collect enough revenue, so the market underprovides it even when the benefits exceed the cost. Example: a bridge costs $900,000. 1,000 residents each value it at $1,000 (total benefit $1,000,000). Net benefit is $100,000, so building is efficient, but a private seller cannot get each resident to pay. The government can provide it and fund it with taxes. Cost-benefit analysis compares total benefits with total costs.</p><p><strong>Tragedy of the commons:</strong> a common resource is overused because each user ignores the cost their use imposes on others (a negative externality). Solutions: assign private property rights, tradable quotas, permits or fees, regulation, or community rules.</p>"},{"h":"7. Asymmetric information and government failure","body":"<p><strong>Asymmetric information</strong> means one side of a deal knows more than the other. It creates two classic problems:</p><ul><li><strong>Adverse selection</strong> (hidden characteristics <em>before</em> the deal). The seller of a used car knows if it is a lemon; buyers do not, so they offer an average price, which drives good-car owners out and leaves mostly lemons. Insurance works the same way: high-risk people are most eager to buy, so premiums rise, low-risk people leave, and premiums rise again (a death spiral). Fixes: warranties, inspections, signaling (credentials, brand), screening (medical exams), mandatory pooling.</li><li><strong>Moral hazard</strong> (hidden action <em>after</em> the deal). A person with full insurance may take less care; a manager with a bonus plan may take hidden risks. Fixes: deductibles and copays, monitoring, performance-based pay, incentive contracts.</li></ul><p><strong>Government failure.</strong> Government can fix market failures, but it can also make things worse. Common reasons: <em>rent-seeking</em> (firms lobby for favors), <strong>regulatory capture</strong> (the regulator ends up serving the industry it regulates), poor information, short election-cycle thinking, and unintended side effects. A good manager asks: is the cure better than the disease?</p><p><strong>Quick decision tree:</strong> Is a cost or benefit hitting outsiders? That is an externality. Can non-payers use it and does use not reduce others' use? Public good. Free for all but depleted by use? Common resource. One side hiding information before the deal? Adverse selection. After the deal? Moral hazard.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Game theory studies decisions where your best move depends on what your rivals do. A payoff matrix lists the profit each player gets from every combination of choices.","A dominant strategy is the best choice for a player no matter what the other player does.","A Nash equilibrium is an outcome where every player is making their best choice given what the others are doing, so nobody wants to switch on their own.","In the prisoner's dilemma, each player has a dominant strategy to defect, so both end up worse off than if they had cooperated.","Cartels are prisoner's dilemmas. Each member earns more by cheating on the quota, so cartels tend to be unstable without monitoring and punishment.","In repeated games, strategies like tit for tat can sustain cooperation, because cheating today is punished tomorrow. A threat only works if it is credible, and first movers gain when they can make a credible commitment.","An externality is a cost or benefit that falls on people outside the transaction. Negative externalities cause overproduction, and positive externalities cause underproduction.","A Pigouvian tax equal to the marginal external cost fixes a negative externality, and a subsidy equal to the marginal external benefit fixes a positive one. Cap and trade fixes the quantity of pollution and lets permits move to the firms with the lowest abatement cost.","The Coase theorem says that with clear property rights and low transaction costs, private bargaining reaches an efficient outcome no matter who holds the rights.","Public goods are nonrival and nonexcludable, which creates the free rider problem and underprovision. Common resources are rival but nonexcludable, which leads to overuse, the tragedy of the commons.","Adverse selection is hidden information before a deal, like the used car lemons problem. Moral hazard is hidden action after a deal, like less careful behavior once insured.","Government failure happens when policy makes things worse, through rent seeking, regulatory capture, or unintended consequences. Always compare the cure with the disease."],"examples":[{"q":"Two competing retailers each choose Advertise or Don't. Payoffs ($ millions, Firm A first): neither advertises (10, 10); A advertises only (14, 4); B advertises only (4, 14); both advertise (7, 7). Find any dominant strategies and the Nash equilibrium. Why can't they reach (10, 10)?","steps":["Firm A: if B doesn't advertise, A gets 14 (advertise) vs 10 (don't). If B advertises, A gets 7 vs 4. Advertising is better in both cases, so it is dominant.","By symmetry, advertising is dominant for Firm B.","Both play their dominant strategy, so the Nash equilibrium is (Advertise, Advertise) with payoffs (7, 7).","Check (10, 10): either firm could switch to advertising and jump from 10 to 14, so it is not stable.","This is a prisoner's dilemma: the equilibrium (7, 7) is worse for both than (10, 10)."],"answer":"Advertise is dominant for both; Nash equilibrium is (Advertise, Advertise) at (7, 7); (10, 10) is unstable because each firm gains by deviating."},{"q":"A chemical plant sells at private demand P = 100 - Q with private marginal cost P = 20 + Q. Each unit causes $20 of pollution damage to neighbors. Find the market output, the socially optimal output, the Pigouvian tax, the deadweight loss of the unregulated market, and the prices after the tax.","steps":["Market: 100 - Q = 20 + Q gives Q = 40 and P = 60.","Marginal social cost = 20 + Q + 20 = 40 + Q.","Social optimum: 100 - Q = 40 + Q gives Q = 30 and P = 70.","Pigouvian tax = marginal external cost = $20 per unit.","DWL: at Q = 40, MSC = 80 and the marginal buyer's value = 60. DWL = 0.5 x (40 - 30) x (80 - 60) = 100.","After the tax buyers pay $70; sellers keep 70 - 20 = $50; tax revenue = 20 x 30 = $600."],"answer":"Market Q = 40, optimum Q = 30, tax = $20, DWL = $100; buyers pay $70 and sellers keep $50."},{"q":"Firms A and B each emit 60 tons of CO2. The cap is 100 tons total (50 permits each). Abatement costs $30 per ton for A and $50 per ton for B. Compare the total abatement cost of forcing each firm to cut 10 tons versus allowing permit trading.","steps":["Required total cut = 120 - 100 = 20 tons.","Equal cuts: A cuts 10 x $30 = $300; B cuts 10 x $50 = $500; total = $800.","With trading, the cheapest way to cut 20 tons is for A (cost $30 per ton) to do all of it: 20 x $30 = $600.","A cuts 20 tons, so it emits 40 and needs only 40 of its 50 permits; it sells its 10 spare permits to B at some price between $30 and $50.","B emits 60 tons, so it needs 60 permits and buys 10 for less than the $50 per ton it would pay to abate.","Savings = $800 - $600 = $200, with the same total pollution."],"answer":"Equal cuts cost $800; trading costs $600, saving $200 while meeting the same 100-ton cap."}],"videos":[{"title":"Prisoner's dilemma and Nash equilibrium explained with a payoff matrix","query":"prisoner's dilemma Nash equilibrium payoff matrix economics explained"},{"title":"Externalities, Pigouvian tax and deadweight loss graph","query":"negative externality Pigouvian tax graph deadweight loss explained"},{"title":"Public goods, free rider problem, tragedy of the commons","query":"public goods vs common resources free rider tragedy of the commons"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Dominant strategy","b":"The best choice for a player regardless of what the other players do."},{"f":"Nash equilibrium","b":"An outcome where each player's strategy is a best response to the others', so no one gains by switching alone."},{"f":"Prisoner's dilemma","b":"A game where individual self-interest (dominant strategy to defect) leaves everyone worse off than cooperation would."},{"f":"Why are cartels unstable?","b":"Each member can earn more by cheating on its quota while others comply, so cheating is a dominant strategy."},{"f":"Tit-for-tat","b":"Cooperate in round one, then copy the rival's previous move; sustains cooperation in repeated games."},{"f":"Backward induction","b":"Solve a sequential game by working from the last move back to the first."},{"f":"Credible threat","b":"A threat that the player would actually carry out because it is in their interest once the moment comes."},{"f":"First-mover advantage","b":"A benefit from moving first, usually by making a credible commitment that changes rivals' best responses."},{"f":"Market failure","b":"A situation where the market outcome is not efficient."},{"f":"Externality","b":"A cost or benefit of a transaction that falls on someone outside it."},{"f":"Negative externality effect","b":"Market overproduces (social cost > private cost). Fix with a Pigouvian tax."},{"f":"Positive externality effect","b":"Market underproduces (social benefit > private benefit). Fix with a subsidy."},{"f":"Pigouvian tax","b":"A tax equal to the marginal external cost, forcing producers to pay the full social cost."},{"f":"Cap and trade","b":"Government sets a total pollution cap and issues tradable permits; pollution is cut where it is cheapest."},{"f":"Coase theorem","b":"With clear property rights and low transaction costs, bargaining yields an efficient outcome regardless of initial rights."},{"f":"Public good","b":"Nonrival and nonexcludable (national defense). Suffers from free riders and is underprovided."},{"f":"Common resource","b":"Rival but nonexcludable (ocean fish). Overused: tragedy of the commons."},{"f":"Club good","b":"Nonrival but excludable (a subscription service or gated park)."},{"f":"Free-rider problem","b":"People benefit from a good without paying because they cannot be excluded."},{"f":"Adverse selection","b":"Hidden information before a deal (used-car lemons, insurance risk); bad types crowd out good ones."},{"f":"Moral hazard","b":"Hidden action after a deal: protected people take more risk (insured driver less careful)."},{"f":"Regulatory capture","b":"The regulator ends up serving the interests of the industry it is supposed to regulate."}],"questions":[{"q":"A firm has a dominant strategy when that strategy:","o":["is best regardless of what rivals choose","is best only if rivals cooperate","gives the highest total payoff to both players","is chosen by the rival first"],"a":0,"e":"A dominant strategy beats every alternative against every rival choice. It may not maximize joint payoffs (that is the prisoner's dilemma), and it does not depend on cooperation or move order.","d":1},{"q":"A Nash equilibrium occurs when:","o":["every player earns the highest possible joint payoff in the game","one player has a dominant strategy and the others follow","players agree to cooperate in a written contract","each player is doing the best it can given the others' choices"],"a":3,"e":"Nash equilibrium is mutual best response. It need not be jointly best, and it does not need an agreement or even a dominant strategy.","d":1},{"q":"A chemical plant dumps waste into a river, harming a fishing town downstream. This is an example of a:","o":["positive externality","negative externality","public good","moral hazard"],"a":1,"e":"A cost imposed on a third party outside the transaction is a negative externality. A positive externality is a benefit to outsiders. Moral hazard is a hidden-action problem, not a spillover.","d":1},{"q":"A neighbor's flu shot reduces your chance of catching the flu. This is:","o":["a negative externality","adverse selection","a common resource problem","a positive externality"],"a":3,"e":"The benefit spills over to people not in the transaction, so it is a positive externality. Markets tend to underproduce vaccines for this reason.","d":1},{"q":"Which pair of features defines a pure public good?","o":["rival and excludable","nonrival and nonexcludable","rival and nonexcludable","nonrival and excludable"],"a":1,"e":"Public goods are nonrival (my use does not reduce yours) and nonexcludable (non-payers cannot be kept out). Rival and nonexcludable is a common resource; nonrival and excludable is a club good.","d":1},{"q":"The free-rider problem means that people:","o":["pay more than the good is worth","overproduce goods that pollute","enjoy a good without paying for it","hide information from sellers"],"a":2,"e":"Because non-payers cannot be excluded, individuals can enjoy the good for free, so private markets underprovide it.","d":1},{"q":"A used-car buyer cannot tell good cars from lemons before buying. This hidden information problem is called:","o":["moral hazard","adverse selection","the tragedy of the commons","a Pigouvian externality"],"a":1,"e":"Adverse selection is hidden characteristics before the transaction. Moral hazard is hidden behavior after a deal.","d":1},{"q":"A driver with full insurance coverage becomes less careful about parking in safe areas. This is:","o":["adverse selection","free riding","moral hazard","a positive externality"],"a":2,"e":"Moral hazard is a change in behavior after being protected from risk. Adverse selection concerns who chooses to buy coverage in the first place.","d":1},{"q":"Two oil producers each choose Comply or Cheat on a quota. Payoffs (A, B): both comply (10, 10); A cheats, B complies (15, 5); A complies, B cheats (5, 15); both cheat (6, 6). The Nash equilibrium is:","o":["both comply, with payoffs (10, 10)","both cheat, with payoffs (6, 6)","A cheats and B complies, with payoffs (15, 5)","A complies and B cheats, with payoffs (5, 15)"],"a":1,"e":"Cheating is better for each producer whatever the other does (15 > 10 and 6 > 5). So both cheat. (10, 10) is jointly best but unstable because each gains by deviating. The mixed outcomes are not equilibria since the complying party would switch.","d":2},{"q":"Why does a first mover sometimes gain an advantage in a market, such as by building a large factory before rivals?","o":["Rivals are legally barred from ever entering after it","The first mover always has the lowest costs of production","Moving first guarantees higher profit in every game played","It can commit credibly, changing rivals' best responses"],"a":3,"e":"A visible commitment (like sunk capacity) signals that the first mover will produce a lot, so rivals rationally scale back. There is no guarantee in every game, and no legal ban is implied.","d":2},{"q":"A factory emits pollution that imposes a $20 per unit external cost. The efficient Pigouvian tax is:","o":["$0, because the market is efficient","$40 per unit, to fully deter output","$20 per unit","Half the market price"],"a":2,"e":"The tax should equal the marginal external cost so producers internalize it. Setting it higher over-corrects; zero ignores the externality.","d":2},{"q":"In the pollution example (private demand P = 100 - Q, private cost P = 20 + Q, external cost $20), the unregulated market produces 40 units. After the efficient $20 tax, output is:","o":["20 units","40 units","60 units","30 units"],"a":3,"e":"With the tax, effective supply is P = 40 + Q. Setting 100 - Q = 40 + Q gives Q = 30. 40 is the pre-tax output. 20 and 60 do not satisfy either equation.","d":2},{"q":"Vaccination creates benefits for people other than the person vaccinated. To reach the efficient level of vaccinations, government should:","o":["subsidize vaccination by the amount of the external benefit","tax vaccination by the amount of the external benefit","ban private vaccination clinics","do nothing, since the market already provides the efficient amount"],"a":0,"e":"A positive externality means underproduction, so a subsidy equal to the marginal external benefit raises output. A tax would reduce it even further.","d":2},{"q":"Firm A can cut emissions at $30 per ton and Firm B at $50 per ton. Compared with a rule that each must cut 10 tons, a tradable permit system that has A cut 20 tons and B cut none lowers total abatement cost by:","o":["$100","$600","$800","$200"],"a":3,"e":"Equal cuts cost 10 x 30 + 10 x 50 = $800. Trading costs 20 x 30 = $600. The saving is $200. $600 and $800 are the total costs, not the savings.","d":2},{"q":"The Coase theorem says private bargaining can solve an externality when:","o":["property rights are clear and transaction costs are low","the government sets a tax equal to the damage","there are many affected parties who each negotiate separately","the polluter holds the legal right to pollute"],"a":0,"e":"Coase requires clear rights and low bargaining costs. It does not matter who holds the rights for efficiency. Many parties raise transaction costs and weaken the result.","d":2},{"q":"A factory's smoke causes $500,000 of damage to a town, while a filter costs $200,000. Under the Coase theorem, the efficient outcome is that:","o":["the filter is installed only if the town holds the right","the filter is installed, whichever side holds the legal right","the factory keeps polluting because the filter costs too much","the town accepts the damage and the filter is not installed"],"a":1,"e":"Installing the filter costs less than the damage, so it is efficient in either case; only who pays changes. Skipping it would waste $300,000 of net value.","d":2},{"q":"In a repeated prisoner's dilemma with no known final round, a tit-for-tat strategy works by:","o":["always defecting to protect against cheating","cooperating forever regardless of how the rival has behaved before","cooperating first and then copying the rival's previous move","choosing randomly each round"],"a":2,"e":"Tit-for-tat starts nice and then mirrors, rewarding cooperation and punishing defection. Always defecting is a different strategy, and unconditional cooperation can be exploited.","d":2},{"q":"Overfishing of a public ocean fishery occurs because fish are:","o":["nonrival and nonexcludable, so no one is ever asked to pay for it","nonrival but excludable, so access is rationed by price","privately owned, so the owners overuse them for profit","rival but nonexcludable, so each boat ignores harm to others"],"a":3,"e":"A rival, nonexcludable good is a common resource. One boat's catch reduces what remains for others, but no one can be kept out, so the resource is overused.","d":2},{"q":"Which good is best classified as a club good?","o":["National defense","A streaming-video subscription","Ocean fish in international waters","A sandwich at a deli"],"a":1,"e":"Streaming is nonrival (one more viewer costs almost nothing) but excludable by a paywall. Defense is public, ocean fish is a common resource, and a sandwich is private.","d":2},{"q":"A buyer cannot distinguish high-quality from low-quality used cars, so offers an average price. Over time, mostly low-quality cars are offered. Which action best reduces this problem?","o":["Offering a warranty or inspection report","Raising the price on every car in the lot","Banning the resale of used cars altogether","Reducing the number of buyers in the market"],"a":0,"e":"Warranties and inspections signal quality and reduce hidden information. The other actions do not address what buyers do not know.","d":2},{"q":"A health insurer finds that after offering full coverage with no copay, claims rise because patients use more services than necessary. Which tool best reduces this?","o":["Copayments and deductibles","Lowering premiums","Eliminating coverage for low-risk people","Providing coverage to anyone who applies"],"a":0,"e":"This is moral hazard (behavior after coverage). Cost-sharing restores some incentive for care. Adverse selection is a different, pre-deal problem.","d":2},{"q":"An entrant decides first whether to enter a market. Payoffs (entrant, incumbent): stay out (0, 8); enter and incumbent fights (-2, 2); enter and incumbent accommodates (3, 4). The incumbent threatens to fight. What does backward induction predict?","o":["The entrant enters and the incumbent accommodates","The entrant stays out because the threat is credible","The entrant enters and the incumbent fights","The entrant stays out because 0 beats 3"],"a":0,"e":"If entry occurs, the incumbent earns 4 by accommodating versus 2 by fighting, so it will accommodate; the threat is not credible. Knowing that, the entrant gets 3 by entering versus 0 by staying out.","d":3},{"q":"Two firms choose between Standard X and Standard Y. Payoffs (A, B): (X, X) = (4, 4); (Y, Y) = (2, 2); mismatches = (0, 0). How many pure-strategy Nash equilibria are there?","o":["0","2","1","4"],"a":1,"e":"At (X, X) and at (Y, Y), neither firm gains by switching alone. At mismatches, each firm would switch to match. So there are two equilibria, and neither is dominant for both players.","d":3},{"q":"Which statement about repeated prisoner's dilemma games is most accurate?","o":["With no known end, cooperation can persist if the future matters enough","Cooperation always fails because defection is dominant in the one-shot game","Cooperation is guaranteed whenever the game repeats at least once","Repetition has no effect on the incentive to cheat"],"a":0,"e":"Repetition lets players punish cheaters in later rounds, which can make cooperation worthwhile if the future matters enough. With a known last round, backward reasoning suggests unraveling, but with an unknown end it can hold.","d":3},{"q":"Private demand is P = 100 - Q, private marginal cost is P = 20 + Q, and each unit causes $20 of external damage. The deadweight loss of the unregulated market is:","o":["$100","$200","$50","$800"],"a":0,"e":"Market Q = 40, optimum Q = 30. At Q = 40 marginal social cost = 80 but marginal benefit = 60, a gap of 20. DWL = 0.5 x 10 x 20 = $100. $200 forgets the one-half. $50 uses a wrong triangle base.","d":3},{"q":"With the efficient $20 tax in that market, buyers pay $70 per unit. What net price per unit do sellers keep, and what revenue does the tax raise?","o":["$70 per unit and $600","$50 per unit and $1,400","$90 per unit and $600","$50 per unit and $600"],"a":3,"e":"Sellers receive the buyer's price minus the tax: 70 - 20 = $50. Revenue = $20 x 30 units = $600. Using 70 for sellers ignores the tax, and $1,400 multiplies by the wrong quantity.","d":3},{"q":"A vaccine's private marginal benefit is P = 60 - Q, each dose gives outsiders $10 of benefit, and marginal cost is P = 20 + Q. The market produces 20 doses. What is the deadweight loss from underproduction?","o":["$10","$50","$25","$100"],"a":2,"e":"Social optimum: 70 - Q = 20 + Q, so Q = 25. At Q = 20, social benefit 50 exceeds cost 40 by 10. DWL = 0.5 x (25 - 20) x 10 = $25. $50 forgets the one-half, and $10 is just the per-unit gap.","d":3},{"q":"A bridge costs $900,000. Each of 1,000 residents values it at $1,000, and no one can be excluded once it is built. A private firm cannot profitably build it. What is the best conclusion?","o":["Do not build it, because the private market has clearly shown that it is unprofitable","Build it only if each resident values it at more than $1,500 apiece for the bridge","Build it: $1,000,000 of benefits exceed the cost, but free riders block private supply","Build it, but only a private monopoly could ever supply it efficiently for everyone"],"a":2,"e":"Total benefit $1,000,000 minus cost $900,000 gives a net gain of $100,000. Free riding prevents a private seller from collecting that value, a classic public-goods failure.","d":3},{"q":"A regulator charged with protecting consumers increasingly adopts rules favored by the industry it regulates, which hires former regulators and funds its campaigns. This is an example of:","o":["adverse selection","a Pigouvian tax","regulatory capture","the Coase theorem"],"a":2,"e":"When a regulator serves the regulated industry instead of the public, that is regulatory capture. It is a government failure, not a market failure of hidden information.","d":3},{"q":"A health insurer prices policies at the average risk of all applicants. Low-risk people find them overpriced and drop out, the remaining pool is sicker, and premiums rise again. This pattern is called:","o":["moral hazard","adverse selection","the free-rider problem","a positive externality"],"a":1,"e":"Pricing at the average attracts high-risk buyers and repels low-risk ones, a pre-deal hidden-information problem. Moral hazard is about behavior after coverage starts.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Dominant strategy","Best choice whatever rivals do"],["Nash equilibrium","No player gains by switching alone"],["Pigouvian tax","Tax equal to marginal external cost"],["Cap and trade","Tradable permits under a total pollution limit"],["Common resource","Rival but nonexcludable good"],["Free rider","Benefits without paying for it"],["Adverse selection","Hidden characteristics before the deal"],["Moral hazard","Riskier behavior after being protected"]],"traps":["Trap: A Nash equilibrium is the outcome that gives the players the highest total payoff. Truth: it is only a mutual best response; in a prisoner's dilemma it is worse for both.","Trap: Positive externalities mean the market overproduces. Truth: positive externalities mean underproduction; negative externalities mean overproduction.","Trap: The Coase theorem says the polluter must pay. Truth: with low transaction costs and clear rights, the efficient result occurs whichever side holds the right.","Trap: Adverse selection and moral hazard are the same thing. Truth: adverse selection is hidden information before the deal; moral hazard is hidden action after it.","Trap: Public goods and common resources are the same. Truth: both are nonexcludable, but public goods are nonrival while common resources are rival."],"mnemonic":"Externalities: Negative = Neighbors hurt, Naughty overproduction (tax it); Positive = Perks for others, underproduction (subsidize it).","cram":["Dominant strategy beats every rival choice; Nash equilibrium is mutual best response.","Prisoner's dilemma: both defect, both worse off; cartels collapse for this reason.","Credible threats matter: backward induction removes empty threats.","Negative externality: overproduction, Pigouvian tax equals marginal external cost.","Positive externality: underproduction, subsidy equals marginal external benefit.","Coase: clear property rights plus low transaction costs gives efficient bargaining.","Public good: nonrival and nonexcludable. Common resource: rival and nonexcludable.","Adverse selection is before the deal; moral hazard is after the deal."],"ytv":[{"title":"Game Theory and Oligopoly: Crash Course Economics #26","channel":"CrashCourse","id":"PCcVODWm-oY","why":"Covers payoff matrices, the prisoner's dilemma, Nash equilibrium and why cartels are hard to hold together."},{"title":"Market Failures, Taxes, and Subsidies: Crash Course Economics #21","channel":"CrashCourse","id":"13JOGWzY8kE","why":"Explains externalities, public goods, and how taxes and subsidies correct them."}]},{"id":"u7","title":"Measuring the Macroeconomy","day":4,"summary":"Learn how GDP is defined and calculated, how nominal and real values, deflators, CPI and inflation work, who counts as unemployed, the types of unemployment, and the phases of the business cycle.","lesson":[{"h":"1. What GDP measures (and what it leaves out)","body":"<p><strong>Gross domestic product (GDP)</strong> is the <strong>market value of all final goods and services produced within a country during a specific period</strong> (usually a quarter or a year). Read each word:</p><ul><li><strong>Market value:</strong> goods are added up at prices, so apples and airplanes can be combined into one number.</li><li><strong>Final:</strong> only goods sold to their end user count. Intermediate goods (flour sold to a bakery) are not counted separately, because their value is already inside the price of the final good (bread). Counting both would be <em>double counting</em>. Another way to avoid double counting is to add up <strong>value added</strong> at each stage.</li><li><strong>Produced:</strong> GDP counts current production, not past production. A used car sold today is not new production, though the dealer's service margin is.</li><li><strong>Within a country:</strong> location, not ownership. A Toyota factory in Alabama counts in US GDP, and the profit of a US company's plant in Mexico counts in Mexico's GDP.</li></ul><p><strong>Not counted:</strong> transfer payments (Social Security, unemployment benefits; no good is produced in exchange), purchases of used goods, financial transactions (buying stocks or bonds), intermediate goods, household work and volunteering, and the underground economy. Also GDP is not a measure of well-being: it ignores leisure, environmental damage, and the distribution of income.</p><p><strong>Value-added example:</strong> a farmer sells wheat for $20 to a mill, the mill sells flour for $50 to a bakery, and the bakery sells bread to consumers for $120. Value added is $20 + $30 + $70 = <strong>$120</strong>, exactly the price of the final good. GDP counts $120, not $190.</p>"},{"h":"2. The expenditure approach: C + I + G + NX","body":"<p>GDP can be measured by adding up all spending on final goods and services:</p><p><code>GDP = C + I + G + NX</code>, where <code>NX = exports - imports</code>.</p><ul><li><strong>C (consumption):</strong> household spending on goods and services (the biggest part, about two-thirds of US GDP). Buying a new house is <em>not</em> consumption.</li><li><strong>I (investment):</strong> business spending on equipment, buildings and software, <strong>new residential construction</strong>, and <strong>changes in inventories</strong>. In economics, buying stocks or bonds is not investment.</li><li><strong>G (government purchases):</strong> federal, state, and local spending on goods and services (roads, military equipment, teachers' salaries). Transfer payments are excluded.</li><li><strong>NX (net exports):</strong> exports are produced here but bought abroad (add), imports are produced abroad but bought here (subtract so that we do not count foreign production).</li></ul><p><strong>Numbers ($ billions):</strong> C = 14,000, I = 3,500, G = 3,600, exports = 2,800, imports = 3,300. NX = 2,800 - 3,300 = -500. GDP = 14,000 + 3,500 + 3,600 - 500 = <strong>20,600</strong>. Imports are subtracted because spending on foreign-made goods is already inside C, I, and G, and it is not domestic production. A trade deficit does not by itself mean GDP is lower; it just means some of that spending went abroad.</p><p><strong>Inventories:</strong> if a firm produces $5 million of goods but sells only $3 million, the unsold $2 million is counted as inventory investment, so GDP still includes all $5 million of production.</p><p><strong>Used-car dealer:</strong> if a dealer buys a used car for $10,000 and sells it for $12,000, only the $2,000 margin (the dealer's service) is current production.</p>"},{"h":"3. Nominal vs. real GDP, the GDP deflator, growth, and per-capita GDP","body":"<p><strong>Nominal GDP</strong> uses current-year prices, so it can rise just because prices rose. <strong>Real GDP</strong> uses the prices of a fixed <strong>base year</strong>, so changes reflect only changes in quantities produced. Real GDP is what economists use for growth and recessions.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> an economy produces pizzas and phones. In the base year (2020): 100 pizzas at $10 and 20 phones at $200, so nominal GDP = 1,000 + 4,000 = $5,000. In 2024: 110 pizzas at $12 and 25 phones at $220. Nominal GDP = 1,320 + 5,500 = <strong>$6,820</strong>. Real GDP at base-year prices = 110 x $10 + 25 x $200 = 1,100 + 5,000 = <strong>$6,100</strong>.</p><p><strong>GDP deflator</strong> = (nominal GDP / real GDP) x 100 = (6,820 / 6,100) x 100 = <strong>111.8</strong>. It means prices of all domestically produced goods are about 11.8 percent higher than in the base year. The base year deflator is always 100.</p><p><strong>Real GDP growth rate</strong> = (real GDP this year - real GDP last year) / real GDP last year. If real GDP rises from $20.0 trillion to $20.8 trillion, growth = 0.8 / 20.0 = <strong>4.0%</strong>. To get real growth from nominal data: (1 + nominal growth) / (1 + inflation) - 1. If nominal GDP rises 8% and the deflator rises 5%, real growth is 1.08 / 1.05 - 1 = about <strong>2.9%</strong>.</p><p><strong>GDP per capita</strong> = GDP / population. Real GDP of $600 billion with 20 million people is $30,000 per person. It is a better indicator of average living standards than total GDP, since a large country can have a large GDP and still be poor per person.</p>"},{"h":"4. CPI, inflation, and why measures can be biased","body":"<p><strong>Inflation</strong> is a sustained rise in the general price level. The most common measure is the <strong>Consumer Price Index (CPI)</strong>, which tracks the cost of a fixed basket of goods and services bought by a typical urban household (housing, food, transportation, medical care, and so on).</p><p><code>CPI = (cost of the basket in the current year / cost of the basket in the base year) x 100</code></p><p>If the basket cost $400 in the base year and $436 now, CPI = 436 / 400 x 100 = <strong>109</strong>. The <strong>inflation rate</strong> is the percentage change in the price index: <code>(CPI now - CPI before) / CPI before x 100</code>. If CPI rises from 250.0 to 258.0, inflation = 8 / 250 = <strong>3.2%</strong>.</p><p><strong>Real vs. nominal wages:</strong> real wage growth is about nominal wage growth minus inflation. If your pay rises 3% while prices rise 5%, your purchasing power falls: 1.03 / 1.05 - 1 = about <strong>-1.9%</strong>. This is why contracts and Social Security use cost-of-living adjustments (COLAs).</p><p><strong>Biases in the CPI</strong> (usually pushing measured inflation up):</p><ul><li><strong>Substitution bias:</strong> a fixed basket ignores that shoppers switch to cheaper goods when prices rise.</li><li><strong>New products:</strong> new goods enter the basket after a delay, missing early price drops.</li><li><strong>Quality changes:</strong> a $1,000 phone today does far more than a $1,000 phone ten years ago, but a price rise is partly a quality gain.</li><li><strong>Outlet substitution:</strong> consumers shift to discount stores.</li></ul><p><strong>CPI vs. GDP deflator:</strong> the CPI covers goods consumers buy (including imports) using a fixed basket. The deflator covers all goods <em>produced domestically</em> (consumption, investment, government, exports) and its basket changes each year. If the price of imported TVs jumps, the CPI rises but the GDP deflator does not.</p>"},{"h":"5. The labor market: who is counted?","body":"<p>The Bureau of Labor Statistics divides the adult population (age 16 or older, civilian, not institutionalized) into three groups:</p><ul><li><strong>Employed:</strong> worked for pay at least 1 hour in the survey week (part-time workers who want full-time work still count as employed).</li><li><strong>Unemployed:</strong> not working, <em>actively looked for work in the past 4 weeks</em>, and available to work.</li><li><strong>Not in the labor force:</strong> everyone else (students, retirees, stay-at-home parents, and <strong>discouraged workers</strong> who want a job but have given up searching).</li></ul><p><code>Labor force = employed + unemployed</code></p><p><code>Unemployment rate = unemployed / labor force x 100</code></p><p><code>Labor force participation rate (LFPR) = labor force / adult population x 100</code></p><p><strong>Example:</strong> adult population 1,000; employed 600; unemployed 40; not in the labor force 360. Labor force = 640. Unemployment rate = 40 / 640 = <strong>6.25%</strong>. LFPR = 640 / 1,000 = <strong>64%</strong>. (Note that 40 / 1,000 = 4% is the wrong denominator.)</p><p><strong>Discouraged-worker effect:</strong> if 20 of the 40 unemployed give up searching, the unemployed fall to 20, the labor force falls to 620, and the rate drops to 20 / 620 = <strong>3.2%</strong> while LFPR falls to 62%. The rate improved even though nobody found a job. This is a reason the headline rate can understate labor market weakness.</p>"},{"h":"6. Types of unemployment and the natural rate","body":"<ul><li><strong>Frictional:</strong> short-term job search and transitions: new graduates, people changing jobs or cities. It exists even in healthy economies and is partly good because it improves matches.</li><li><strong>Structural:</strong> a mismatch between workers' skills or location and the jobs available, caused by technology, trade, or industry decline (coal miners when a mine closes, retail clerks replaced by automation). It lasts longer and often needs retraining.</li><li><strong>Cyclical:</strong> caused by downturns in the business cycle, when spending falls and firms lay off workers. It rises in recessions and disappears in expansions.</li><li><strong>Seasonal:</strong> predictable changes with the calendar (ski instructors in summer, retail workers after the holidays, farm workers). Published data are often seasonally adjusted to remove it.</li></ul><p><strong>Natural rate of unemployment</strong> = frictional + structural unemployment (the rate at full employment, with zero cyclical unemployment). It is not zero. If the actual rate is 7% and the natural rate is 5%, cyclical unemployment = 7% - 5% = <strong>2%</strong>. If the actual rate is 4.5% with a natural rate of 5%, cyclical unemployment is -0.5%, meaning the economy is running above its sustainable capacity (overheating).</p><p><strong>Why it matters for managers:</strong> high cyclical unemployment means customers are cutting spending but hiring is easy and cheap; low unemployment means labor shortages and wage pressure.</p>"},{"h":"7. The business cycle","body":"<p>Real GDP does not grow smoothly. The <strong>business cycle</strong> is the pattern of expansions and contractions around a long-term growth trend. It has four phases:</p><ol><li><strong>Expansion:</strong> real GDP rises, unemployment falls, spending and hiring grow.</li><li><strong>Peak:</strong> the high point of activity before a decline.</li><li><strong>Contraction (recession):</strong> real GDP falls, unemployment rises, profits drop. A common rule of thumb is two consecutive quarters of declining real GDP. In the US, the National Bureau of Economic Research (NBER) officially dates recessions using a broader set of indicators: employment, income, production, and sales.</li><li><strong>Trough:</strong> the low point before a recovery begins.</li></ol><p>A very deep and long downturn is called a depression. Cycles vary in length and are not regular.</p><p><strong>Indicators:</strong> <em>leading</em> indicators change before the economy turns (building permits, stock prices, new orders), <em>coincident</em> indicators move with it (employment, industrial production), and <em>lagging</em> indicators change afterward (unemployment duration, inflation).</p><p><strong>Manager's use:</strong> in contractions, firms cut costs, delay investment, and focus on cash; in expansions, they add capacity and hire. Seasonal patterns, trends, and cycles should be separated when reading sales data.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Gross domestic product is the market value of all final goods and services produced within a country during a period. Only final goods count, to avoid double counting of intermediate goods.","The expenditure approach says GDP equals consumption plus investment plus government purchases plus net exports, where net exports are exports minus imports.","Transfer payments, used goods sales, stock and bond purchases, and household work do not count in GDP. Unsold output counts as inventory investment.","Nominal GDP uses current prices. Real GDP uses base year prices, so it shows only changes in quantity. The GDP deflator is nominal GDP divided by real GDP, times one hundred.","The real GDP growth rate is the percentage change in real GDP. GDP per capita divides GDP by population and is a better gauge of average living standards.","The consumer price index compares the cost of a fixed basket of consumer goods to its cost in a base year. The inflation rate is the percentage change in the index.","The consumer price index tends to overstate inflation because of substitution bias, new products, quality changes, and outlet substitution. It includes imports, while the GDP deflator covers only domestic production.","A person is unemployed only if they have no job, searched in the past four weeks, and are available to work. Discouraged workers who stopped searching are not in the labor force.","The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labor force. The labor force participation rate is the labor force divided by the adult population.","Frictional unemployment is short term job search, structural unemployment is a mismatch of skills or location, cyclical unemployment comes from downturns, and seasonal unemployment follows the calendar.","The natural rate of unemployment is frictional plus structural unemployment. Cyclical unemployment is the actual rate minus the natural rate.","The business cycle has expansion, peak, contraction, and trough. A recession is commonly defined as two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP."],"examples":[{"q":"In billions of dollars: consumption 14,000; investment 3,500; government purchases 3,600; exports 2,800; imports 3,300; Social Security payments 1,200; purchases of used cars 400. Find GDP and say which items were left out.","steps":["Net exports = exports - imports = 2,800 - 3,300 = -500.","GDP = C + I + G + NX = 14,000 + 3,500 + 3,600 + (-500).","GDP = 21,100 - 500 = 20,600.","Social Security payments are transfer payments: no good or service is produced in exchange, so they are excluded.","Used-car purchases are not current production (only the dealer's margin would count), so they are excluded."],"answer":"GDP = $20,600 billion; transfers and used-car sales are not counted."},{"q":"An economy makes pizzas and phones. Base year 2020: 100 pizzas at $10, 20 phones at $200. In 2024: 110 pizzas at $12, 25 phones at $220. Find nominal GDP in 2024, real GDP in 2024 (base 2020), the GDP deflator, and real GDP growth since 2020.","steps":["Nominal 2020 = 100 x 10 + 20 x 200 = 1,000 + 4,000 = 5,000.","Nominal 2024 = 110 x 12 + 25 x 220 = 1,320 + 5,500 = 6,820.","Real 2024 = 110 x 10 + 25 x 200 = 1,100 + 5,000 = 6,100 (base-year prices).","Deflator = 6,820 / 6,100 x 100 = 111.8.","Real growth since 2020 = (6,100 - 5,000) / 5,000 = 22%."],"answer":"Nominal $6,820; real $6,100; deflator 111.8; real GDP is 22% higher than in 2020."},{"q":"Adult population is 1,000. There are 600 employed, 40 unemployed, and 360 not in the labor force. Compute the unemployment rate and labor force participation rate. Then 20 of the unemployed become discouraged and stop searching. Recompute both.","steps":["Labor force = 600 + 40 = 640.","Unemployment rate = 40 / 640 = 6.25%.","LFPR = 640 / 1,000 = 64%.","After 20 stop searching: unemployed = 20, labor force = 620, not in labor force = 380.","New unemployment rate = 20 / 620 = 3.2%; new LFPR = 620 / 1,000 = 62%."],"answer":"Before: 6.25% and 64%. After: about 3.2% and 62%. The rate fell without any new jobs."}],"videos":[{"title":"GDP explained: C + I + G + NX with examples","query":"GDP expenditure approach C+I+G+NX explained what counts what doesn't"},{"title":"Nominal vs real GDP and GDP deflator","query":"nominal GDP vs real GDP GDP deflator calculation explained"},{"title":"Unemployment rate, labor force participation, types of unemployment","query":"unemployment rate labor force participation frictional structural cyclical explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"GDP definition","b":"Market value of all final goods and services produced within a country in a given period."},{"f":"Expenditure formula","b":"GDP = C + I + G + NX, where NX = exports - imports."},{"f":"What is counted as investment (I) in GDP?","b":"Business equipment and structures, new residential construction, and inventory changes; not stock or bond purchases."},{"f":"Name four things NOT counted in GDP","b":"Transfer payments, used goods, intermediate goods, and financial asset purchases (also household work and the underground economy)."},{"f":"Value added","b":"The value a firm adds at its stage: sales minus the cost of intermediate inputs. Sum across stages equals the final price."},{"f":"Nominal GDP vs real GDP","b":"Nominal uses current prices; real uses base-year prices and shows only output changes."},{"f":"GDP deflator formula","b":"(Nominal GDP / Real GDP) x 100. Base-year value is 100."},{"f":"Real GDP growth rate formula","b":"(Real GDP now - Real GDP before) / Real GDP before x 100."},{"f":"GDP per capita","b":"GDP divided by population; a gauge of average living standards."},{"f":"CPI formula","b":"(Cost of basket now / cost of basket in base year) x 100."},{"f":"Inflation rate formula","b":"(CPI now - CPI before) / CPI before x 100."},{"f":"Substitution bias","b":"The fixed CPI basket ignores shifts to cheaper goods, so the CPI overstates the cost of living."},{"f":"CPI vs GDP deflator","b":"CPI: fixed consumer basket, includes imports. Deflator: all domestic output, changing basket, excludes imports."},{"f":"Who is unemployed?","b":"No job, searched in the past 4 weeks, and available to work."},{"f":"Discouraged worker","b":"Wants a job but stopped looking; counted as not in the labor force, not unemployed."},{"f":"Unemployment rate","b":"Unemployed / labor force x 100."},{"f":"Labor force participation rate","b":"Labor force / adult population x 100."},{"f":"Frictional unemployment","b":"Short-term search between jobs or after school; present in healthy economies."},{"f":"Structural unemployment","b":"Skills or location mismatch caused by technology, trade, or industry change; long lasting."},{"f":"Cyclical unemployment","b":"Joblessness from downturns; actual rate minus natural rate."},{"f":"Natural rate of unemployment","b":"Frictional plus structural unemployment; the rate at full employment (zero cyclical)."},{"f":"Business cycle phases","b":"Expansion, peak, contraction (recession), trough."}],"questions":[{"q":"GDP is best defined as the:","o":["total value of all goods and services bought and sold, including used goods","market value of final goods and services produced within a country in a period","sum of all incomes received by a country's citizens living abroad","value of all financial assets held in a country"],"a":1,"e":"GDP counts final production within borders in a period. Used goods and financial assets are not current production, and income of citizens abroad is a different concept (GNP-like).","d":1},{"q":"In the expenditure approach, GDP equals:","o":["C + I + G - NX","C + I + G + NX","C + S + T + NX","C + I + transfers + NX"],"a":1,"e":"The standard formula is consumption plus investment plus government purchases plus net exports. Transfers are not purchases, and net exports are added (exports minus imports).","d":1},{"q":"Which transaction is counted in this year's GDP?","o":["A family buys a used truck from a neighbor","The government sends a Social Security payment","An investor buys shares of stock","A business buys a new delivery truck"],"a":3,"e":"A new truck bought by a firm is investment in current production. A used sale, a transfer payment, and a stock purchase are not new production.","d":1},{"q":"Nominal GDP differs from real GDP because nominal GDP is measured using:","o":["base-year prices","current-year prices","constant quantities","foreign currency prices"],"a":1,"e":"Nominal GDP values current output at current prices, so it can rise from inflation alone. Real GDP uses base-year prices.","d":1},{"q":"The Consumer Price Index (CPI) measures the:","o":["average wage paid to workers","prices of all goods produced in the country","change in stock market prices","cost of a fixed basket of consumer goods"],"a":3,"e":"CPI tracks the cost of a typical consumer basket over time. Wages, all domestic output prices (the deflator), and stocks are different measures.","d":1},{"q":"Which person is counted as unemployed?","o":["A person with no job who searched last week and is available","A retiree who does not want any kind of job","A full-time student who is not seeking work","A worker who has stopped searching because he is discouraged"],"a":0,"e":"Unemployed means no job, actively searching in the past four weeks, and available. The others are not in the labor force.","d":1},{"q":"Unemployment that results from a downturn in the business cycle is called:","o":["frictional unemployment","cyclical unemployment","structural unemployment","seasonal unemployment"],"a":1,"e":"Cyclical unemployment rises in recessions when spending and production fall. Frictional is job search, structural is skill mismatch, and seasonal follows the calendar.","d":1},{"q":"The four phases of the business cycle are:","o":["expansion, peak, contraction, and trough","inflation, deflation, stagflation, and recovery","boom, bust, bubble, and burst","supply, demand, equilibrium, and shortage"],"a":0,"e":"The standard sequence is expansion, peak, contraction (recession), and trough. The other lists are not the formal phases.","d":1},{"q":"Consumption is $14,000, investment $3,500, government purchases $3,600, exports $2,800, and imports $3,300 (all in billions). GDP is:","o":["$23,900 billion","$20,600 billion","$27,200 billion","$21,100 billion"],"a":1,"e":"NX = 2,800 - 3,300 = -500, so GDP = 14,000 + 3,500 + 3,600 - 500 = 20,600. $23,900 ignores imports. $27,200 adds imports instead of subtracting. $21,100 is C + I + G only.","d":2},{"q":"Nominal GDP is $6,820 billion and real GDP (in base-year prices) is $6,100 billion. The GDP deflator is about:","o":["111.8","89.4","122.0","100.0"],"a":0,"e":"Deflator = nominal / real x 100 = 6,820 / 6,100 x 100 = 111.8. 89.4 flips the ratio. 122.0 is not supported by the data. 100.0 is the base-year value.","d":2},{"q":"Real GDP rises from $20.0 trillion to $20.8 trillion. The real growth rate is:","o":["0.8%","3.8%","5.0%","4.0%"],"a":3,"e":"Growth = 0.8 / 20.0 = 4.0%. 0.8% is the dollar change in trillions, not a percent. 3.8% divides by the new level instead of the old.","d":2},{"q":"A country has real GDP of $600 billion and 20 million people. Real GDP per capita is:","o":["$3,000","$300,000","$30,000","$20,000"],"a":2,"e":"600,000,000,000 / 20,000,000 = $30,000. $3,000 and $300,000 are decimal slips.","d":2},{"q":"CPI rises from 250.0 to 258.0 in one year. The inflation rate is:","o":["8.0%","3.1%","3.2%","0.8%"],"a":2,"e":"Inflation = (258 - 250) / 250 = 3.2%. 8.0 is the change in index points, not percent. 3.1% divides by the new CPI.","d":2},{"q":"A basket of goods cost $400 in the base year and costs $436 today. The CPI today is:","o":["91.7","36","109","9.0"],"a":2,"e":"CPI = 436 / 400 x 100 = 109. 91.7 inverts the ratio. 36 and 9.0 are dollar or percent differences, not index values.","d":2},{"q":"There are 600 employed, 40 unemployed, and 360 people not in the labor force. The unemployment rate is:","o":["4.0%","6.7%","3.6%","6.25%"],"a":3,"e":"Labor force = 640, so rate = 40 / 640 = 6.25%. 4.0% divides by the whole population. 6.7% divides by employed only. 3.6% matches no correct calculation (the share not in the labor force is 36%).","d":2},{"q":"Using the same data (600 employed, 40 unemployed, 360 not in the labor force), the labor force participation rate is:","o":["64%","60%","36%","6.25%"],"a":0,"e":"LFPR = labor force / adult population = 640 / 1,000 = 64%. 60% is the employment-population ratio. 36% is the share not in the labor force. 6.25% is the unemployment rate.","d":2},{"q":"When unemployed workers give up searching and drop out of the labor force, the measured unemployment rate:","o":["rises, because fewer people are working","stays the same, since they remain jobless","falls only if the economy is in expansion","falls, even though no new jobs were created"],"a":3,"e":"They leave both the unemployed count and the labor force, so the rate falls (and participation falls). The hidden weakness is why participation rate matters.","d":2},{"q":"A recent college graduate spends two months interviewing before accepting an engineering job. This is:","o":["structural unemployment","cyclical unemployment","frictional unemployment","seasonal unemployment"],"a":2,"e":"A short search while matching with a job is frictional. Structural requires a lasting skills or location mismatch, and cyclical is caused by recession.","d":2},{"q":"Automated machines permanently replace assembly workers in a plant, and the laid-off workers lack the skills for available openings. This is mainly:","o":["structural unemployment","frictional unemployment","cyclical unemployment","seasonal unemployment"],"a":0,"e":"Technology created a skills mismatch, which lasts until workers retrain or relocate. It is not caused by the business cycle.","d":2},{"q":"The actual unemployment rate is 7% and the natural rate is 5%. Cyclical unemployment is:","o":["5%","2%","12%","7%"],"a":1,"e":"Cyclical = actual - natural = 7 - 5 = 2 percentage points. 12% adds them. 5% and 7% are the given components.","d":2},{"q":"Consumers switch to cheaper brands when prices rise, but the CPI basket stays fixed. This causes the CPI to:","o":["understate the increase in the cost of living","measure inflation exactly","overstate the increase in the cost of living","ignore changes in prices"],"a":2,"e":"A fixed basket keeps weighting goods that people stopped buying so much of, which overstates cost-of-living increases. This is substitution bias.","d":2},{"q":"A sharp rise in the price of imported consumer electronics will most directly:","o":["raise the GDP deflator but not the CPI","raise both by exactly the same amount","raise real GDP","raise the CPI but not the GDP deflator"],"a":3,"e":"The CPI includes imported consumer goods. The deflator covers only domestically produced goods and services, so import prices do not enter it.","d":3},{"q":"A dealership buys a used car for $10,000 and sells it for $12,000. How much does this sale add to current-year GDP?","o":["$12,000","$10,000","$22,000","$2,000"],"a":3,"e":"Only the dealer's value added (the service margin of $2,000) is current production. The car itself was counted in the year it was made. Adding $12,000 or $22,000 double counts.","d":3},{"q":"A farmer sells wheat for $20 to a mill. The mill sells flour for $50 to a bakery, which sells bread to consumers for $120. How much value does the flour mill add?","o":["$50","$30","$20","$70"],"a":1,"e":"Value added = sales minus intermediate inputs = 50 - 20 = $30. $50 is its sales. $20 is the farmer's value added. $70 is the baker's.","d":3},{"q":"Your nominal pay rises 3% while the CPI rises 5%. Your real wage changes by approximately:","o":["+8.0%","+2.0%","-1.9%","0%"],"a":2,"e":"Real change = 1.03 / 1.05 - 1 = -1.9%. Adding the two (+8%) is wrong. Simple subtraction (about -2%) is only an approximation of the exact -1.9%. A raise below inflation is a real pay cut.","d":3},{"q":"Nominal GDP grows 8% while the GDP deflator rises 5%. Real GDP growth is about:","o":["13.4%","2.9%","5.0%","8.0%"],"a":1,"e":"Real growth = 1.08 / 1.05 - 1 = 2.9%. Multiplying the two (13.4%) goes the wrong direction. 5% is inflation, and 8% is nominal growth.","d":3},{"q":"Real GDP growth rates for four consecutive quarters are +1.2%, -0.4%, -0.9%, and +0.3%. Which statement is most accurate?","o":["Q2 and Q3 meet the recession rule of thumb; the NBER makes the official call","There was no recession at all, since the full-year change was positive","A recession began in quarter 1 because growth was already slowing","A recession occurs only when unemployment rises above 10 percent"],"a":0,"e":"Two consecutive quarters of falling real GDP (Q2 and Q3) meets the rule of thumb. The NBER officially dates US recessions using broader data. There is no 10% unemployment rule.","d":3},{"q":"An economy has an adult population of 1,000, with 600 employed and 40 unemployed. If 20 unemployed people give up searching, the new unemployment rate is:","o":["3.2%","6.25%","2.0%","3.3%"],"a":0,"e":"New unemployed = 20, labor force = 620, so 20 / 620 = 3.2%. 6.25% is the old rate. 2.0% divides by 1,000. 3.3% divides by the 600 employed instead of the labor force.","d":3},{"q":"The actual unemployment rate is 4.5% and the natural rate is 5.0%. Which conclusion is correct?","o":["Cyclical unemployment is -0.5%, so the economy is above normal capacity","Cyclical unemployment is 0.5% and the economy is in recession","Cyclical unemployment is zero because the actual rate is below 5%","The natural rate must therefore be exactly 4.5% as well"],"a":0,"e":"Cyclical = actual - natural = 4.5 - 5.0 = -0.5, meaning employment is above its sustainable level (overheating). The natural rate is a structural feature and does not simply equal the actual rate.","d":3},{"q":"A firm produces $5 million of goods in a year but sells only $3 million to customers. What does this add to GDP?","o":["$3 million, because only completed sales count in GDP","$2 million, which is only the unsold portion of output","$5 million, with $2 million as inventory investment","$8 million, which adds production and sales together"],"a":2,"e":"GDP measures production, so unsold output is counted as inventory investment in I. Counting only sales would omit production. Adding sales and production double counts.","d":3}],"pairs":[["GDP","Value of final output within borders"],["Real GDP","Output valued at base-year prices"],["GDP deflator","Nominal over real GDP, times 100"],["CPI","Cost of fixed consumer basket index"],["Labor force","Employed plus unemployed people"],["Discouraged worker","Stopped searching; not in labor force"],["Natural rate","Frictional plus structural unemployment"],["Trough","Low point before recovery begins"]],"traps":["Trap: Social Security checks and used-car sales add to GDP. Truth: transfers and used goods are not current production, so they are excluded.","Trap: Imports are not part of the formula, so they do not matter. Truth: imports are subtracted in net exports so that foreign production is not counted as domestic.","Trap: Unemployment rate = unemployed divided by the total population. Truth: the denominator is the labor force (employed plus unemployed).","Trap: Natural rate of unemployment means zero unemployment. Truth: it equals frictional plus structural unemployment, which exist even at full employment.","Trap: A falling unemployment rate always means more jobs. Truth: discouraged workers who quit searching leave the labor force and lower the rate."],"mnemonic":"GDP = C + I + G + NX: 'Consumers, Investors, Government, Net exports.' Unemployment rate always divides by the LABOR FORCE, never the population.","cram":["GDP counts final goods made within borders; skips transfers, used goods, intermediates, securities.","GDP is C plus I plus G plus net exports; inventories count as investment.","Deflator is nominal over real GDP times 100; real growth strips out inflation.","CPI is current basket cost over base cost times 100; inflation is percent change.","CPI overstates inflation: substitution bias, new products, quality change, outlets.","Unemployment rate is unemployed over labor force; participation is labor force over adults.","Unemployed need a search in four weeks; discouraged workers are outside the labor force.","Natural rate: frictional plus structural. Cycle: expansion, peak, contraction, trough."],"ytv":[{"title":"Macroeconomics: Crash Course Economics #5","channel":"CrashCourse","id":"d8uTB5XorBw","why":"Introduces GDP, inflation, unemployment, and the macro big picture in plain language."},{"title":"Unemployment- Macro Topic 2.3","channel":"Jacob Clifford","id":"9i6vf5Wz6nY","why":"Walks through the labor force, unemployment rate, and types of unemployment with calculations."},{"title":"Inflation and Bubbles and Tulips: Crash Course Economics #7","channel":"CrashCourse","id":"T8-85cZRI9o","why":"Covers inflation, the CPI, and how price changes are measured and why they matter."}]},{"id":"u8","title":"Aggregate Demand, Supply & Growth","day":4,"summary":"Learn the AD-AS model: why aggregate demand slopes down, what shifts AD and SRAS, how the economy reaches long-run equilibrium, recessionary and inflationary gaps, types of inflation and stagflation, the spending multiplier, and the sources of long-run growth with the rule of 70.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Aggregate demand and why it slopes downward","body":"<p>The <strong>AD-AS model</strong> explains the overall price level and real GDP. The horizontal axis is real GDP (total output) and the vertical axis is the price level (for example the GDP deflator or CPI). Do not confuse it with market supply and demand for a single product.</p><p><strong>Aggregate demand (AD)</strong> is the total quantity of real GDP demanded at each price level. It is made of the same spending categories as GDP: <code>AD = C + I + G + NX</code> (consumption, investment, government purchases, net exports).</p><p><strong>Why AD slopes downward</strong> (not for the same reasons as a single-product demand curve):</p><ul><li><strong>Wealth effect:</strong> a lower price level raises the real value of savings and cash, so households spend more.</li><li><strong>Interest-rate effect:</strong> a lower price level means people need less money for transactions, so interest rates fall, which encourages borrowing for homes, cars, and business investment.</li><li><strong>Exchange-rate (net export) effect:</strong> lower domestic prices make exports cheaper and imports relatively expensive, so net exports rise.</li></ul><p>A change in the price level causes a <em>movement along</em> AD. Anything else that changes spending at every price level <em>shifts</em> AD.</p>"},{"h":"2. What shifts aggregate demand","body":"<p>AD shifts <strong>right</strong> (increase) when spending rises at every price level, and <strong>left</strong> when it falls. Think through the components:</p><ul><li><strong>Consumption (C):</strong> consumer confidence, household wealth (stock and housing prices), taxes (lower taxes raise disposable income), expectations about future income.</li><li><strong>Investment (I):</strong> interest rates (lower rates raise I), business confidence and expected profits, taxes on business, new technology that requires new equipment.</li><li><strong>Government (G):</strong> government purchases and, indirectly, tax and transfer policy (fiscal policy).</li><li><strong>Net exports (NX):</strong> foreign income (a boom abroad raises exports), exchange rates (a stronger domestic currency makes exports pricey and imports cheap, so NX falls), trade policy.</li><li><strong>Monetary policy:</strong> a central bank cutting interest rates raises C and I, so AD shifts right.</li></ul><p><strong>Business example:</strong> if consumer confidence plunges, retailers see weaker sales across the board. That is a leftward shift in AD, which means lower output and a lower price level in the short run, and rising unemployment.</p><p>Many shifts have a <strong>multiplier effect</strong> (section 6): the final change in AD is larger than the initial change in spending.</p>"},{"h":"3. Aggregate supply: short run vs. long run","body":"<p><strong>Short-run aggregate supply (SRAS)</strong> slopes upward. When the price level rises, firms' output prices rise while many costs (wages in contracts, rents, supplier prices) are <strong>sticky</strong> and adjust slowly. Profits rise, so firms produce more. </p><p><strong>SRAS shifters</strong> (anything that changes production costs at every price level):</p><ul><li><strong>Input prices:</strong> wages, oil and raw materials. Higher costs shift SRAS <em>left</em> (a supply shock); lower costs shift it right.</li><li><strong>Productivity:</strong> higher productivity lowers cost per unit and shifts SRAS right.</li><li><strong>Taxes, subsidies, regulation:</strong> a new regulation or business tax raises costs (left); a subsidy lowers them (right).</li><li><strong>Expected inflation:</strong> if workers and firms expect higher prices, they negotiate higher wages and prices now, shifting SRAS left.</li></ul><p><strong>Long-run aggregate supply (LRAS)</strong> is a <strong>vertical</strong> line at <strong>potential GDP</strong> (full-employment output). In the long run, all wages and prices are flexible, so output depends on real resources and technology, not on the price level. LRAS shifts right with more labor, more capital, better technology, more human capital, and better institutions (section 7).</p><p><strong>Potential GDP</strong> is the output the economy can sustain with unemployment at the natural rate.</p>"},{"h":"4. Equilibrium, output gaps, and self-correction","body":"<p><strong>Short-run equilibrium</strong> is where AD intersects SRAS. <strong>Long-run equilibrium</strong> is where AD, SRAS, and LRAS all intersect, so actual output equals potential output.</p><ul><li><strong>Recessionary gap:</strong> equilibrium real GDP is <em>below</em> potential. Unemployment is above the natural rate. This often follows a fall in AD. Example: potential GDP = $1,000 billion, actual = $950 billion, so the gap is $50 billion.</li><li><strong>Inflationary gap:</strong> equilibrium real GDP is <em>above</em> potential. Unemployment is below the natural rate. The economy is overheating. Example: potential = $1,000 billion, actual = $1,060 billion, gap = $60 billion.</li></ul><p><strong>Self-correction:</strong> in a recessionary gap, high unemployment eventually pushes wages and input prices down, SRAS shifts <strong>right</strong>, the price level falls, and output returns to potential. In an inflationary gap, labor and materials become scarce, wages and costs rise, SRAS shifts <strong>left</strong>, the price level rises, and output falls back to potential. In both cases the long-run result is output at potential.</p><p><strong>Key insight:</strong> in the long run, an increase in AD raises only the <em>price level</em>, not real GDP. Real output is determined by supply (LRAS). Self-correction can be slow, especially when wages are sticky downward (contracts, worker morale), which is why governments sometimes use fiscal or monetary policy instead of waiting (the next unit).</p>"},{"h":"5. Types of inflation and stagflation","body":"<ul><li><strong>Demand-pull inflation:</strong> a rightward shift of AD pulls the price level up while output rises (too much money chasing too few goods). Typical of an overheating economy: output and prices rise together, and unemployment falls.</li><li><strong>Cost-push inflation:</strong> a leftward shift of SRAS (higher wages, oil, or materials) pushes prices up while output falls.</li></ul><p><strong>Stagflation</strong> is the combination of <strong>stagnant output (high unemployment) and inflation</strong>. It is caused by a negative supply shock, such as the 1970s oil embargoes. On the graph, SRAS shifts left: the price level rises and real GDP falls.</p><p><strong>Why it is a policy headache:</strong> stimulating demand to fight unemployment pushes prices up even more, while fighting inflation by cutting demand deepens the recession. There is no simple fix. Waiting for self-correction (wages fall, SRAS recovers) may take time. Supply-side improvements (productivity) help.</p><p><strong>Business example:</strong> if oil prices jump 50%, a trucking company's costs rise on every route (SRAS left). The firm raises prices, but customers also cut back as the economy weakens.</p><p><strong>Quick diagnosis:</strong> price up and output up = AD increase. Price up and output down = SRAS decrease. Price down and output down = AD decrease. Price down and output up = SRAS increase.</p>"},{"h":"6. The spending multiplier","body":"<p>When spending rises by $1, it becomes someone's income, and they spend a fraction of it, which becomes someone else's income, and so on. The total change in GDP is bigger than the original change. The fraction of extra income that is spent is the <strong>marginal propensity to consume (MPC)</strong>; the fraction saved is the <strong>marginal propensity to save (MPS)</strong>, and <code>MPC + MPS = 1</code>.</p><p><code>Spending multiplier = 1 / (1 - MPC) = 1 / MPS</code></p><p><strong>Numbers:</strong> MPC = 0.8 gives a multiplier of 1 / 0.2 = <strong>5</strong>. A $100 billion increase in government spending raises GDP by $500 billion. MPC = 0.75 gives a multiplier of 4, so a $40 billion increase gives $160 billion. The round-by-round view: 100, then 80, then 64, and so on, which sums to 500.</p><p><strong>Closing a gap:</strong> required spending change = gap / multiplier. Gap $50 billion with a multiplier of 5 needs a $10 billion increase. Inflationary gap $60 billion with a multiplier of 4 needs a $15 billion cut in spending.</p><p><strong>Taxes:</strong> a tax change has a smaller effect than an equal change in spending because part of a tax cut is saved immediately. The tax multiplier is <code>-MPC / (1 - MPC)</code>. With MPC = 0.8 it is -4, so a $50 billion tax cut raises GDP by $200 billion (versus $250 billion for $50 billion of extra spending).</p><p><strong>Real-world limits:</strong> the multiplier is smaller in practice because of leakages (taxes, imports, saving), rising interest rates (crowding out), and inflation.</p>"},{"h":"7. Long-run growth and the rule of 70","body":"<p><strong>Economic growth</strong> is a sustained increase in real GDP, or more usefully in real GDP per capita. On the graph, it is a rightward shift of LRAS (and outward shift of the production possibilities frontier).</p><p><strong>Sources of long-run growth:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Productivity</strong> (output per worker-hour): the single most important driver of living standards.</li><li><strong>Physical capital:</strong> more and better machinery, buildings, and infrastructure (capital deepening).</li><li><strong>Technology:</strong> new ideas and methods raise output from the same inputs.</li><li><strong>Human capital:</strong> education, training, and health of the workforce.</li><li><strong>Institutions:</strong> secure property rights, rule of law, honest government, stable money, and open trade. They give people incentives to invest and innovate.</li><li><strong>Natural resources and labor force growth</strong> also matter, but resource-poor countries can still prosper.</li></ul><p>Printing money or price controls do not raise long-run growth. Growth requires more or better inputs, or better institutions.</p><p><strong>Rule of 70:</strong> the number of years it takes a variable to double is roughly <code>70 / (annual growth rate in percent)</code>. At 3.5% growth, income doubles in 20 years; at 2.5% it takes 28 years; at 7% it takes 10 years. Conversely, to double in 14 years you need 70 / 14 = 5% growth.</p><p><strong>Why small differences matter:</strong> two countries start at $20,000 per person. Country A grows 2% (doubles every 35 years), country B grows 4% (doubles every 17.5 years). After 35 years, A has about $40,000 and B has about $80,000 (two doublings). Compounding makes a 2-point difference in growth enormous over a generation.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Aggregate demand is total spending on domestic output at each price level, and it equals consumption plus investment plus government purchases plus net exports.","Aggregate demand slopes downward because of the wealth effect, the interest rate effect, and the exchange rate effect. A change in the price level moves along the curve, while other changes in spending shift it.","Aggregate demand shifts right with higher consumer confidence, lower interest rates, more government spending, higher foreign income, or a weaker domestic currency.","Short run aggregate supply slopes upward because wages and some costs are sticky. It shifts left when input prices or regulation costs rise, and right when productivity improves or input prices fall.","Long run aggregate supply is vertical at potential output, where unemployment is at the natural rate. It shifts right with more labor, capital, technology, human capital, and better institutions.","A recessionary gap means output is below potential and unemployment is above its natural rate. An inflationary gap means output is above potential and unemployment is below its natural rate.","With self correction, wages and prices fall in a recessionary gap so short run supply shifts right, and they rise in an inflationary gap so short run supply shifts left. In the long run, output returns to potential.","Demand pull inflation comes from a rightward shift of aggregate demand. Cost push inflation comes from a leftward shift of short run supply. Stagflation is falling output together with rising prices, caused by a negative supply shock.","The spending multiplier equals one divided by one minus the marginal propensity to consume. With a marginal propensity to consume of point eight, the multiplier is five.","To close a gap, divide the gap by the multiplier. The tax multiplier is smaller than the spending multiplier because part of a tax cut is saved.","Long run growth comes from productivity, physical capital, technology, human capital, and good institutions such as property rights and the rule of law.","The rule of seventy says the years to double equal seventy divided by the growth rate in percent. Small differences in growth rates compound into very large gaps over a generation."],"examples":[{"q":"An economy's potential GDP is $1,000 billion and equilibrium GDP is $950 billion. The MPC is 0.8. (a) Name the gap. (b) How much must government spending increase to close it? (c) Alternatively, how large a tax cut closes it?","steps":["Actual (950) is below potential (1,000), so this is a recessionary gap of $50 billion.","Spending multiplier = 1 / (1 - 0.8) = 5.","Required increase in G = gap / multiplier = 50 / 5 = $10 billion.","Tax multiplier = -MPC / (1 - MPC) = -0.8 / 0.2 = -4.","Required tax cut = 50 / 4 = $12.5 billion.","Check: 12.5 x 4 = 50, and 10 x 5 = 50."],"answer":"A $50 billion recessionary gap; raise spending by $10 billion or cut taxes by $12.5 billion."},{"q":"A global oil price spike raises production costs for many industries. Using AD-AS, describe the short-run effect on the price level, real GDP, and unemployment, and explain why policy makers face a dilemma.","steps":["Higher input costs shift SRAS to the left; AD and LRAS do not change.","The new short-run equilibrium has a higher price level and lower real GDP.","With output below potential, unemployment rises above the natural rate: this is stagflation.","Policy dilemma: raising AD to restore output pushes prices up further; cutting AD to reduce inflation worsens the recession.","Without intervention, high unemployment eventually lowers wages and costs, SRAS shifts back right, and output returns to potential."],"answer":"Prices rise and output falls (stagflation) with higher unemployment; any demand policy trades off inflation against output."},{"q":"Country A grows at 2% a year and country B at 4% a year; both start at $20,000 per person. Use the rule of 70 to estimate each country's income after 35 years, and find how many years each needs to double.","steps":["Country A: 70 / 2 = 35 years to double, so one doubling in 35 years: $40,000.","Country B: 70 / 4 = 17.5 years to double, so 35 years is two doublings: $20,000 x 2 x 2 = $80,000.","Check with compounding: 1.02^35 = 2.00 and 1.04^35 = 3.95, giving about $40,000 and $78,900.","B ends with about twice the income of A."],"answer":"A doubles in 35 years to about $40,000; B doubles every 17.5 years and reaches about $80,000."}],"videos":[{"title":"Aggregate demand and aggregate supply model explained","query":"aggregate demand aggregate supply model explained short run long run equilibrium"},{"title":"Recessionary and inflationary gap, stagflation, self-correction","query":"recessionary gap inflationary gap stagflation self correction AD AS"},{"title":"Spending multiplier and rule of 70 economic growth","query":"spending multiplier MPC explained and rule of 70 economic growth"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Aggregate demand (AD)","b":"Total real GDP demanded at each price level: AD = C + I + G + NX."},{"f":"Three reasons AD slopes down","b":"Wealth effect, interest-rate effect, and exchange-rate (net export) effect."},{"f":"What shifts AD right?","b":"More consumer confidence or wealth, lower interest rates or taxes, more government spending, higher foreign income, weaker currency."},{"f":"SRAS shape and why","b":"Upward sloping because wages and other costs are sticky in the short run."},{"f":"What shifts SRAS?","b":"Input prices (wages, oil), productivity, taxes and regulation, and inflation expectations."},{"f":"LRAS","b":"Vertical line at potential GDP; output depends on resources and technology, not the price level."},{"f":"Potential GDP","b":"Output sustainable at the natural rate of unemployment (full employment)."},{"f":"Long-run equilibrium","b":"AD, SRAS, and LRAS intersect at the same point; actual output equals potential."},{"f":"Recessionary gap","b":"Equilibrium GDP below potential; unemployment above the natural rate."},{"f":"Inflationary gap","b":"Equilibrium GDP above potential; unemployment below the natural rate; overheating."},{"f":"Self-correction in a recession","b":"High unemployment lowers wages and costs; SRAS shifts right; price level falls; output returns to potential."},{"f":"Self-correction in an inflationary gap","b":"Wages and costs rise; SRAS shifts left; price level rises; output falls back to potential."},{"f":"Demand-pull inflation","b":"Price rise caused by a rightward AD shift (output and prices both up)."},{"f":"Cost-push inflation","b":"Price rise caused by a leftward SRAS shift (prices up, output down)."},{"f":"Stagflation","b":"Rising prices with falling output and high unemployment; caused by a negative supply shock."},{"f":"Effect of a long-run AD increase","b":"Higher price level only; real GDP returns to potential."},{"f":"MPC and MPS","b":"MPC: fraction of extra income spent. MPS: fraction saved. MPC + MPS = 1."},{"f":"Spending multiplier","b":"1 / (1 - MPC) = 1 / MPS. MPC of 0.8 gives 5."},{"f":"Required spending change to close a gap","b":"Gap divided by the multiplier."},{"f":"Tax multiplier","b":"-MPC / (1 - MPC); smaller in size than the spending multiplier since part of a tax change is saved."},{"f":"Sources of long-run growth","b":"Productivity, physical capital, technology, human capital, and institutions."},{"f":"Rule of 70","b":"Years to double is about 70 divided by the percent growth rate."}],"questions":[{"q":"Aggregate demand is made up of:","o":["consumption, saving, taxes, and imports of goods","consumption, investment, government, and net exports","wages, rents, interest, and profits earned","labor, capital, land, and entrepreneurship"],"a":1,"e":"AD = C + I + G + NX, the same spending categories as GDP. The other lists are income components, leakages, or factors of production.","d":1},{"q":"The downward slope of the aggregate demand curve is explained by:","o":["diminishing marginal utility and the substitution effect only","the wealth, interest-rate, and exchange-rate effects","rising marginal cost as output expands","sticky wages and prices in the short run"],"a":1,"e":"A lower price level raises real wealth, lowers interest rates, and makes exports cheaper. Sticky wages explain the upward slope of SRAS, not the downward slope of AD.","d":1},{"q":"Which of the following shifts aggregate demand to the right?","o":["A rise in consumer confidence","A rise in oil prices","An increase in the price level","A decrease in government purchases"],"a":0,"e":"Confidence boosts consumption at every price level. Oil prices shift SRAS. A price level change moves along AD. Less government spending shifts AD left.","d":1},{"q":"The long-run aggregate supply (LRAS) curve is:","o":["vertical at potential GDP","horizontal at the current price level","upward sloping","downward sloping"],"a":0,"e":"In the long run, output depends on resources and technology, not the price level, so LRAS is vertical at potential output.","d":1},{"q":"Which event shifts short-run aggregate supply to the left?","o":["An increase in labor productivity","A fall in wages","A new subsidy for businesses","A sharp rise in oil prices"],"a":3,"e":"Higher input costs raise costs of production at every price level, shifting SRAS left. The others lower costs and shift it right.","d":1},{"q":"Stagflation is a combination of:","o":["rising prices and falling output","falling prices and rising output","rising prices and rising output","falling prices and falling output"],"a":0,"e":"Stagflation means stagnant growth (high unemployment) with inflation, caused by a negative supply shock. The other combinations describe AD or SRAS shifts of other kinds.","d":1},{"q":"Demand-pull inflation is caused by:","o":["a leftward shift in short-run aggregate supply","a rightward shift in long-run aggregate supply","a rightward shift in aggregate demand","a fall in government spending"],"a":2,"e":"Too much demand pulls up the price level while raising output. A leftward SRAS shift is cost-push.","d":1},{"q":"The rule of 70 says that the number of years for a variable to double is approximately:","o":["70 times the annual growth rate in percent","the growth rate divided by 70, in years","70 minus the annual growth rate in percent","70 divided by the growth rate in percent"],"a":3,"e":"Years to double = 70 / growth rate (in percent). For 5% growth, 14 years. The other formulas give nonsense.","d":1},{"q":"If the marginal propensity to consume is 0.8, the spending multiplier is:","o":["4","5","1.25","8"],"a":1,"e":"Multiplier = 1 / (1 - 0.8) = 1 / 0.2 = 5. 4 would be 0.75's multiplier. 1.25 is 1 / 0.8. 8 is the MPC times 10.","d":2},{"q":"MPC is 0.75. The government raises purchases by $40 billion. The total increase in GDP is:","o":["$30 billion","$53 billion","$40 billion","$160 billion"],"a":3,"e":"Multiplier = 1 / 0.25 = 4, so GDP rises by 4 x 40 = $160 billion. $40 billion ignores the multiplier. $30 billion is 0.75 x 40. $53 billion is 40 / 0.75.","d":2},{"q":"A recessionary gap exists when:","o":["equilibrium real GDP is above potential and unemployment is below natural","real GDP is below potential and unemployment is above natural","unemployment equals the natural rate and inflation is zero","the price level is falling but real GDP is at potential"],"a":1,"e":"A recessionary gap is output below potential, with cyclical unemployment. Output above potential is an inflationary gap.","d":2},{"q":"Without government action, how does the economy tend to self-correct from a recessionary gap?","o":["Wages and prices rise, shifting SRAS to the left","Wages and input prices fall, so SRAS shifts right","Aggregate demand shifts left permanently","LRAS shifts to the left"],"a":1,"e":"High unemployment pushes wages and costs down, increasing SRAS, lowering the price level, and returning output to potential. A leftward SRAS shift is the correction for an inflationary gap.","d":2},{"q":"An economy is operating above potential GDP with unemployment below the natural rate. In the long run, which adjustment is expected?","o":["Falling wages shift SRAS right and output rises further","LRAS shifts right until potential output matches demand","Rising wages shift SRAS left and output falls to potential","AD shifts left with no change at all in prices"],"a":2,"e":"An inflationary gap leads to tight labor markets, rising costs, and a leftward SRAS shift. Output returns to potential at a higher price level.","d":2},{"q":"Using the rule of 70, how long does it take real GDP per capita to double if it grows at 2.5% per year?","o":["35 years","14 years","175 years","28 years"],"a":3,"e":"70 / 2.5 = 28 years. 35 is for 2% growth. 14 is for 5%. 175 multiplies instead of dividing.","d":2},{"q":"A country wants its real GDP per capita to double in 14 years. About what annual growth rate is required?","o":["5%","2%","7%","14%"],"a":0,"e":"70 / 14 = 5. 2% would take 35 years, 7% would take 10 years, and 14% is the number of years, not the rate.","d":2},{"q":"The central bank raises interest rates sharply. All else equal, what happens to aggregate demand?","o":["It shifts right because savers earn more interest","It does not change because rates only affect supply","It shifts left as investment and consumption fall","Long-run supply shifts left as the economy shrinks"],"a":2,"e":"Higher rates reduce borrowing for homes, cars, and equipment, lowering I and C. Higher savings returns do not offset this overall effect in the model.","d":2},{"q":"The U.S. dollar appreciates sharply against foreign currencies. What is the effect on aggregate demand?","o":["AD shifts left as exports fall and imports rise","AD shifts right because imports are cheaper","SRAS shifts left because the dollar is costly","LRAS shifts right"],"a":0,"e":"A stronger dollar makes US exports more expensive abroad and imports cheaper here, lowering net exports and AD.","d":2},{"q":"Union wage increases and higher steel prices raise costs for many manufacturers. This is most likely to cause:","o":["demand-pull inflation, as AD shifts right","deflation, as SRAS shifts right","faster growth, as LRAS shifts right","cost-push inflation, as SRAS shifts left"],"a":3,"e":"Higher input costs shift SRAS left, raising prices while lowering output. Demand-pull comes from AD shifting right.","d":2},{"q":"Which change would shift the LRAS curve to the right?","o":["A temporary fall in the price of oil and steel","A permanent gain in technology and education","A sustained increase in consumer confidence","A tax cut that raises household disposable income"],"a":1,"e":"LRAS depends on real resources and technology, so productivity-enhancing changes shift it. A temporary oil price drop shifts SRAS only. Confidence and tax cuts shift AD.","d":2},{"q":"A retailer observes that consumer confidence has fallen sharply. According to the AD-AS model, in the short run the economy should see:","o":["lower real GDP and a lower price level","higher real GDP and a higher price level","lower real GDP and a higher price level","unchanged real GDP and a lower price level"],"a":0,"e":"A leftward AD shift along an upward-sloping SRAS lowers both output and the price level. Lower output with higher prices is a supply shock.","d":2},{"q":"Which policy is most likely to increase an economy's long-run growth rate?","o":["Printing more money to boost nominal spending each year","Investing in education, infrastructure, and property rights","Imposing price controls on all essential goods and services","Closing the economy to nearly all foreign trade and investment"],"a":1,"e":"Growth comes from more and better inputs and good institutions. Printing money raises prices in the long run, price controls cause shortages, and closing trade reduces gains.","d":2},{"q":"Potential GDP is $1,000 billion and equilibrium GDP is $950 billion. If MPC = 0.8, by how much must government purchases rise to close the gap?","o":["$50 billion","$40 billion","$10 billion","$250 billion"],"a":2,"e":"Gap = 50, multiplier = 5, so the change in G = 50 / 5 = $10 billion. $50 billion ignores the multiplier. $250 billion multiplies instead of dividing. $40 billion is 50 x 0.8.","d":3},{"q":"Equilibrium GDP is $1,060 billion and potential GDP is $1,000 billion. With MPC = 0.75, how much should government spending change to close the gap?","o":["Increase by $15 billion","Decrease by $60 billion","Decrease by $15 billion","Decrease by $240 billion"],"a":2,"e":"It is an inflationary gap of 60, so spending must fall. Multiplier = 4, so change = 60 / 4 = 15. Increasing spending would widen the gap. $240 billion multiplies instead of dividing.","d":3},{"q":"A global supply shock raises oil prices sharply. Compared with the prior equilibrium, the economy will experience:","o":["a lower price level, higher real GDP, and lower unemployment","a higher price level, lower real GDP, and higher unemployment","a higher price level, higher real GDP, and lower unemployment","a lower price level, lower real GDP, and higher unemployment"],"a":1,"e":"SRAS shifts left: price up, output down, unemployment up (stagflation). A demand-driven expansion would raise both price and output.","d":3},{"q":"Why is stagflation especially difficult for policy makers?","o":["Both inflation and unemployment can be solved by increasing demand","Supply shocks only affect the long run, so no policy is needed","Boosting demand worsens inflation; cutting it deepens the recession","Stagflation only occurs when the economy is at potential GDP"],"a":2,"e":"Stagflation presents a trade-off: any AD policy improves one problem at the cost of the other. Raising AD cannot fix both at once.","d":3},{"q":"MPC is 0.8. The government cuts taxes by $50 billion. Using the tax multiplier, GDP rises by:","o":["$250 billion","$50 billion","$200 billion","$40 billion"],"a":2,"e":"The tax multiplier is -MPC / (1 - MPC) = -4, so a $50 billion cut raises GDP by $200 billion. $250 billion applies the spending multiplier of 5. $50 billion ignores multiplication, and $40 billion is the first-round spending (0.8 x 50).","d":3},{"q":"Starting from long-run equilibrium, aggregate demand permanently increases. After all adjustments, what is the outcome?","o":["The price level is higher and real GDP is back at potential","The price level is the same and real GDP is permanently higher","Both the price level and real GDP are permanently higher","The price level is lower and real GDP is at potential"],"a":0,"e":"In the short run, output rises above potential. Over time, wages and costs rise, SRAS shifts left, and output returns to potential at a higher price level. LRAS is vertical, so AD does not change long-run output.","d":3},{"q":"Why might recovery from a recessionary gap take years through self-correction alone?","o":["Wages and prices are sticky downward, so SRAS shifts right slowly","Aggregate demand rises automatically when unemployment is high","Potential GDP falls immediately during a recession","Interest rates always rise in a recession"],"a":0,"e":"Contracts, minimum wages, and worker morale resist wage cuts, so costs fall slowly, delaying the SRAS shift. The other statements are not true.","d":3},{"q":"Country A grows at 2% and country B grows at 4%. Both start at $20,000 per person. Using the rule of 70, B's income after 35 years compared with A's will be about:","o":["the same","10% higher","four times as high","twice as high"],"a":3,"e":"A doubles once in 35 years to about $40,000. B doubles every 17.5 years, so twice in 35 years to about $80,000. B ends up about twice A's level.","d":3},{"q":"In practice, the actual spending multiplier is smaller than 1 / (1 - MPC) mainly because of:","o":["a higher MPC than economists had originally expected","firms refusing to accept any additional revenue from buyers","a vertical aggregate demand curve in the short run","taxes, imports, and higher interest rates that crowd out spending"],"a":3,"e":"Taxes, saving, and imports reduce each round of spending, and higher interest rates reduce private investment. A higher MPC would make the multiplier larger, not smaller.","d":3}],"pairs":[["Aggregate demand","Total spending on domestic output"],["LRAS","Vertical line at potential output"],["Recessionary gap","Output below potential, high unemployment"],["Stagflation","Rising prices with falling output"],["Demand-pull inflation","Prices rise from increased aggregate demand"],["Spending multiplier","One divided by one minus MPC"],["Human capital","Workers' education, skills, and health"],["Rule of 70","Seventy over growth rate gives doubling time"]],"traps":["Trap: AD slopes downward for the same reasons as a single product's demand curve. Truth: it slopes down because of the wealth, interest-rate, and exchange-rate effects.","Trap: In the long run, more aggregate demand permanently raises real GDP. Truth: LRAS is vertical, so long-run AD increases only raise the price level.","Trap: Stagflation can be fixed by simply boosting spending. Truth: that raises prices further; stagflation comes from a leftward SRAS shift and policy has a trade-off.","Trap: Spending multiplier equals 1 / MPC. Truth: it equals 1 / (1 - MPC), which is 1 / MPS.","Trap: The gap equals the spending change needed. Truth: the required change equals the gap divided by the multiplier."],"mnemonic":"'Price up, output up: AD ups. Price up, output down: SRAS down.' And multiplier = 1 over MPS: 'save little, multiply a lot.'","cram":["AD slopes down from wealth, interest-rate, and exchange-rate effects; AD equals C+I+G+NX.","SRAS shifts left with higher input costs; LRAS is vertical at potential output.","Recessionary gap: below potential, SRAS shifts right; inflationary gap: above potential, SRAS shifts left.","Demand-pull: AD right. Cost-push: SRAS left. Stagflation: SRAS left, prices up, output down.","Multiplier is one over one minus MPC; spending change equals gap over multiplier.","Tax multiplier is negative MPC over one minus MPC, smaller than the spending multiplier.","Long-run growth: productivity, capital, technology, human capital, institutions.","Rule of 70: years to double equals 70 divided by percent growth."],"ytv":[{"title":"Aggregate Demand- Macro Topic 3.1","channel":"Jacob Clifford","id":"ujiHgvLzEDw","why":"Short, clear explanation of what AD is, why it slopes down, and what shifts it."},{"title":"Productivity and Growth: Crash Course Economics #6","channel":"CrashCourse","id":"UHiUYj5EA0w","why":"Explains productivity, capital, technology, and human capital as sources of long-run growth."},{"title":"Macroeconomics: Crash Course Economics #5","channel":"CrashCourse","id":"d8uTB5XorBw","why":"Big-picture macro overview that frames GDP, inflation, unemployment, and the business cycle used in AD-AS."}]},{"id":"u9","title":"Money, Banking & Monetary Policy","day":5,"summary":"Learn what counts as money, how banks create it, how the Federal Reserve steers interest rates with its tools, and how rate changes flow through to borrowing, investment, and the economy.","lesson":[{"h":"1. What money is and what it does","body":"<p><strong>Money</strong> is anything people generally accept as payment for goods, services, and debts. It does three jobs.</p><ul><li><strong>Medium of exchange:</strong> you sell a pallet of parts for money, then use that money to pay a supplier. No need to find someone who wants exactly what you have (that problem is called the double coincidence of wants under barter).</li><li><strong>Unit of account:</strong> prices, contracts, and financial statements are quoted in dollars, so everything can be compared on one scale.</li><li><strong>Store of value:</strong> money lets you save purchasing power for later. It is an imperfect store because inflation erodes it.</li></ul><p>A fourth job sometimes listed is <strong>standard of deferred payment</strong>: loans and contracts are written in money terms.</p><p>Modern money is <strong>fiat money</strong>: it has value because the government says it is legal tender and people trust it, not because it is backed by gold. <strong>Liquidity</strong> means how quickly and cheaply an asset converts into spending power. Cash is perfectly liquid; a building is not.</p>"},{"h":"2. Measuring money: M1 vs M2","body":"<p>The Federal Reserve counts the money supply in layers, from most liquid to less liquid.</p><ul><li><strong>M1:</strong> currency in circulation plus checkable (demand) deposits. This is money you can spend immediately. (Since 2020 the Fed also counts savings deposits in M1, but the exam idea is the same: M1 is the most liquid layer.)</li><li><strong>M2:</strong> M1 plus near-money: savings deposits, small time deposits (CDs under $100,000), and retail money market funds. These are easy to convert to cash but not spent directly.</li></ul><p>Things that are <em>not</em> money: credit cards (they are a way to borrow, not an asset) and stocks or bonds. Also, your own bank balance counts, but the Treasury's cash and the Fed's reserves held for banks do not count in M1 or M2.</p><p><strong>Manager tip:</strong> when the Fed talks about the money supply, it means these measures, and a rapid rise in M2 can signal future inflation pressure.</p>"},{"h":"3. Banks and how money gets created","body":"<p>Banks run on <strong>fractional reserve banking</strong>: they keep only a fraction of deposits as <strong>reserves</strong> (vault cash plus deposits at the Fed) and lend out the rest. Deposits are the bank's liability; loans and reserves are its assets.</p><p>The <strong>reserve ratio (rr)</strong> is the fraction of deposits held as reserves. If the required reserve ratio is 10%, a bank with $500 million of deposits must hold $50 million. Anything above that is <strong>excess reserves</strong> and can be lent.</p><p><strong>How the chain works:</strong> you deposit $1,000 cash. The bank keeps $100 and lends $900. The borrower spends it, the seller deposits it, and that bank keeps $90 and lends $810, and so on. Each loan creates a new deposit, so the money supply grows.</p><p>The <strong>simple money multiplier = 1 / rr.</strong> With rr = 0.10 the multiplier is 10, so $1,000 of new reserves can support up to $10,000 of total deposits, which is $9,000 of newly created money beyond the original $1,000.</p><p><strong>Why the real multiplier is smaller:</strong> banks hold voluntary excess reserves, and people keep some money as cash instead of depositing it (these are called leakages). A higher rr or more leakage means a smaller multiplier.</p>"},{"h":"4. The Federal Reserve: structure and goals","body":"<p>The Fed is the U.S. central bank. It has a <strong>Board of Governors</strong> in Washington (7 members, 14-year staggered terms, appointed by the President and confirmed by the Senate) and <strong>12 regional Federal Reserve Banks</strong>. Monetary policy is decided by the <strong>FOMC</strong> (Federal Open Market Committee): the 7 governors, the New York Fed president, and 4 rotating regional presidents, all voting.</p><p>Its <strong>dual mandate</strong> from Congress is <strong>maximum employment</strong> and <strong>stable prices</strong> (the Fed defines this as 2% inflation over time). Other jobs: supervise banks, act as lender of last resort, run the payment system.</p><p><strong>Central bank independence:</strong> the Fed makes policy without needing approval from the President or Congress. Reason: politicians are tempted to boost the economy before elections even if it causes inflation later. Independent central banks tend to have lower, more stable inflation.</p><p><strong>Other central banks:</strong> the <strong>European Central Bank (ECB)</strong> sets one monetary policy for the euro area (about 20 countries). Its <em>primary</em> mandate is price stability (also a 2% target); supporting growth and employment comes second. The Bank of England, Bank of Japan, and others play the same role for their currencies.</p>"},{"h":"5. The Fed's tools","body":"<ul><li><strong>Open market operations (OMOs):</strong> buying or selling government securities. <em>Buy</em> bonds: the Fed pays banks with new reserves, reserves rise, rates fall (expansionary). <em>Sell</em> bonds: reserves drain, rates rise (contractionary).</li><li><strong>Discount rate:</strong> the rate the Fed charges banks that borrow directly from it. Lower rate encourages borrowing and signals easing; higher rate does the opposite. It is usually set above the target fed funds rate.</li><li><strong>Interest on reserve balances (IORB):</strong> the rate the Fed pays banks for reserves they leave at the Fed. Raise it and banks will not lend below that rate, so market rates rise. Today this is the main tool for steering the fed funds rate because banks hold abundant reserves.</li><li><strong>Reserve requirements:</strong> the minimum reserve ratio. Changing it alters the multiplier directly, but it is rarely used and has been set to 0% since March 2020.</li><li><strong>Quantitative easing (QE):</strong> large purchases of long-term securities with newly created reserves, used when the short-term rate is already near zero and the Fed still wants to lower long-term rates. The reverse is quantitative tightening.</li></ul><p>The <strong>federal funds rate</strong> is the interest rate banks charge each other for overnight loans of reserves. The FOMC sets a <em>target range</em> for it. It is the benchmark that influences prime rates, mortgage rates, and business loan rates.</p>"},{"h":"6. Policy in action: from rates to the economy","body":"<p><strong>Expansionary (easy) policy</strong> is used in a recession or when unemployment is high. The chain: Fed buys bonds or lowers its rate, so reserves rise, so the fed funds rate and other interest rates fall, so borrowing for investment (equipment, housing) and durable goods rises, so aggregate demand shifts right, raising real GDP and employment (and the price level).</p><p><strong>Contractionary (tight) policy</strong> is used when inflation is too high. The chain reverses: higher rates, less investment and consumption, aggregate demand shifts left, inflation cools, at the cost of slower growth and higher unemployment.</p><p><strong>Business effects:</strong> your floating-rate loan costs more when rates rise; a project's NPV falls because the discount rate rises; a higher U.S. rate also tends to attract foreign capital, strengthening the dollar and hurting exporters (more in the exchange-rate unit).</p><p><strong>Limits:</strong> policy has lags (the effect can take 12-18 months), and in a deep slump firms may not borrow even at near-zero rates (a liquidity-trap problem), which is why QE and forward guidance emerged.</p>"},{"h":"7. Quantity theory and real vs nominal rates","body":"<p><strong>Equation of exchange: M x V = P x Y.</strong> M is money supply, V is velocity (how many times a dollar is spent per year), P is the price level, Y is real output. So P x Y is nominal GDP.</p><p>If velocity and real output are roughly stable, then prices move in proportion to money: growth of M minus growth of Y is about inflation. Example: M grows 6%, V constant, Y grows 2%, then inflation is about 4%. Long-run lesson: <em>too much money chasing too few goods causes inflation.</em> Short-run caution: velocity is not constant, which is why huge QE in 2009-2014 did not cause big inflation (banks sat on excess reserves and velocity fell).</p><p><strong>Fisher equation:</strong> nominal rate = real rate + expected inflation (so real is about nominal minus inflation). A 7% loan with 3% inflation costs about a 4% real rate. Lenders care about real returns; if inflation surprises higher than expected, borrowers with fixed-rate loans win and lenders lose.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Money has three main jobs: it is a medium of exchange, a unit of account, and a store of value.","M1 is the most liquid money: currency and checkable deposits. M2 adds savings deposits, small time deposits, and retail money market funds.","Banks keep only a fraction of deposits as reserves and lend out the rest, which is how the banking system creates money.","The simple money multiplier equals one divided by the reserve ratio. A ten percent reserve ratio gives a multiplier of ten.","The real multiplier is smaller than the simple one because banks hold excess reserves and people hold cash.","The Federal Reserve has a dual mandate: maximum employment and stable prices, which it interprets as two percent inflation.","Independent central banks are shielded from political pressure, which helps keep inflation low and stable.","The Fed's tools are open market operations, the discount rate, interest on reserve balances, reserve requirements, and quantitative easing.","When the Fed buys bonds, reserves rise and the federal funds rate falls. That is expansionary policy. Selling bonds does the opposite.","Lower interest rates raise investment and consumption, shift aggregate demand to the right, and raise output and prices in the short run.","The quantity theory says M times V equals P times Y. With stable velocity and output, faster money growth means higher inflation.","The Fisher equation says the nominal interest rate equals the real rate plus expected inflation."],"examples":[{"q":"The required reserve ratio is 10%. A customer deposits $1,000,000 in cash at a bank. (a) What is the money multiplier? (b) What is the maximum total deposits the system can support from that deposit? (c) How much new money is created beyond the original deposit?","steps":["Money multiplier = 1 / rr = 1 / 0.10 = 10.","Maximum total deposits = 10 x $1,000,000 = $10,000,000.","New money created = $10,000,000 - $1,000,000 = $9,000,000 (these are the newly lent and re-deposited dollars).","Check with the chain: the first bank lends $900,000, the next $810,000, and so on; the loans sum to $900,000 / 0.10 = $9,000,000."],"answer":"(a) 10; (b) $10,000,000; (c) $9,000,000"},{"q":"The Fed buys $50 million of Treasury securities from banks. The reserve ratio is 20%. What is the maximum increase in the money supply? Then, if banks also hold voluntary excess reserves so that the effective reserve ratio is 25%, what is the increase?","steps":["Multiplier at 20% = 1 / 0.20 = 5.","Maximum increase = 5 x $50 million = $250 million.","With an effective ratio of 25%: multiplier = 1 / 0.25 = 4.","Increase = 4 x $50 million = $200 million. Holding extra reserves shrinks the multiplier."],"answer":"$250 million; $200 million"},{"q":"Money supply is $2,000 billion, velocity is 4, and real output is 2,000 (index units). (a) What is nominal GDP and the price level? (b) A lender wants a 3% real return and expects 4% inflation. What nominal rate is needed (approx.)?","steps":["MV = PY, so PY = 2,000 x 4 = 8,000 (nominal GDP).","P = 8,000 / 2,000 = 4.0.","Fisher: nominal = real + expected inflation = 3% + 4% = 7%.","Exact form: (1.03)(1.04) - 1 = 7.12%, close to 7%."],"answer":"(a) nominal GDP 8,000 and P = 4.0; (b) about 7%"}],"videos":[{"title":"Monetary policy and the Federal Reserve explained","query":"monetary policy federal reserve explained open market operations Jacob Clifford"},{"title":"Money multiplier and fractional reserve banking","query":"money multiplier fractional reserve banking explained"},{"title":"Quantity theory of money and Fisher effect","query":"quantity theory of money equation of exchange Fisher equation explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Three functions of money","b":"Medium of exchange, unit of account, store of value."},{"f":"Fiat money","b":"Money valuable because government decrees it legal tender, not backed by a commodity."},{"f":"Liquidity","b":"How quickly and cheaply an asset converts to spending power."},{"f":"M1","b":"Currency plus checkable deposits: the most liquid money."},{"f":"M2","b":"M1 plus savings deposits, small time deposits, and retail money market funds."},{"f":"Fractional reserve banking","b":"Banks keep a fraction of deposits as reserves and lend the rest."},{"f":"Required reserves","b":"Deposits x required reserve ratio."},{"f":"Excess reserves","b":"Reserves held above the required amount; available to lend."},{"f":"Simple money multiplier","b":"1 / reserve ratio."},{"f":"Why the real multiplier is smaller","b":"Banks hold excess reserves and the public holds cash (leakages)."},{"f":"Dual mandate","b":"Maximum employment and stable prices."},{"f":"FOMC","b":"Committee that sets monetary policy: 7 governors, NY Fed president, 4 rotating presidents."},{"f":"Federal funds rate","b":"Rate banks charge each other for overnight reserve loans."},{"f":"Open market operation","b":"Fed buys or sells government securities to change reserves."},{"f":"Discount rate","b":"Rate the Fed charges banks that borrow directly from it."},{"f":"Interest on reserve balances (IORB)","b":"Rate the Fed pays banks on reserves; its main rate-steering tool today."},{"f":"Quantitative easing","b":"Large purchases of long-term securities to lower long-term rates near zero."},{"f":"Expansionary monetary policy","b":"Lower rates and more reserves to fight recession and unemployment."},{"f":"Equation of exchange","b":"M x V = P x Y."},{"f":"Fisher equation","b":"Nominal rate = real rate + expected inflation."},{"f":"Central bank independence","b":"Policy set free of short-term political pressure; supports low inflation."},{"f":"ECB primary mandate","b":"Price stability (2% target) for the euro area."}],"pairs":[["Medium of exchange","Accepted in payment for goods"],["M2","M1 plus near-money savings"],["Reserve ratio","Share of deposits held in reserve"],["Money multiplier","1 divided by reserve ratio"],["Open market operations","Fed buying or selling securities"],["Discount rate","Fed's loan rate to banks"],["Quantitative easing","Buying long-term securities to cut yields"],["Fisher equation","Nominal = real + expected inflation"]],"traps":["Trap: The money multiplier is the reserve ratio itself. Truth: It is the reciprocal, 1 / rr; a 10% ratio gives 10.","Trap: Fed buying bonds takes money out of the economy. Truth: Buying adds reserves and lowers rates; selling drains reserves.","Trap: The discount rate and the federal funds rate are the same. Truth: Discount is Fed-to-bank; fed funds is bank-to-bank overnight.","Trap: A credit card balance is part of M1. Truth: Credit is borrowing, not money; only the paid-off bank deposit counts.","Trap: Higher inflation always means a higher real rate. Truth: Real is approximately nominal minus inflation; higher inflation lowers it."],"mnemonic":"Buy bonds, Bank reserves up, rates down, spending up; Sell bonds, Squeeze: reserves down, rates up. (Multiplier = flip the reserve ratio.)","cram":["Money multiplier = 1 / reserve ratio.","M1 = currency + checkable deposits; M2 adds near-money savings.","Fed buys bonds: reserves rise, fed funds rate falls, expansionary.","Dual mandate: maximum employment and stable prices (2% inflation goal).","Equation of exchange: M x V = P x Y.","Fisher: nominal rate = real rate + expected inflation.","Reserve requirement is 0% since 2020; IORB steers rates.","ECB primary mandate is price stability; Fed's is dual."],"ytv":[{"title":"What is the Federal Reserve? Monetary Policy Explained","channel":"Ryan O'Connell, CFA, FRM","id":"Vu1DV2MmEoM","why":"How the Fed sets rates and moves the money supply"},{"title":"Monetary Policy, part 1 of 4: The Fed and the Dual Mandate","channel":"Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis","id":"vHurDbCAfkA","why":"The Fed's own explanation of its dual mandate"}],"questions":[{"q":"A catalog lists every product in dollars so buyers can compare a $40 item with a $60 item. Which function of money is this?","o":["Unit of account","Medium of exchange","Store of value","Standard of liquidity"],"a":0,"e":"Quoting all prices in one currency is the unit-of-account function. Medium of exchange is tempting, but that is about actually using money to complete a transaction, not about measuring and comparing prices.","d":1},{"q":"Which of the following is included in M2 but NOT in M1 (using the traditional definitions)?","o":["Paper currency in circulation","A business checking account balance at a bank","A 12-month certificate of deposit of $5,000","Traveler's checks held by the general public"],"a":2,"e":"Small time deposits such as CDs are near-money in M2. Currency and checking balances are the most liquid and are already in M1, so they are not the extra pieces M2 adds.","d":1},{"q":"If the required reserve ratio is 20%, the simple money multiplier is:","o":["20","5","0.2","80"],"a":1,"e":"Multiplier = 1 / rr = 1 / 0.20 = 5. Choosing 0.2 or 20 just repeats the ratio without taking the reciprocal.","d":1},{"q":"The Federal Reserve's dual mandate consists of:","o":["Maximum employment and stable prices","Low taxes and balanced budgets","Stable exchange rates and trade surpluses","Economic growth and low interest rates"],"a":0,"e":"Congress directed the Fed to pursue maximum employment and stable prices. Low interest rates are a tool, not a goal, so the growth-and-low-rates option mixes up means and ends.","d":1},{"q":"To lower interest rates and stimulate a weak economy, the Fed would most likely:","o":["Buy government securities in the open market","Sell government securities in the open market","Raise the discount rate","Raise the reserve requirement"],"a":0,"e":"Buying securities puts new reserves in banks, pushing the federal funds rate down. Selling securities, a higher discount rate, or higher reserve requirements all tighten policy.","d":1},{"q":"A bond pays a nominal rate of 8% and expected inflation is 3%. The approximate real interest rate is:","o":["11%","3%","8%","5%"],"a":3,"e":"Fisher: real is about nominal minus inflation = 8% - 3% = 5%. Adding them (11%) goes in the wrong direction.","d":1},{"q":"The main goal of contractionary monetary policy is to:","o":["Reduce unemployment","Reduce inflationary pressure","Increase exports directly","Raise government revenue"],"a":1,"e":"Tight policy raises rates and slows demand to cool inflation. Reducing unemployment is the goal of expansionary policy, which is the tempting opposite.","d":1},{"q":"The federal funds rate is the rate at which:","o":["The Fed lends to banks in emergencies","The Treasury borrows from the public","Banks lend to their best business customers","Banks lend reserves to each other overnight"],"a":3,"e":"It is the interbank overnight rate. The rate the Fed charges banks directly is the discount rate, and the best-customer rate is the prime rate.","d":1},{"q":"The Fed buys $30 million of securities from banks and the reserve ratio is 10%. The maximum possible increase in the money supply is:","o":["$30 million","$3 million","$300 million","$270 million"],"a":2,"e":"Multiplier = 10, so 10 x $30M = $300M. The $270M answer subtracts the original amount, which would apply only if the initial injection were a deposit already counted in the money supply.","d":2},{"q":"A bank has $500 million in deposits, reserves of $80 million, and a 10% required reserve ratio. The most it can newly lend on its own is:","o":["$80 million","$50 million","$450 million","$30 million"],"a":3,"e":"Required reserves = 10% x $500M = $50M. Excess = $80M - $50M = $30M, which is what it can lend. The full $80M of reserves is not lendable because $50M is required.","d":2},{"q":"Which chain best describes how easy monetary policy raises output in the short run?","o":["Lower interest rates, less saving, aggregate supply shifts left","Higher interest rates, more saving and less borrowing, aggregate demand shifts right","Lower interest rates, more investment and spending, aggregate demand shifts right","Lower taxes, higher interest rates, aggregate demand shifts left"],"a":2,"e":"Cheaper credit encourages investment and durable purchases, shifting AD right. Higher rates do not raise investment, and monetary policy works through interest rates, not tax changes.","d":2},{"q":"Why is the actual money multiplier usually smaller than 1 / rr?","o":["Banks hold excess reserves and households keep some cash outside banks","Banks are legally forbidden to lend more than half their deposits","The Fed removes money created by lending","Loans never get redeposited in banks"],"a":0,"e":"Leakages from excess reserves and cash holdings break the lend-and-redeposit chain. The other options invent rules or claim redeposits never happen, but most spending does return as deposits.","d":2},{"q":"The money supply is 2,000, velocity is 4, and real output is 2,000. According to the equation of exchange, the price level is:","o":["4","2","8","0.25"],"a":0,"e":"PY = MV = 8,000, so P = 8,000 / 2,000 = 4. Answer 8 forgets to divide by real output.","d":2},{"q":"Money supply grows 6% a year, velocity is constant, and real output grows 2%. The quantity theory predicts inflation of about:","o":["8%","4%","6%","2%"],"a":1,"e":"In growth terms: %M + %V = %P + %Y, so %P = 6 + 0 - 2 = 4%. Adding output growth (8%) has the sign backwards, and 6% ignores that extra output absorbs some of the money.","d":2},{"q":"The strongest argument for central bank independence is that it:","o":["Guarantees interest rates never rise","Lets the central bank ignore inflation when unemployment is high","Prevents policy being steered by short-term election-year pressures","Allows the President to set interest rates directly"],"a":2,"e":"Independence protects against political pressure for easy money that causes inflation later. It does not guarantee anything about rates, and giving the President control is the opposite of independence.","d":2},{"q":"A rise in the discount rate is most likely to:","o":["Increase excess reserves held across the banking system","Lower the federal funds rate within a few days","Discourage bank borrowing from the Fed and signal tightening","Raise the money multiplier for all banks"],"a":2,"e":"A costlier Fed loan means fewer borrowed reserves and tighter conditions. It does not lower the fed funds rate or raise the multiplier.","d":2},{"q":"The Fed conducts quantitative easing mainly when:","o":["Inflation is far above target","Short-term rates are near zero and it wants lower long-term rates","The dollar is too weak","Banks hold too few excess reserves because required reserves are too high"],"a":1,"e":"QE buys long-term securities to push down long-term yields once the policy rate cannot go lower. Rising inflation calls for tightening, which is the opposite action.","d":2},{"q":"A firm's loan is priced at the prime rate plus 2%. The Fed raises its target federal funds rate by 0.75 percentage points. What is the likely effect on the firm?","o":["Its borrowing cost rises, so marginal capital projects may be postponed","Its borrowing cost falls because the Fed is helping banks","Nothing, because loan rates are set only by the firm's credit score","Its loan principal automatically shrinks"],"a":0,"e":"Prime moves with the fed funds target, so a floating-rate loan costs more and projects with thin returns may fail the hurdle. Credit score affects the spread, not the base rate.","d":2},{"q":"A lender requires a 3% real return and expects 4% inflation. The nominal rate it should charge is about:","o":["1%","7%","3%","12%"],"a":1,"e":"Fisher: nominal = real + expected inflation = 3% + 4% = 7%. Subtracting (1%) mistakes the direction of the equation.","d":2},{"q":"If the Fed raises the interest rate it pays on reserves held at the Fed, the likely effect is:","o":["Banks lend more because holding reserves is now costly","Banks lend less below that rate, so market rates rise","The money multiplier rises as banks lend more","Banks must raise the reserves they are required to hold"],"a":1,"e":"Paying more on reserves creates a floor: why lend at 4% if the Fed pays 4.4% risk-free? Lending falls and rates rise. It does not change the legal reserve requirement.","d":2},{"q":"The FOMC announces it will SELL government securities. Bank reserves and the federal funds rate will most likely:","o":["Reserves rise; the rate rises","Reserves fall; the rate falls","Reserves fall; the rate rises","Reserves rise; the rate falls"],"a":2,"e":"Banks pay the Fed with reserves, so reserves drop and the scarce overnight funds cost more. Pairing falling reserves with a falling rate confuses selling with buying.","d":2},{"q":"Inflation is running at 5% against a 2% target, and unemployment is low. Which FOMC decision best fits the dual mandate?","o":["Cut the target federal funds rate range","Lower the reserve requirement further","Raise the target federal funds rate range","Buy long-term bonds through quantitative easing"],"a":2,"e":"With inflation too high and the job market tight, the Fed tightens by raising the target rate. Cutting rates or buying bonds adds stimulus, which would push inflation higher.","d":3},{"q":"Banks hold the 10% required reserves plus 5% voluntary excess reserves, and the Fed injects $90 million of new reserves. The maximum increase in deposits is:","o":["$900 million","$450 million","$1,350 million","$600 million"],"a":3,"e":"Effective reserve ratio = 15%, multiplier = 1 / 0.15 = 6.67, so $90M x 6.67 = $600M. Using 10% alone ($900M) ignores banks' voluntary holdings.","d":3},{"q":"A firm took a fixed 6% loan expecting 3% inflation (expected real rate 3%). Inflation turns out to be only 1%. Compared with expectations, the firm:","o":["Is worse off because the real rate it pays is 5%, not 3%","Is better off because the real rate it pays fell to 1%, below the expected 3%","Is unaffected because the loan is fixed","Is better off because the nominal rate it pays rose above the fixed 6%"],"a":0,"e":"Ex post real = 6% - 1% = 5%, higher than expected, a transfer from borrower to lender. A fixed nominal rate does not protect against surprises in the real burden.","d":3},{"q":"Reserve requirements have been 0% since March 2020. Which tool is now the main way the Fed steers the federal funds rate?","o":["Raising the reserve requirement ratio back to its prior level","Interest on reserve balances and the reverse repo rate","Large-scale daily open market bond sales","Changing the tax rate on bank profits"],"a":1,"e":"With abundant reserves, the Fed sets administered rates to create a floor. Reserve requirements are unused and small daily bond trades no longer drive the rate.","d":3},{"q":"Which statement correctly contrasts the Fed with the European Central Bank (ECB)?","o":["The ECB has a dual mandate of jobs and prices, while the Fed targets only inflation","The ECB's primary mandate is price stability, while the Fed has a dual mandate","Both report to and take orders from their national finance ministers","The ECB sets policy for the U.S. dollar area too"],"a":1,"e":"The ECB must put price stability first and support other goals only if consistent; the Fed's mandate is explicitly dual. Neither takes orders from the finance ministry, and the ECB covers only the euro area.","d":3},{"q":"A U.S. exporter notes that the Fed has raised rates sharply while other central banks held steady. What is the likely effect on the exporter?","o":["The dollar weakens, making exports cheaper for foreign buyers","Exports are unaffected because monetary policy is purely domestic","Foreign demand rises because U.S. firms pay more interest","Capital flows in, the dollar appreciates, and U.S. goods cost more abroad"],"a":3,"e":"Higher relative returns raise demand for dollars, so the dollar appreciates and hurts export competitiveness. Monetary policy has international effects through exchange rates.","d":3},{"q":"The Fed raises rates today. Why is there a risk of overshooting and causing an unnecessary recession?","o":["Rate increases take effect instantly, then fade completely within about a month","The Fed cannot observe interest rates, so it must set them blindly each quarter","Higher rates always lower velocity to zero","Policy works with long lags, so the full effect appears 12-18 months later"],"a":3,"e":"Because impact is delayed, policymakers may keep tightening while earlier increases are still working. The instant-effect option has the timing backwards.","d":3},{"q":"After 2008 the Fed's balance sheet and bank reserves rose enormously, yet inflation stayed low for years. Which explanation fits the quantity theory?","o":["Banks held the new reserves as excess reserves and velocity fell","Velocity is constant by law, so a rise in reserves could never matter","Money supply is unrelated to prices over any horizon, short or long","Real output fell by the same percentage as prices"],"a":0,"e":"MV = PY: if V drops while M rises, PY need not rise. Much of the new base money sat idle instead of being lent. The constant-velocity assumption is exactly what failed.","d":3},{"q":"Reserve ratio is 10%. A customer deposits $1,000 in cash and the banking system lends out all excess reserves at every round. The total NEW loans made across the system equal:","o":["$10,000","$1,000","$900","$9,000"],"a":3,"e":"Total deposits = $1,000 x 10 = $10,000, but only $9,000 of that comes from new lending; the original $1,000 was the deposit itself. $10,000 is the total deposits, and $900 is just the first bank's loan.","d":3}]},{"id":"u10","title":"Fiscal Policy & Government Finance","day":5,"summary":"Learn how government spending and taxes steer the economy, how multipliers amplify them, why deficits and debt are different, and when fiscal policy works better or worse than monetary policy.","lesson":[{"h":"1. What fiscal policy is","body":"<p><strong>Fiscal policy</strong> is the government's use of <strong>spending (G)</strong> and <strong>taxes (T)</strong> to influence the economy. Congress and the President control it (unlike monetary policy, which the independent Fed controls).</p><ul><li><strong>Government spending:</strong> buying goods and services (roads, defense, salaries). This counts directly in GDP as G.</li><li><strong>Transfer payments:</strong> Social Security, unemployment benefits, and similar payments. They are <em>not</em> counted in G because nothing is produced in return; they work through households' spending.</li><li><strong>Taxes:</strong> income, payroll, corporate, and sales taxes. They reduce the income households and firms have left to spend.</li></ul><p>The <strong>budget balance</strong> is T minus G (including transfers). If T &gt; G there is a <strong>surplus</strong>; if G &gt; T there is a <strong>deficit</strong>; if equal, the budget is balanced.</p><p><strong>Business link:</strong> a defense contractor or highway builder feels spending changes directly; a retailer feels tax changes through customers' take-home pay.</p>"},{"h":"2. Expansionary vs contractionary fiscal policy","body":"<p><strong>Expansionary fiscal policy</strong> fights a recession: <em>raise G, cut taxes, or raise transfers.</em> It shifts aggregate demand to the right, raising real GDP and lowering unemployment (and pushing prices up somewhat). It usually increases the deficit.</p><p><strong>Contractionary fiscal policy</strong> fights inflation from an overheated economy: <em>cut G, raise taxes, or cut transfers.</em> It shifts AD left, cooling prices and slowing growth. It usually reduces the deficit.</p><p>Quick test: ask 'does this put more money into spending or take it out?' Spending increases and tax cuts add demand; the reverse removes it.</p><p><strong>Where to aim it:</strong> if the economy is far below full employment, expansion mostly raises output. Near full employment, the same stimulus mostly raises prices (inflation) because there are no idle resources.</p>"},{"h":"3. Multipliers: why a dollar of spending can create more than a dollar of GDP","body":"<p>When the government spends $1, that dollar becomes someone's income. They spend part of it, which becomes someone else's income, and so on. How much is re-spent depends on the <strong>marginal propensity to consume (MPC)</strong>: the fraction of each extra dollar of income that is spent. <strong>MPS = 1 - MPC</strong> is the fraction saved.</p><ul><li><strong>Spending multiplier = 1 / (1 - MPC) = 1 / MPS.</strong></li><li><strong>Tax multiplier = - MPC / (1 - MPC).</strong> It is negative because higher taxes reduce GDP (and a tax cut raises it).</li><li><strong>Balanced-budget multiplier = 1.</strong> Raise G and T by the same amount: spending multiplier + tax multiplier = 1/(1-MPC) - MPC/(1-MPC) = 1. GDP rises by exactly the amount of the change.</li></ul><p><strong>Example, MPC = 0.8:</strong> spending multiplier = 5, tax multiplier = -4. A $100 billion spending increase adds $500 billion to GDP; a $100 billion tax cut adds $400 billion.</p><p><strong>Why is the tax multiplier smaller?</strong> Government spending is a full dollar of demand in round one. A tax cut first lands in households' pockets, and they save part of it (MPS), so only the MPC share is spent in round one. Transfers behave like tax cuts.</p><p><strong>Reality check:</strong> these textbook multipliers ignore imports, income taxes, crowding out, and inflation, so real-world multipliers are smaller (often 0.5 to 2).</p>"},{"h":"4. Automatic stabilizers, discretionary policy, and lags","body":"<p><strong>Automatic stabilizers</strong> are built into the system and change the budget without any new law. In a recession, tax revenue falls (progressive income taxes take less as incomes drop) and unemployment benefits and other transfers rise, which cushions demand. In a boom, the opposite happens and taxes rise faster than income, cooling demand. They act immediately and without politics.</p><p><strong>Discretionary fiscal policy</strong> requires deliberate new legislation, such as a stimulus bill or a tax law. It can be larger and targeted but is slow.</p><p>Policy <strong>lags</strong> are fiscal policy's main weakness:</p><ol><li><strong>Recognition lag:</strong> time to notice the problem.</li><li><strong>Decision (legislative) lag:</strong> time for Congress to agree. This is the long one for fiscal policy.</li><li><strong>Implementation lag:</strong> time to start spending (hiring contractors, building).</li><li><strong>Impact lag:</strong> time for the effects to flow through the economy.</li></ol><p>Danger: by the time stimulus arrives, the recession may be over, so the policy adds fuel to a recovery (procyclical rather than stabilizing).</p>"},{"h":"5. Deficits, debt, and crowding out","body":"<p>A <strong>deficit</strong> is a one-year flow: spending minus revenue in a year. The <strong>national debt</strong> is the accumulated stock of past deficits (minus surpluses). Each year's deficit adds to the debt: debt of $30 trillion plus a $1.5 trillion deficit becomes $31.5 trillion.</p><p>Analysts judge sustainability with <strong>debt-to-GDP</strong> (debt divided by GDP): $31.5 trillion of debt with GDP of $28 trillion is 112.5%. The debt is manageable if the economy grows as fast as or faster than the debt, and interest rates are not too high. Rising interest costs can crowd out other spending.</p><p><strong>Crowding out:</strong> when the government borrows heavily, it competes with firms for loanable funds, which can push interest rates up and reduce private investment. This offsets part of the stimulus. It is weaker when the economy is in a deep slump with idle savings.</p><p><strong>Ricardian equivalence (intro):</strong> if households are forward-looking, they know today's tax cut financed by borrowing means higher taxes later, so they save the tax cut instead of spending it. Then the tax multiplier is near zero. Evidence says this holds only partly, since many households are cash-constrained and spend extra income.</p>"},{"h":"6. Supply-side policy and the Laffer curve","body":"<p><strong>Supply-side policy</strong> aims to shift <em>aggregate supply</em> to the right, increasing the economy's potential output rather than pumping demand. Tools: lower marginal tax rates (to reward work and investment), investment tax credits, deregulation, and spending on infrastructure and education.</p><p>The <strong>Laffer curve</strong> shows tax revenue against the tax rate. At a 0% rate revenue is zero. At a 100% rate nobody would work in the taxed economy, so revenue is also near zero. So revenue rises, peaks, then falls. On the downward side (the 'prohibitive' range), cutting the rate can actually raise revenue.</p><p><strong>Example:</strong> at a 30% rate on a $1,000 billion base, revenue = $300 billion. Raise the rate to 40% and the base shrinks to $700 billion, revenue = $280 billion, which is lower. That is the prohibitive side.</p><p><strong>Caution:</strong> most estimates say that the U.S. is on the left side of the peak, so broad tax cuts reduce revenue overall, even though they may raise growth somewhat.</p>"},{"h":"7. Fiscal vs monetary policy","body":"<p>Both aim to stabilize output and prices, but they differ.</p><ul><li><strong>Who decides:</strong> fiscal = Congress and the President (political); monetary = the independent central bank.</li><li><strong>Speed:</strong> monetary policy can change within a day (the decision lag is short), though its effect takes months. Fiscal policy has a long decision lag but, once enacted, direct spending hits quickly.</li><li><strong>Targeting:</strong> fiscal policy can aim at specific groups or sectors (infrastructure, low-income households). Monetary policy is a blunt, economy-wide tool through interest rates.</li><li><strong>Side effects:</strong> fiscal stimulus raises the deficit and debt and can crowd out investment; monetary easing can fuel inflation or asset bubbles and weakens the currency.</li><li><strong>Near-zero rates:</strong> when interest rates cannot fall further, fiscal policy becomes the stronger tool.</li></ul><p><strong>Policy mix:</strong> large deficits combined with tight money (as in the early 1980s) pushed interest rates and the dollar up, hurting exporters and rate-sensitive industries. Coordinated easing, as in 2020, works in the same direction.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["Fiscal policy is the government's use of spending and taxes to influence the economy. Congress and the President control it.","Expansionary fiscal policy raises government spending or cuts taxes. It shifts aggregate demand right and usually increases the deficit.","Contractionary fiscal policy cuts spending or raises taxes to cool inflation.","The spending multiplier is one divided by one minus the marginal propensity to consume. With an MPC of zero point eight, it equals five.","The tax multiplier is negative MPC divided by one minus MPC. It is smaller in size than the spending multiplier because households save part of a tax cut.","The balanced budget multiplier equals one. Raising spending and taxes by the same amount raises GDP by that same amount.","Automatic stabilizers, like progressive taxes and unemployment benefits, work without new laws. Discretionary policy needs legislation and suffers from lags.","A deficit is a one year shortfall. The debt is the accumulated total of past deficits.","Crowding out happens when government borrowing pushes up interest rates and reduces private investment.","Ricardian equivalence says forward looking households save a debt financed tax cut because they expect higher taxes later.","Supply side policy tries to shift aggregate supply to the right with lower marginal tax rates and investment incentives.","The Laffer curve says tax revenue rises, peaks, and then falls as the tax rate increases toward one hundred percent."],"examples":[{"q":"The MPC is 0.8. Compute the change in GDP from (a) a $100 billion increase in government spending, (b) a $100 billion tax cut, and (c) a $100 billion increase in both G and taxes (balanced budget).","steps":["Spending multiplier = 1 / (1 - 0.8) = 5. Tax multiplier = -0.8 / 0.2 = -4.","(a) 5 x $100B = +$500 billion.","(b) A tax cut is -$100B in taxes, so -4 x (-$100B) = +$400 billion.","(c) +$500B from G plus -4 x (+$100B) = -$400B from taxes = +$100 billion, which equals a multiplier of 1."],"answer":"(a) +$500B; (b) +$400B; (c) +$100B"},{"q":"An economy is $400 billion below potential GDP. The MPC is 0.75. How much must the government (a) raise spending, or (b) cut taxes, to close the gap using the simple multipliers?","steps":["MPC = 0.75, so spending multiplier = 1 / 0.25 = 4. Tax multiplier = -0.75 / 0.25 = -3.","(a) Change in G = gap / multiplier = $400B / 4 = $100 billion.","(b) Change in taxes = $400B / (-3) = -$133.33B, so a tax cut of about $133.3 billion.","A tax cut must be larger than the spending increase because part of it is saved."],"answer":"(a) +$100B in spending; (b) about $133.3B tax cut"},{"q":"Federal revenue is $4.5 trillion and spending is $6.0 trillion. The debt at the start of the year is $30 trillion and GDP is $28 trillion. Find the deficit, ending debt (ignoring other changes), ending debt-to-GDP, and the deficit as a percent of GDP.","steps":["Deficit = 6.0 - 4.5 = $1.5 trillion.","Ending debt = 30 + 1.5 = $31.5 trillion.","Debt-to-GDP = 31.5 / 28 = 1.125 = 112.5%.","Deficit-to-GDP = 1.5 / 28 = 5.36%."],"answer":"Deficit $1.5T; debt $31.5T; debt/GDP 112.5%; deficit/GDP about 5.4%"}],"videos":[{"title":"Fiscal policy and the multiplier explained","query":"fiscal policy multiplier explained government spending tax multiplier"},{"title":"Crowding out and Ricardian equivalence","query":"crowding out effect Ricardian equivalence explained"},{"title":"Automatic stabilizers vs discretionary fiscal policy","query":"automatic stabilizers discretionary fiscal policy lags explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Fiscal policy","b":"Government use of spending and taxes to steer the economy."},{"f":"Expansionary fiscal policy","b":"Raise G or cut taxes to fight recession."},{"f":"Contractionary fiscal policy","b":"Cut G or raise taxes to fight inflation."},{"f":"Transfer payment","b":"Payment with no good produced in return; not part of G."},{"f":"MPC","b":"Fraction of an extra dollar of income that is spent."},{"f":"MPS","b":"1 - MPC: fraction of extra income saved."},{"f":"Spending multiplier","b":"1 / (1 - MPC)."},{"f":"Tax multiplier","b":"-MPC / (1 - MPC)."},{"f":"Balanced-budget multiplier","b":"Equals 1: equal rise in G and T raises GDP by that amount."},{"f":"Why tax multiplier is smaller","b":"Households save part of a tax change, so less is spent."},{"f":"Automatic stabilizer","b":"Built-in tax or transfer change that cushions swings without new law."},{"f":"Discretionary fiscal policy","b":"Deliberate new laws changing spending or taxes."},{"f":"Recognition lag","b":"Time taken to realize a problem exists."},{"f":"Decision lag","b":"Time for legislators to agree and pass policy."},{"f":"Budget deficit","b":"Annual shortfall when spending exceeds revenue."},{"f":"National debt","b":"Accumulated total of past deficits net of surpluses."},{"f":"Debt-to-GDP ratio","b":"Debt divided by GDP; measures burden relative to economy size."},{"f":"Crowding out","b":"Government borrowing raises rates and reduces private investment."},{"f":"Ricardian equivalence","b":"Households save tax cuts anticipating future taxes to repay debt."},{"f":"Supply-side policy","b":"Policies, like lower marginal taxes, that raise potential output."},{"f":"Laffer curve","b":"Tax revenue rises, peaks, then falls as tax rate increases."}],"pairs":[["Fiscal policy","Government spending and taxation choices"],["Spending multiplier","1 divided by (1 - MPC)"],["Tax multiplier","Negative MPC over (1 - MPC)"],["Automatic stabilizer","Built-in tax or benefit response"],["Deficit","One-year spending minus revenue"],["Crowding out","Public borrowing displaces private investment"],["Ricardian equivalence","Tax cut saved; future taxes expected"],["Laffer curve","Revenue peaks at intermediate tax rate"]],"traps":["Trap: A deficit and the national debt are the same thing. Truth: Deficit is one year's gap; debt is the accumulated total.","Trap: The tax multiplier equals the spending multiplier. Truth: It is smaller (MPC/(1-MPC)) and negative, because part of a tax change is saved.","Trap: Transfer payments are part of government spending G in GDP. Truth: Transfers are excluded from G; they work through household spending.","Trap: Automatic stabilizers need Congress to act. Truth: They change automatically with income and unemployment, with no new law.","Trap: Cutting taxes always raises revenue. Truth: Only on the prohibitive side of the Laffer curve; usually revenue falls."],"mnemonic":"Spending is a Full dollar, a tax cut is a Fraction: multiplier = 1 over MPS; tax multiplier = MPC over MPS, and balanced budget = 1.","cram":["Spending multiplier 1/(1-MPC); tax multiplier -MPC/(1-MPC).","Balanced-budget multiplier equals 1.","Deficit is annual flow; debt is accumulated stock.","Automatic stabilizers act without new laws; discretionary needs legislation.","Fiscal lags: recognition, decision, implementation, impact.","Crowding out: public borrowing raises rates, reduces private investment.","Laffer curve: revenue peaks at an intermediate tax rate.","Ricardian equivalence: households save tax cuts to pay future taxes."],"ytv":[{"title":"The Multiplier Effect- Macro Topic 3.2","channel":"Jacob Clifford","id":"RqWYmQQzXxs","why":"Why $1 of spending creates more than $1 of GDP"},{"title":"The Spending Multiplier & Tax Multiplier Explained (Step-by-Step)","channel":"Econbusters","id":"sQ1s7b7ewNM","why":"Worked spending vs tax multiplier problems"},{"title":"Explaining the Fiscal Multiplier - A Level and IB Economics","channel":"tutor2u","id":"v26MS8VTeCc","why":"Fiscal multiplier with a clear diagram"}],"questions":[{"q":"Which pair of tools makes up fiscal policy?","o":["Interest rates and the money supply","Government spending and taxation","Tariffs and quotas","Exchange rates and reserve requirements"],"a":1,"e":"Fiscal policy is spending and taxes set by Congress and the President. Interest rates and money supply belong to monetary policy, and tariffs are trade policy.","d":1},{"q":"Which action is expansionary fiscal policy?","o":["Increasing infrastructure spending","Raising the income tax rate","Selling Treasury bonds to reduce reserves","Raising the discount rate"],"a":0,"e":"More government purchases add to demand. A tax increase takes demand out, and the last two options are monetary policy actions.","d":1},{"q":"If the MPC is 0.8, the simple spending multiplier is:","o":["4","5","0.8","1.25"],"a":1,"e":"1 / (1 - 0.8) = 1 / 0.2 = 5. Four is the size of the tax multiplier, and 1.25 is the reciprocal of the MPC rather than of the MPS.","d":1},{"q":"Which of these is an automatic stabilizer?","o":["A stimulus bill passed by Congress during a recession","A new infrastructure program announced by the President","A Fed decision to cut interest rates","Unemployment insurance payments that rise during a recession"],"a":3,"e":"Unemployment benefits rise automatically without a new law. A stimulus bill or new program is discretionary, and the Fed's action is monetary policy.","d":1},{"q":"A government spends $5.0 trillion and collects $4.2 trillion in revenue this year. This is:","o":["A surplus of $0.8 trillion","A national debt of $0.8 trillion","A deficit of $0.8 trillion","A balanced budget"],"a":2,"e":"Spending exceeds revenue by $0.8T, which is the deficit. The debt is the accumulated total over many years, not one year's gap.","d":1},{"q":"The national debt is best described as:","o":["The accumulated total of past deficits less surpluses","The deficit in a single year","The value of all government spending and transfers in one year","The trade deficit with other countries"],"a":0,"e":"Debt is a stock built up over time. A single year's shortfall is the deficit, which is the common confusion.","d":1},{"q":"Which policy is most appropriate when the economy is overheating and inflation is rising?","o":["Cut taxes and raise transfers","Increase government purchases","Run larger deficits","Cut government spending or raise taxes"],"a":3,"e":"Contractionary fiscal policy removes demand to cool prices. The other options add demand and would worsen inflation.","d":1},{"q":"'Crowding out' refers to:","o":["Foreign firms replacing domestic firms in local markets","Taxes pushing firms to relocate their plants abroad","Public borrowing that raises rates and cuts private investment","The Fed buying bonds from private banks for reserves"],"a":2,"e":"Heavy public borrowing competes for loanable funds and can raise rates, reducing business investment. It is not about foreign competition or Fed bond purchases.","d":1},{"q":"The MPC is 0.75. Government spending rises by $80 billion. The maximum increase in GDP from the simple multiplier is:","o":["$240 billion","$80 billion","$106.7 billion","$320 billion"],"a":3,"e":"Multiplier = 1 / 0.25 = 4, so 4 x $80B = $320B. $240B uses the tax multiplier (3), which applies to tax changes, not spending.","d":2},{"q":"The MPC is 0.9. Taxes are cut by $50 billion. The simple tax multiplier implies GDP rises by:","o":["$450 billion","$500 billion","$45 billion","$55.6 billion"],"a":0,"e":"Tax multiplier = -0.9 / 0.1 = -9; a $50B cut gives +$450B. $500B applies the spending multiplier (10), which overstates a tax cut.","d":2},{"q":"The tax multiplier is smaller in size than the spending multiplier mainly because:","o":["Taxes are collected more slowly than spending occurs, delaying the full effect","Households save part of a tax cut, so less is spent in the first round","Government spending never leaks into savings","Tax changes affect only firms, not households"],"a":1,"e":"A tax cut raises disposable income, but the saved share (MPS) does not enter demand in round one. Spending injects the full amount directly.","d":2},{"q":"Government raises both spending and taxes by $60 billion. According to the balanced-budget multiplier, GDP changes by:","o":["+$300 billion","+$60 billion","0","-$60 billion"],"a":1,"e":"Spending effect plus tax effect = 1/(1-MPC) - MPC/(1-MPC) = 1, so GDP rises by the $60B. Zero would be the answer only if the tax and spending effects had equal sizes, but spending has the larger effect.","d":2},{"q":"During a recession, tax revenue falls and unemployment benefits rise without any new law. This illustrates:","o":["Discretionary fiscal policy","Automatic stabilizers","Crowding out","Supply-side policy"],"a":1,"e":"Built-in changes that cushion demand are automatic stabilizers. Discretionary policy needs legislation, which has not happened here.","d":2},{"q":"Congress passes a stimulus package six months after a recession has already ended. This is an example of a problem with:","o":["Fiscal policy lags","Automatic stabilizers","The Laffer curve","Ricardian equivalence"],"a":0,"e":"Recognition, decision, and implementation lags can make stimulus arrive too late and add to a recovery that has already begun. Stabilizers, by contrast, act immediately.","d":2},{"q":"A country has debt of $36 trillion and GDP of $30 trillion. Its debt-to-GDP ratio is:","o":["83%","6 trillion","1.2 trillion","120%"],"a":3,"e":"36 / 30 = 1.20 = 120%. 83% flips the fraction (30/36), and the other options are not ratios.","d":2},{"q":"Ricardian equivalence suggests that a deficit-financed tax cut will:","o":["Be spent fully because households ignore the government budget","Always reduce interest rates","Be largely saved because households expect higher future taxes","Increase tax revenue immediately"],"a":2,"e":"Forward-looking households save to pay the future tax bill, so consumption barely changes. The fully spent outcome assumes households do not look ahead.","d":2},{"q":"On the downward-sloping (prohibitive) part of the Laffer curve, cutting the tax rate would:","o":["Decrease tax revenue","Leave revenue unchanged","Increase tax revenue","Always create a surplus"],"a":2,"e":"Past the revenue peak, a high rate shrinks the tax base so much that a lower rate yields more revenue. Most empirical estimates place the U.S. left of the peak, but this question concerns the prohibitive range.","d":2},{"q":"A supply-side economist would most likely recommend:","o":["Higher government transfers to boost consumer demand in recessions","Higher reserve requirements","Lower marginal tax rates to encourage work and investment","Larger tariffs on imports to shield domestic producers from competition"],"a":2,"e":"Supply-side policy aims to expand potential output with incentives. Transfers work on demand, and the other options are monetary or trade tools.","d":2},{"q":"Which is a genuine advantage of fiscal policy over monetary policy?","o":["It is always faster to enact than rate changes","It never adds to the national debt when spending is well planned","It is insulated from politics and lobbying","It can target specific sectors or groups of households"],"a":3,"e":"Fiscal policy can aim spending at specific needs, while rate changes are economy-wide. It is slower to enact and highly political, and deficits raise debt.","d":2},{"q":"Congress approves a temporary 50% bonus depreciation for new equipment. What is the most likely effect on a manufacturer's capital budgeting?","o":["Higher after-tax returns, so more equipment purchases are approved","Lower after-tax returns, so planned equipment purchases are cancelled","No effect on decisions because depreciation is only a non-cash accounting item","Higher interest rates on all loans"],"a":0,"e":"Faster depreciation deductions cut early-year taxes and raise the NPV of equipment purchases. Non-cash does not mean irrelevant, because the tax shield has real cash value.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following is counted directly as government spending (G) in GDP?","o":["A federal purchase of road-building services","A monthly Social Security payment to a retiree","A tax refund sent to households","Interest paid to bondholders abroad"],"a":0,"e":"G counts purchases of currently produced goods and services. Social Security, refunds, and interest are transfers or payments with no matching production, so they affect GDP only through later private spending.","d":2},{"q":"The MPC is 0.6. Government raises spending by $50 billion and, at the same time, cuts taxes by $20 billion. Using the simple multipliers, GDP rises by:","o":["$125 billion","$155 billion","$170 billion","$70 billion"],"a":1,"e":"Spending: 2.5 x 50 = 125. Tax cut: 1.5 x 20 = 30. Total = 155. $125B ignores the tax cut, and $70B just adds the dollar amounts without applying multipliers.","d":3},{"q":"The MPC is 0.75 and real GDP is $400 billion below potential. The government wants to close the gap by raising spending alone. How much must it raise G (simple multiplier)?","o":["$400 billion","$100 billion","$133 billion","$300 billion"],"a":1,"e":"Multiplier = 4, so $400B / 4 = $100B. $133B is the tax-cut size for the same gap, and $300B misuses the tax multiplier.","d":3},{"q":"Same economy (MPC 0.75, gap $400 billion). How large a tax cut is needed to close the gap?","o":["About $133 billion","$100 billion","$400 billion","$300 billion"],"a":0,"e":"Tax multiplier magnitude = 0.75/0.25 = 3, so $400B / 3 = $133.3B. A spending increase of $100B is the smaller alternative because spending has the larger multiplier.","d":3},{"q":"Revenue is $4.5 trillion, spending is $6.0 trillion, starting debt is $30 trillion and GDP is $28 trillion. Ending debt-to-GDP (ignoring GDP growth) is:","o":["112.5%","107.1%","100%","5.4%"],"a":0,"e":"Deficit $1.5T makes debt $31.5T, and 31.5 / 28 = 112.5%. 107.1% uses the starting debt (30/28), and 5.4% is the deficit-to-GDP ratio.","d":3},{"q":"The government raises spending by $100 billion with a simple multiplier of 4, yet GDP rises by far less than $400 billion. Which factor best explains this?","o":["Automatic stabilizers raising tax collections and offsetting the new spending","The balanced-budget multiplier always equalling one in this situation","Crowding out: higher interest rates reduce private investment","Lower government borrowing"],"a":2,"e":"Borrowing to finance spending can lift rates and reduce investment, so net output rises by less. The balanced-budget multiplier applies only to equal changes in G and T.","d":3},{"q":"Policy A: a $10 billion transfer to low-income households with MPC = 0.9. Policy B: a $10 billion tax cut for high earners with MPC = 0.5. Compared with B, the simple multipliers imply A raises GDP by:","o":["The same amount","$8 billion more","$80 billion more","$40 billion more"],"a":2,"e":"A: 0.9/0.1 = 9, giving $90B. B: 0.5/0.5 = 1, giving $10B. Difference = $80B. Households with higher MPC spend more of each dollar, so targeting matters.","d":3},{"q":"An economy is at full employment and the government adds a large spending package. The most likely result is:","o":["A large, lasting increase in real GDP with stable prices throughout","Lower inflation because the multiplier raises real output a lot","A lower budget deficit","Mostly higher prices, with little increase in real output"],"a":3,"e":"With no idle resources, extra demand bids up prices instead of output. The multiplier on real output works mainly when slack exists.","d":3},{"q":"A tax rate of 30% on a base of $1,000 billion raises $300 billion. At 40% the base shrinks to $700 billion. What does this show?","o":["Revenue rises to $400 billion, so higher rates always help","Revenue falls to $280 billion: the prohibitive side of the curve","Revenue is unchanged at $300 billion","The Laffer curve does not apply to this case"],"a":1,"e":"0.40 x 700 = 280 < 300: the rate hike lowered revenue because the base fell more than proportionally. A rise to $400B would require the base to stay at 1,000.","d":3},{"q":"A large deficit-financed tax cut is combined with a Fed that holds money growth tight. The expected outcome is:","o":["Lower interest rates and rising private investment","No change in interest rates or investment","A falling exchange value of the dollar","High interest rates and crowded-out private investment"],"a":3,"e":"Heavy government borrowing against a restricted money supply drives rates up, crowding out investment and (through capital inflows) lifting the dollar. Falling rates would need monetary expansion.","d":3}]},{"id":"u11","title":"International Trade","day":6,"summary":"Learn why countries trade, how to find comparative advantage with opportunity costs, what tariffs and quotas do to prices and welfare, how trade agreements work, and how the balance of payments records a country's international transactions.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Absolute vs comparative advantage","body":"<p>Countries (and companies) trade because specializing lets everyone get more. Two ideas explain it.</p><ul><li><strong>Absolute advantage:</strong> being able to produce a good using fewer resources (or producing more output with the same resources) than another producer. It compares <em>productivity</em>.</li><li><strong>Comparative advantage:</strong> being able to produce a good at a lower <strong>opportunity cost</strong> than another producer. Opportunity cost is what you give up to make one more unit.</li></ul><p><strong>The key insight (Ricardo):</strong> gains from trade depend on comparative advantage, not absolute advantage. Even a country that is better at everything should specialize where its sacrifice is smallest, because it cannot be the lowest-opportunity-cost producer of both goods (one country's opportunity cost of laptops is the inverse of its opportunity cost of coffee).</p><p><strong>How to calculate:</strong> opportunity cost of good X in terms of Y = (output of Y given up) / (output of X gained). With one worker-day, Aland makes either 6 laptops or 12 tons of coffee; Bland makes either 2 laptops or 8 tons of coffee.</p><ul><li>Aland: 1 laptop costs 12/6 = 2 tons of coffee; 1 ton of coffee costs 6/12 = 0.5 laptop.</li><li>Bland: 1 laptop costs 8/2 = 4 tons of coffee; 1 ton of coffee costs 2/8 = 0.25 laptop.</li></ul><p>Aland has an absolute advantage in both goods (6 > 2 and 12 > 8). But Aland has the comparative advantage in laptops (2 < 4), and Bland has the comparative advantage in coffee (0.25 < 0.5).</p>"},{"h":"2. Specialization, gains from trade, and terms of trade","body":"<p>If each country specializes in the good with the lower opportunity cost and trades, total output rises. Using 10 worker-days each:</p><ul><li><strong>No trade:</strong> Aland splits days 5/5 and gets 30 laptops and 60 coffee. Bland splits 5/5 and gets 10 laptops and 40 coffee.</li><li><strong>Specialize:</strong> Bland makes only coffee (80). Aland makes laptops for 7 days (42) and coffee for 3 days (36). World output is 42 laptops and 116 coffee, up from 40 laptops and 100 coffee.</li></ul><p>The <strong>terms of trade</strong> is the rate at which goods swap. Each country will only trade if it gets a better deal than making the good itself. For laptops: Aland would refuse to sell below 2 coffee per laptop, and Bland would refuse to pay above 4. So the mutually beneficial range is <strong>between 2 and 4 tons of coffee per laptop</strong>. Where it lands inside the range decides how the gains are split.</p><p>At 1 laptop = 3 coffee, Aland sells 12 laptops for 36 coffee and ends with 30 laptops and 72 coffee (vs 30 and 60). Bland ends with 12 laptops and 44 coffee (vs 10 and 40). Both are better off, consuming beyond what they could alone.</p><p><strong>Business view:</strong> the same logic explains outsourcing: a firm focuses on its comparative advantage and buys the rest.</p>"},{"h":"3. Trade barriers: tariffs, quotas, subsidies, embargoes","body":"<ul><li><strong>Tariff:</strong> a tax on imports. It raises the domestic price, so consumers lose, domestic producers gain, government collects revenue, and the economy suffers a net loss (deadweight loss).</li><li><strong>Quota:</strong> a legal limit on the quantity imported. It raises the domestic price too, but there is no tax revenue; the gain goes to whoever holds import licenses (<strong>quota rents</strong>), unless the government auctions the licenses.</li><li><strong>Subsidy:</strong> a government payment to domestic producers (including exporters), lowering their costs so they can undercut foreign rivals. Taxpayers pay. Trading partners may respond with countervailing duties.</li><li><strong>Embargo:</strong> a complete ban on trade with a country, usually for political reasons.</li><li><strong>Non-tariff barriers:</strong> standards, licensing, paperwork, local-content rules.</li></ul><p><strong>Dumping</strong> is selling abroad below cost or below the home price; importing countries may respond with anti-dumping duties.</p>"},{"h":"4. The tariff diagram with numbers","body":"<p>Suppose domestic demand is Qd = 1000 - P and domestic supply is Qs = P (P in dollars, Q in units). With no trade, P = 500 and Q = 500.</p><p>The world price is $200. Free trade: Qd = 800, Qs = 200, so imports = 600. Now impose a $100 tariff, which lifts the domestic price to $300.</p><ul><li>Qd = 700, Qs = 300, so imports = 400.</li><li><strong>Consumer surplus lost</strong> = rectangle 100 x 700 + triangle 0.5 x 100 x 100 = 70,000 + 5,000 = $75,000.</li><li><strong>Producer surplus gained</strong> = rectangle 100 x 200 + triangle 0.5 x 100 x 100 = 20,000 + 5,000 = $25,000.</li><li><strong>Government revenue</strong> = tariff x imports = 100 x 400 = $40,000.</li><li><strong>Deadweight loss</strong> = two triangles = 5,000 (inefficient extra domestic production) + 5,000 (lost consumption) = $10,000.</li></ul><p>Check: +25,000 + 40,000 - 75,000 = -10,000. The nation as a whole loses. A <strong>quota</strong> of 400 units gives the same price ($300) but the 40,000 goes to license holders rather than the government.</p>"},{"h":"5. Arguments for protection and their flaws","body":"<ul><li><strong>Infant industry:</strong> temporary protection lets a new industry grow to compete. <em>Flaw:</em> hard to pick winners, and protection tends to become permanent and breed inefficiency.</li><li><strong>National security:</strong> keep key industries (defense, steel, chips) at home. <em>Flaw:</em> any industry can claim it; targeted subsidies or stockpiles may cost less.</li><li><strong>Protect jobs:</strong> tariffs save jobs in the shielded industry. <em>Flaw:</em> jobs are lost in industries that use the protected input and in exporters hit by retaliation; consumers often pay far more than the wages of the jobs saved.</li><li><strong>Unfair competition / dumping:</strong> counter foreign subsidies. <em>Partly valid</em> if dumping is proven.</li><li><strong>Bargaining chip:</strong> threaten tariffs to win concessions. <em>Flaw:</em> can trigger a trade war.</li></ul><p><strong>Manager example:</strong> a 25% tariff on steel imported at $800 per ton adds $200 per ton. A fabricator buying 10,000 tons faces $2,000,000 more in annual cost, to be absorbed or passed to customers.</p>"},{"h":"6. Trade agreements and institutions","body":"<ul><li><strong>WTO (World Trade Organization):</strong> founded 1995, successor to GATT. Sets trade rules, runs negotiations, and settles disputes. Key principles: <em>most-favored-nation</em> (treat all members alike) and <em>national treatment</em> (treat imports like domestic goods once inside).</li><li><strong>Levels of integration (low to high):</strong> free trade area (no tariffs among members, each sets its own outside tariffs), customs union (adds a common external tariff), common market (adds free movement of labor and capital), economic union (harmonized policies); monetary union (shared currency).</li><li><strong>USMCA:</strong> replaced NAFTA in 2020. A free trade area of the U.S., Mexico, and Canada with rules of origin (e.g., autos).</li><li><strong>EU:</strong> a single market and customs union, and a monetary union for euro members.</li><li><strong>Other blocs:</strong> ASEAN, Mercosur, CPTPP, African Continental Free Trade Area.</li></ul><p><strong>Trade creation</strong> (shifting to a cheaper member producer) is good; <strong>trade diversion</strong> (shifting from a cheaper outside producer to a costlier member because of the bloc's tariff) is the cost.</p>"},{"h":"7. The balance of payments","body":"<p>The <strong>balance of payments (BoP)</strong> records every transaction between a country and the rest of the world in a year. It has two main accounts.</p><ul><li><strong>Current account:</strong> trade in goods and services (exports minus imports), net primary income (investment income, wages), and secondary income (remittances, foreign aid).</li><li><strong>Financial (capital) account:</strong> flows of financial assets and investment: foreign direct investment, portfolio investment (stocks/bonds), and reserves. A smaller <em>capital account</em> covers minor capital transfers.</li></ul><p>They must roughly offset: <strong>current account + financial account ≈ 0</strong> (statistical discrepancy aside). A current account <em>deficit</em> means the country spends more abroad than it earns, financed by a net inflow of foreign capital (foreigners acquire assets or lend).</p><p><strong>Trade deficit</strong> = imports exceed exports of goods and services. Example: exports $3.0 trillion, imports $3.9 trillion, trade deficit $0.9 trillion; with net income outflows of $0.1 trillion the current account is -$1.0 trillion, matched by a +$1.0 trillion financial account inflow.</p><p>A deficit is not automatically bad: it may reflect strong investment opportunities. It becomes a worry when it persists on borrowed money that is spent on consumption.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["A producer has an absolute advantage when it can make more of a good with the same resources. It has a comparative advantage when its opportunity cost is lower.","Gains from trade come from comparative advantage, not absolute advantage. Even a country better at everything gains by specializing.","To find opportunity cost, divide what you give up by what you gain. If Aland gives up twelve coffee for six laptops, a laptop costs two coffee.","The terms of trade must lie between the two countries' opportunity costs for both to benefit.","A tariff is a tax on imports. It raises the domestic price, hurts consumers, helps domestic producers, raises government revenue, and creates a deadweight loss.","A quota limits the quantity of imports. It raises prices like a tariff, but the extra revenue goes to license holders instead of the government.","Subsidies lower domestic producers' costs, while an embargo bans trade altogether.","Common arguments for protection are the infant industry, national security, and saving jobs. Each has serious flaws, such as higher consumer costs and foreign retaliation.","The World Trade Organization sets rules and settles disputes. USMCA replaced NAFTA among the United States, Mexico, and Canada.","A customs union is a free trade area plus a common external tariff. The European Union goes further as a single market.","The balance of payments has a current account and a financial account. Together they roughly sum to zero.","A trade deficit means imports exceed exports. A current account deficit is financed by capital inflows."],"examples":[{"q":"In one worker-day, Aland makes 6 laptops or 12 tons of coffee. Bland makes 2 laptops or 8 tons of coffee. (a) Find each country's opportunity cost of a laptop and of a ton of coffee. (b) Who has the comparative advantage in each good? (c) What terms of trade (coffee per laptop) benefit both?","steps":["Aland: laptop = 12/6 = 2 coffee; coffee = 6/12 = 0.5 laptop.","Bland: laptop = 8/2 = 4 coffee; coffee = 2/8 = 0.25 laptop.","Lower opportunity cost of laptops: Aland (2 < 4). Lower opportunity cost of coffee: Bland (0.25 < 0.5). Aland has an absolute advantage in both, but that does not decide it.","Aland will sell a laptop only for more than 2 coffee; Bland will pay less than 4 coffee. The range is between 2 and 4 coffee per laptop (e.g., 3)."],"answer":"Aland: laptops (opp. cost 2 coffee); Bland: coffee (0.25 laptop); terms between 2 and 4 coffee per laptop"},{"q":"Domestic demand is Qd = 1000 - P and supply is Qs = P. The world price is $200 and the government imposes a $100 per unit tariff. Find the new imports, tariff revenue, and deadweight loss.","steps":["Domestic price with tariff = 200 + 100 = $300.","Qd = 1000 - 300 = 700; Qs = 300; imports = 700 - 300 = 400 (down from 800 - 200 = 600).","Revenue = $100 x 400 = $40,000.","DWL = 0.5 x 100 x (300 - 200) [production side] + 0.5 x 100 x (800 - 700) [consumption side] = 5,000 + 5,000 = $10,000."],"answer":"Imports 400; revenue $40,000; DWL $10,000"},{"q":"A country exports $3.0 trillion of goods and services and imports $3.9 trillion. Net primary and secondary income is -$0.1 trillion. (a) What is the trade balance? (b) The current account? (c) What must the financial account be (approximately)?","steps":["Trade balance = 3.0 - 3.9 = -$0.9 trillion (a trade deficit).","Current account = -0.9 + (-0.1) = -$1.0 trillion.","Current account + financial account is about 0, so the financial account is about +$1.0 trillion.","Interpretation: a net inflow of $1.0 trillion of foreign capital funds the gap."],"answer":"(a) -$0.9T; (b) -$1.0T; (c) about +$1.0T"}],"videos":[{"title":"Comparative advantage and gains from trade","query":"comparative advantage absolute advantage opportunity cost explained example"},{"title":"Tariffs and quotas: deadweight loss","query":"tariffs quotas deadweight loss consumer surplus explained"},{"title":"Balance of payments current and financial account","query":"balance of payments current account financial account explained"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Absolute advantage","b":"Ability to produce more with the same resources."},{"f":"Comparative advantage","b":"Ability to produce at lower opportunity cost."},{"f":"Opportunity cost","b":"What you give up to get one more unit."},{"f":"Gains from trade","b":"Higher total output and consumption through specialization and exchange."},{"f":"Terms of trade","b":"The rate at which two goods are exchanged."},{"f":"Range for terms of trade","b":"Between the two countries' opportunity costs."},{"f":"Tariff","b":"A tax on imported goods."},{"f":"Effect of a tariff on domestic price","b":"Raises it by up to the amount of the tariff."},{"f":"Deadweight loss of a tariff","b":"Two triangles: extra costly production plus lost consumption."},{"f":"Quota","b":"Legal limit on the quantity of an import."},{"f":"Quota rent","b":"Profit earned by holders of import licenses."},{"f":"Embargo","b":"A complete ban on trade with a country."},{"f":"Export subsidy","b":"Government payment lowering exporters' costs and foreign prices."},{"f":"Dumping","b":"Selling abroad below cost or below home price."},{"f":"Infant industry argument","b":"Temporary protection until a new industry can compete."},{"f":"WTO","b":"Sets trade rules and settles disputes; successor to GATT."},{"f":"Free trade area vs customs union","b":"Customs union adds a common external tariff."},{"f":"USMCA","b":"Trade pact among US, Mexico, and Canada replacing NAFTA."},{"f":"Trade diversion","b":"Bloc tariff shifts buying from cheaper outsiders to costlier members."},{"f":"Current account","b":"Trade in goods/services plus income and transfers."},{"f":"Financial account","b":"Cross-border investment and asset flows (FDI, portfolio)."},{"f":"Trade deficit","b":"Imports exceed exports of goods and services."}],"pairs":[["Absolute advantage","Fewer resources needed per unit"],["Comparative advantage","Lower opportunity cost"],["Terms of trade","Rate goods exchange between countries"],["Tariff","Tax on imported goods"],["Quota","Cap on imported quantity"],["Embargo","Total ban on trade"],["Customs union","Free trade plus common external tariff"],["Current account","Goods, services, income, transfers"]],"traps":["Trap: The country that is best at everything gains nothing from trade. Truth: Gains come from comparative advantage; opportunity costs always differ.","Trap: Comparative advantage means producing more output. Truth: It means a lower opportunity cost, which is not the same as higher productivity.","Trap: Tariffs help the nation overall. Truth: Producers and government gain, but consumers lose more, leaving a deadweight loss.","Trap: A quota produces government revenue just like a tariff. Truth: Revenue goes to license holders unless licenses are auctioned.","Trap: A current account deficit means the country has no capital account. Truth: It is financed by a matching financial account inflow."],"mnemonic":"Opportunity cost decides, Output doesn't: trade where you give up the LEAST. Tariff = Tax, Quota = Quantity, Embargo = Entire ban.","cram":["Comparative advantage = lowest opportunity cost, not highest output.","Opportunity cost of X = give up Y divided by gain of X.","Terms of trade lie between the two opportunity costs.","Tariff: price up, consumers lose, producers and government gain, DWL.","Quota rents go to license holders; tariff revenue goes to government.","Customs union = free trade area plus common external tariff.","Current account plus financial account approximately equals zero.","USMCA replaced NAFTA in 2020; WTO replaced GATT in 1995."],"ytv":[{"title":"Comparative Advantage - Why Does Trade Take Place?","channel":"EconplusDal","id":"qh60Kt13AWc","why":"Opportunity-cost method for comparative advantage"},{"title":"Comparative Advantage, Free Trade, and Tariffs","channel":"Ric Thomas - Campus Macro","id":"uEaiDlUQg6M","why":"Gains from trade and what tariffs do"},{"title":"Comparative Advantage Gains from International Trade","channel":"Nick Bergan","id":"az3n8xQtX9I","why":"Step-by-step gains-from-trade example"}],"questions":[{"q":"A country that can produce a good using fewer resources than any other country has:","o":["A comparative advantage","A trade surplus","A tariff advantage","An absolute advantage"],"a":3,"e":"Absolute advantage compares productivity. Comparative advantage compares opportunity costs, which is the common mix-up.","d":1},{"q":"Comparative advantage is determined by comparing:","o":["Opportunity costs","Total output only","Wage levels only","Tariff rates"],"a":0,"e":"Comparative advantage goes to the producer with the lower opportunity cost. Total output is absolute advantage, and wages or tariffs do not define it.","d":1},{"q":"In one day Aland can make 6 laptops or 12 tons of coffee. Aland's opportunity cost of one laptop is:","o":["0.5 tons of coffee","6 tons of coffee","12 tons of coffee","2 tons of coffee"],"a":3,"e":"Opportunity cost = 12 / 6 = 2 tons of coffee. The 0.5 figure is the opportunity cost of a ton of coffee (in laptops).","d":1},{"q":"A tariff is:","o":["A tax on imported goods","A limit on the quantity of imports","A ban on all trade with a country","A payment to domestic producers"],"a":0,"e":"A tariff is a tax. A quantity limit is a quota, a ban is an embargo, and a producer payment is a subsidy.","d":1},{"q":"A quota on imported shoes:","o":["Charges a tax on each pair of shoes imported","Subsidizes domestic shoe makers","Bans all shoe imports forever","Limits the quantity that can be imported"],"a":3,"e":"A quota is a quantity restriction. Taxing each pair would be a tariff.","d":1},{"q":"A complete government ban on trading with another country is called:","o":["A quota","A subsidy","A customs union","An embargo"],"a":3,"e":"An embargo prohibits trade. A quota only limits it, and a subsidy supports producers.","d":1},{"q":"The World Trade Organization (WTO) primarily:","o":["Sets trade rules and settles member disputes","Sets exchange rates and currency pegs for its members","Lends money to countries in crisis","Collects tariffs on behalf of members"],"a":0,"e":"The WTO administers trade agreements and dispute settlement. Lending to countries in crisis is the IMF's job.","d":1},{"q":"A country has a trade deficit when:","o":["Exports exceed imports","Its government spends more than it taxes","Imports exceed exports","Its currency is depreciating"],"a":2,"e":"The trade balance is exports minus imports. A budget deficit (spending above taxes) is a different concept.","d":1},{"q":"Aland: 6 laptops or 12 coffee per day. Bland: 2 laptops or 8 coffee per day. Who has the comparative advantage in coffee?","o":["Aland, because it produces more coffee per day","Aland, because its opportunity cost is 0.5 laptop per ton","Bland, because its opportunity cost is 0.25 laptop per ton","Neither, because Aland has an absolute advantage in both"],"a":2,"e":"Bland gives up 0.25 laptop per ton versus Aland's 0.5. Aland's higher output is an absolute advantage, which does not determine comparative advantage.","d":2},{"q":"Using the Aland/Bland numbers, which price of one laptop would make BOTH countries better off?","o":["1 ton of coffee","2 tons of coffee","5 tons of coffee","3 tons of coffee"],"a":3,"e":"The range is above Aland's 2 and below Bland's 4. At 2 Aland gains nothing; at 5 Bland would be better off making laptops itself; 1 is below Aland's cost.","d":2},{"q":"A country is more productive than its partner in every good. Should it still trade?","o":["Yes, because gains come from differences in opportunity costs","No, because it can make every good more cheaply at home than abroad","No, because the partner would lose all its industries","Yes, but only if tariffs are set to zero"],"a":0,"e":"Even if one country is better at everything, opportunity costs differ, so each gains by specializing. Absolute superiority does not remove the gain.","d":2},{"q":"A tariff on imported steel will most likely cause the domestic price of steel to:","o":["Fall, helping consumers and also hurting domestic steel producers","Rise, hurting consumers and helping domestic steel producers","Stay the same because foreign firms absorb it entirely","Fall, because of increased competition"],"a":1,"e":"A tariff raises the domestic price toward the world price plus tax. Consumers and users of steel pay more, while domestic producers earn more.","d":2},{"q":"At a world price of $200 a $100 tariff cuts imports to 400 units. Tariff revenue is:","o":["$100,000","$60,000","$40,000","$80,000"],"a":2,"e":"Revenue = tariff x imports after the tariff = 100 x 400 = $40,000. $60,000 would apply the pre-tariff import level of 600, which is the wrong quantity.","d":2},{"q":"How does a quota differ from an equivalent tariff?","o":["The extra revenue goes to license holders, not government","It raises the domestic price while a tariff lowers it","It has no deadweight loss at all","It increases the quantity imported"],"a":0,"e":"Both raise prices by restricting imports, but a quota creates rents for license holders unless the licenses are auctioned. Both have deadweight loss.","d":2},{"q":"A government pays a subsidy on each unit of wheat exported. Who most clearly bears the cost, and who benefits?","o":["Domestic taxpayers pay; foreign buyers and exporters benefit","Foreign taxpayers pay; domestic consumers benefit","Domestic consumers pay through a tariff; exporters lose","No one pays because it is just a transfer of tax rights"],"a":0,"e":"Subsidies are funded by the home government's taxpayers, and they let exporters sell at lower prices abroad. Foreign taxpayers do not fund them, though foreign rivals may complain of unfair competition.","d":2},{"q":"The infant-industry argument for protection says:","o":["Protect all established industries permanently to preserve their jobs","Foreign infants should be taxed","Only exporting industries should ever receive government subsidies","Temporary tariffs let a young industry grow until it can compete"],"a":3,"e":"The idea is temporary shelter while scale and learning develop. A common flaw is that protection rarely ends once granted.","d":2},{"q":"Why do economists often criticize 'save jobs' tariff arguments?","o":["Tariffs always destroy every job in the protected industry","Jobs are lost in user industries and retaliation targets","Tariffs cut consumer prices so spending rises","Jobs are never saved by tariffs in any industry"],"a":1,"e":"Tariffs can save jobs in a protected industry but cost jobs elsewhere, with high price costs per job saved. The 'never saved' claim goes too far.","d":2},{"q":"What distinguishes a customs union from a free trade area?","o":["Free movement of labor and capital","A shared currency for all members","A common external tariff on non-members","Absence of any tariffs among members"],"a":2,"e":"Both remove tariffs internally; a customs union adds a common outside tariff. Free labor movement defines a common market and a shared currency a monetary union.","d":2},{"q":"USMCA is best described as:","o":["A customs union between North America and the European Union","A shared-currency monetary union across North America","A U.S.-Mexico-Canada trade pact that replaced NAFTA","A WTO dispute panel for Americas trade"],"a":2,"e":"USMCA came into force in 2020 and updated NAFTA, including auto rules of origin. It is not a currency union or a customs union.","d":2},{"q":"A country runs a current account deficit. Its financial account will most likely show:","o":["A net outflow of capital","A net inflow of foreign capital","A matching budget surplus","No transactions"],"a":1,"e":"The accounts offset roughly: spending abroad beyond earnings is financed by foreigners buying assets or lending. Net outflow would worsen the imbalance.","d":2},{"q":"Which transaction is recorded in the current account?","o":["A Japanese firm buying a U.S. factory","A U.S. company exporting software to Germany","A foreign investor buying U.S. Treasury bonds","A U.S. firm opening a plant in Mexico"],"a":1,"e":"Exports of goods and services belong in the current account. Factory purchases, bond buying, and building plants are investment flows in the financial account.","d":2},{"q":"Country X makes 4 cars or 8 tons of steel per day; country Y makes 3 cars or 12 tons of steel. Which price (tons of steel per car) benefits both?","o":["3 tons","2 tons","4 tons","5 tons"],"a":0,"e":"X's cost of a car = 2 steel, Y's = 4 steel. The price must be strictly between 2 and 4. At 2 or 4 one country gains nothing, and 5 exceeds what Y would pay.","d":3},{"q":"Using Qd = 1000 - P, Qs = P, world price $200, tariff $100: the deadweight loss is:","o":["$5,000","$10,000","$40,000","$75,000"],"a":1,"e":"Production triangle 0.5 x 100 x 100 = 5,000 plus consumption triangle 0.5 x 100 x 100 = 5,000 = 10,000. $5,000 counts only one triangle, and $75,000 is the consumer surplus loss.","d":3},{"q":"In the same market, what is the net change in national welfare from the tariff (producer gain + revenue - consumer loss)?","o":["+$40,000","+$25,000","-$75,000","-$10,000"],"a":3,"e":"+25,000 + 40,000 - 75,000 = -10,000. Producers and the government gain, but consumers lose more, which equals the deadweight loss.","d":3},{"q":"The same market imposes a quota of 400 units instead of the tariff, with import licenses given free to foreign exporters. What are the license rents?","o":["$0","$10,000","$40,000","$75,000"],"a":2,"e":"A 400-unit quota raises the price to $300 (Qd 700, Qs 300). Licenses earn (300 - 200) x 400 = $40,000, which is the same size as the tariff revenue but goes abroad.","d":3},{"q":"A country's exports of goods and services are $3.0T, imports $3.9T, and net income and transfers are -$0.1T. Which statement is correct?","o":["Current account is -$0.9T and financial account is -$0.9T","Current account is -$1.0T and the financial account is about +$1.0T","Current account is +$1.0T and financial account is -$1.0T","Current account is -$1.0T and financial account is about -$1.0T"],"a":1,"e":"Trade deficit -0.9 plus income -0.1 = -1.0 for the current account, offset by +1.0 on the financial account. The trade balance alone (-0.9) is not the full current account.","d":3},{"q":"A manufacturer imports 10,000 tons of steel at $800 per ton. A 25% tariff is imposed and the importer pays it all. The added annual cost is:","o":["$8,000,000","$200,000","$2,000,000","$10,000,000"],"a":2,"e":"Tariff per ton = 0.25 x 800 = $200; 200 x 10,000 = $2,000,000. $8M is the original steel bill, not the added cost.","d":3},{"q":"Aland spends 7 days on laptops and 3 on coffee; Bland spends 10 days on coffee (6 or 12 and 2 or 8 per day). World output is:","o":["42 laptops and 80 tons of coffee","42 laptops and 116 tons of coffee","60 laptops and 80 tons of coffee","40 laptops and 100 tons of coffee"],"a":1,"e":"Aland: 7 x 6 = 42 laptops and 3 x 12 = 36 coffee. Bland: 10 x 8 = 80 coffee. Total coffee = 116. The 40 and 100 pair does not follow from the stated day allocations.","d":3},{"q":"At 1 laptop = 3 tons of coffee, Bland buys 12 laptops by exporting coffee. Compared with making those laptops itself (4 coffee each), Bland saves:","o":["36 tons of coffee","12 tons of coffee","48 tons of coffee","0 tons of coffee"],"a":1,"e":"Buying costs 12 x 3 = 36 coffee; producing would cost 12 x 4 = 48. Savings = 12. 36 is only the price paid.","d":3},{"q":"A customs union reduces tariffs among members and sets a common outside tariff. Trade diversion occurs when:","o":["Members switch to costlier partners because outsiders are taxed","Members switch to cheaper outside producers once tariffs are removed","Members stop all trade with each other and rely on domestic output","Members adopt a shared currency together"],"a":0,"e":"The common external tariff can make a higher-cost member more attractive than a lower-cost outsider, a loss of efficiency. Buying from cheaper outsiders is the opposite effect.","d":3}]},{"id":"u12","title":"Exchange Rates & Global Business","day":6,"summary":"Learn to read exchange rates, see who wins and loses when currencies move, understand what drives currency values and exchange-rate regimes, hedge currency risk, and choose how to enter foreign markets.","lesson":[{"h":"1. Reading exchange rates","body":"<p>An <strong>exchange rate</strong> is the price of one currency in terms of another. You can quote it two ways.</p><ul><li><strong>Direct quote:</strong> units of <em>home</em> currency per one unit of <em>foreign</em> currency. For a U.S. manager, $1.10 per euro is direct. Think: 'the price of the foreign currency in dollars.'</li><li><strong>Indirect quote:</strong> units of foreign currency per one unit of home currency: 1 / 1.10 = 0.909 euros per dollar. The two are reciprocals.</li></ul><p>Always say what is in the numerator: 'USD/EUR = 1.10' means 1.10 dollars for 1 euro. Mixing up which currency is the 'one unit' is the most common mistake.</p><p><strong>Cross rates:</strong> if two currencies are each quoted against the dollar, you can find the rate between them. Given $1.10 per euro and $1.30 per pound, a pound is worth 1.30 / 1.10 = 1.18 euros. Given 150 yen per dollar and $1.10 per euro, one euro is 150 x 1.10 = 165 yen.</p><p><strong>Spot vs forward:</strong> the spot rate is for immediate exchange (about two business days); a forward rate is agreed today for exchange at a future date.</p>"},{"h":"2. Appreciation, depreciation, and who wins or loses","body":"<p>A currency <strong>appreciates</strong> when it buys more foreign currency (its price rises) and <strong>depreciates</strong> when it buys less. Under fixed regimes the terms are <strong>revaluation</strong> and <strong>devaluation</strong>. One currency's appreciation is always another's depreciation.</p><p>Example: the euro rises from $1.10 to $1.21, a 10% appreciation of the euro (1.21/1.10 - 1). The dollar has depreciated against the euro, though by a different percentage: in euros per dollar it fell from 0.909 to 0.826, down 9.1%.</p><ul><li><strong>Stronger dollar (appreciation):</strong> U.S. exporters lose competitiveness (their goods cost more abroad, or their foreign revenues shrink when converted). U.S. importers and U.S. tourists abroad win (foreign goods and hotels are cheaper). Foreign tourists in the U.S. pay more.</li><li><strong>Weaker dollar (depreciation):</strong> U.S. exporters gain, U.S. import-dependent firms pay more, foreign visitors find the U.S. a bargain.</li></ul><p><strong>Numbers:</strong> a U.S. firm invoices a machine at EUR 220,000. At $1.10 per euro it receives $242,000; if the euro falls to $1.00 it receives only $220,000. A U.S. importer owing EUR 500,000 pays $550,000 at 1.10 but $605,000 at 1.21, $55,000 more.</p>"},{"h":"3. What moves exchange rates","body":"<p>In a floating system, the price of a currency comes from supply and demand in the foreign exchange (forex) market. Demand for dollars comes from foreigners buying U.S. goods, assets, and investments; supply of dollars comes from Americans buying foreign goods and assets.</p><p>Things that <strong>shift</strong> demand or supply:</p><ul><li><strong>Relative interest rates:</strong> higher U.S. rates attract global investors seeking returns, so demand for dollars rises and the dollar appreciates.</li><li><strong>Relative inflation:</strong> higher inflation at home makes a country's goods less competitive and erodes the currency's value, so it depreciates over time (the PPP link).</li><li><strong>Relative growth and economic strength:</strong> strong growth and attractive investment opportunities attract capital (appreciation), although faster growth also pulls in imports (a pressure toward depreciation).</li><li><strong>Speculation and expectations:</strong> if traders expect a currency to rise, they buy it now, driving it up. Political stability, central-bank credibility, and safe-haven demand matter too.</li><li><strong>Tastes and trade:</strong> a global surge in demand for a country's products raises demand for its currency.</li></ul>"},{"h":"4. Exchange rate regimes","body":"<ul><li><strong>Floating (flexible):</strong> the market sets the rate (U.S. dollar, euro, yen). Pros: automatic adjustment to trade imbalances, independent monetary policy. Cons: volatility, which creates risk for importers and exporters.</li><li><strong>Fixed (pegged):</strong> the government sets the rate against another currency (e.g., the U.S. dollar or a basket) and stands ready to buy or sell to defend it. Pros: stability and predictability, which helps trade and investment. Cons: the central bank needs large reserves, and it gives up independent monetary policy.</li><li><strong>Managed float / crawling peg:</strong> the market sets the rate mostly, with occasional central-bank intervention.</li><li><strong>Currency union:</strong> countries share a currency (the euro).</li></ul><p><strong>Impossible trinity:</strong> a country cannot have all three of (1) a fixed exchange rate, (2) free movement of capital, and (3) independent monetary policy. Pick two.</p><p><strong>Devaluation example:</strong> a peg of 10 local units per dollar moves to 12. The local currency's dollar value falls from $0.100 to $0.083 (-16.7%), making exports cheaper and imports costlier.</p>"},{"h":"5. Purchasing power parity and interest rate parity","body":"<p><strong>Purchasing power parity (PPP)</strong> builds on the <em>law of one price</em>: identical goods should cost the same everywhere once converted to a common currency. The exchange rate that would equalize prices is the PPP rate. In the long run, high-inflation currencies tend to depreciate.</p><p><strong>Big Mac index:</strong> a Big Mac costs $5.00 in the U.S. and 80 pesos in Mexico. The implied PPP rate is 80 / 5 = 16 pesos per dollar. If the actual rate is 18 pesos per dollar, the peso buys less than PPP says it should: it is <strong>undervalued</strong> by (16 - 18) / 18 = -11.1% (about 11%). Limits of PPP: transport costs, trade barriers, non-traded inputs (rent, labor), and different tastes.</p><p><strong>Interest rate parity (IRP)</strong> (covered form): investors should earn the same return on equivalent safe assets after covering exchange risk with a forward contract. So the forward rate reflects the interest rate gap: <strong>F = S x (1 + i home) / (1 + i foreign).</strong> If S = $1.10 per euro, U.S. rate 5%, euro rate 3%, then F = 1.10 x 1.05 / 1.03 = $1.1214. The currency with the higher interest rate trades at a forward <em>discount</em> (the dollar is expected to be worth less forward).</p>"},{"h":"6. Currency risk and hedging","body":"<p><strong>Transaction risk</strong> arises from a specific future foreign-currency payment or receipt whose home-currency value may change. There is also <strong>translation risk</strong> (converting foreign subsidiaries' statements into the parent's currency) and <strong>economic risk</strong> (long-run competitiveness effects).</p><p><strong>Hedging with a forward contract:</strong> agree today on a rate for a future exchange.</p><ul><li>You will <em>receive</em> a foreign currency later: sell it forward.</li><li>You will <em>pay</em> a foreign currency later: buy it forward.</li></ul><p><strong>Example:</strong> a U.S. firm will receive EUR 1,000,000 in 90 days. The 90-day forward rate is $1.12, locking in $1,120,000. If the spot rate then is $1.05, unhedged it would get only $1,050,000, so the hedge saved $70,000. If the spot rate is $1.20, unhedged it would get $1,200,000, so the hedge cost it $80,000 of upside. A hedge removes uncertainty both ways; it is insurance, not a profit device.</p><p>Other tools: options (right but not obligation), currency swaps, <strong>natural hedges</strong> (matching foreign revenues and costs in the same currency, borrowing in the currency you earn), and invoicing in your own currency (which shifts the risk to the customer).</p>"},{"h":"7. Going global: FDI, entry modes, risk, and multinational management","body":"<p><strong>Globalization</strong> is the growing integration of economies through trade, investment, finance, technology, and people. Benefits: larger markets, lower prices, access to inputs and talent, and economies of scale. Costs: job displacement, exposure to foreign shocks, and inequality tensions.</p><p><strong>Foreign direct investment (FDI)</strong> is a lasting, controlling ownership stake in a foreign business (building a plant, buying a company). It differs from <em>portfolio investment</em> (buying stocks or bonds without control).</p><p><strong>Entry modes</strong>, from low to high commitment, control, and risk:</p><ol><li><strong>Exporting:</strong> make at home and sell abroad. Low risk, low control over marketing, tariff and transport exposure.</li><li><strong>Licensing:</strong> a foreign firm pays a fee for rights to your technology or brand. Cheap and quick, but you risk creating a competitor and lose quality control.</li><li><strong>Franchising:</strong> a package of brand, system, and support to local operators for fees (restaurants, hotels).</li><li><strong>Joint venture:</strong> shared ownership with a local partner. Shares cost, risk, and local knowledge; shared control can cause conflict.</li><li><strong>Wholly owned subsidiary</strong> (greenfield or acquisition): full control and profits, but the highest cost and risk.</li></ol><p><strong>Country and political risk:</strong> expropriation or nationalization, sanctions, capital controls, corruption, regulatory change, weak intellectual property enforcement, and instability. <strong>Emerging markets</strong> (e.g., India, Brazil, Vietnam) offer fast growth and young consumers but bring currency volatility, weaker institutions, and sudden capital outflows when U.S. rates rise.</p><p><strong>Multinational management:</strong> balance global efficiency with local responsiveness, adapt to cultural differences, manage currency exposure centrally, and keep transfer pricing and tax compliance in view.</p>"}],"keyPoints":["A direct quote is home currency per one unit of foreign currency. For a United States manager, one dollar ten per euro is a direct quote.","An indirect quote is the reciprocal. One divided by one point one zero equals zero point nine zero nine euros per dollar.","Cross rates come from dividing two rates quoted against the same currency. One thirty per pound divided by one ten per euro gives about one point one eight euros per pound.","When a currency appreciates, exporters from that country lose competitiveness, while importers and tourists traveling abroad gain.","Higher interest rates, lower inflation, and strong investor confidence raise demand for a currency and push it up.","Higher relative inflation weakens a currency over time, which is the idea behind purchasing power parity.","Under a fixed exchange rate, the central bank defends the peg with reserves and gives up independent monetary policy. A floating rate is set by the market.","The Big Mac index applies purchasing power parity. If the implied rate is sixteen pesos but the actual rate is eighteen, the peso is undervalued by about eleven percent.","Interest rate parity says the forward rate reflects the interest rate gap. The higher interest rate currency trades at a forward discount.","To hedge a future foreign currency receipt, sell that currency forward. To hedge a future payment, buy it forward.","Foreign direct investment is a lasting controlling stake in a foreign business. Portfolio investment is not.","Entry modes run from exporting, licensing, and franchising, to joint ventures, to wholly owned subsidiaries, with rising control, cost, and risk."],"examples":[{"q":"The euro trades at $1.10 and the pound at $1.30. The yen trades at 150 per dollar. Find (a) euros per dollar, (b) euros per pound, (c) yen per euro. (d) If the euro rises to $1.21, what is its percentage appreciation and how much more does a $500,000-equivalent (EUR 500,000) import bill cost?","steps":["(a) Indirect quote: 1 / 1.10 = 0.909 euros per dollar.","(b) Cross rate: 1.30 / 1.10 = 1.18 euros per pound.","(c) 150 yen per dollar x $1.10 per euro = 165 yen per euro.","(d) 1.21 / 1.10 - 1 = 10% appreciation. A EUR 500,000 bill: 500,000 x 1.10 = $550,000 before; 500,000 x 1.21 = $605,000 after; increase = $55,000."],"answer":"(a) 0.909; (b) 1.18; (c) 165; (d) +10%, import bill +$55,000"},{"q":"A Big Mac costs $5.00 in the U.S. and 80 pesos in Mexico. The actual exchange rate is 18 pesos per dollar. (a) What is the implied PPP rate? (b) Is the peso over- or undervalued, and by how much? (c) Forward rate: spot $1.10 per euro, U.S. interest 5%, euro interest 3%, 1 year. Find the forward rate by interest rate parity.","steps":["(a) 80 / 5 = 16 pesos per dollar.","(b) PPP says the dollar should buy 16 pesos but actually buys 18, so the peso is cheaper than PPP implies: undervalued by (16 - 18) / 18 = -11.1%.","(c) F = S x (1 + i_US) / (1 + i_EUR) = 1.10 x 1.05 / 1.03 = 1.1214.","The euro trades at a forward premium (F > S) because the euro rate is lower."],"answer":"(a) 16 pesos/$; (b) peso undervalued about 11%; (c) $1.1214 per euro"},{"q":"A U.S. company will receive EUR 1,000,000 in 90 days. The 90-day forward rate is $1.12 per euro. Compare the dollars received (a) with the forward hedge, (b) unhedged if spot is $1.05, and (c) unhedged if spot is $1.20.","steps":["(a) Hedged: 1,000,000 x 1.12 = $1,120,000, certain.","(b) Unhedged at 1.05: $1,050,000. Hedge advantage = 1,120,000 - 1,050,000 = +$70,000.","(c) Unhedged at 1.20: $1,200,000. Hedge result vs unhedged = 1,120,000 - 1,200,000 = -$80,000 (gave up upside).","Lesson: the forward locks in certainty; it will look good or bad afterwards depending on where spot goes."],"answer":"(a) $1,120,000; (b) $1,050,000 (hedge +$70,000); (c) $1,200,000 (hedge -$80,000)"}],"videos":[{"title":"Exchange rates, appreciation and depreciation explained","query":"exchange rates appreciation depreciation explained foreign exchange market supply and demand"},{"title":"Purchasing power parity and the Big Mac index","query":"purchasing power parity Big Mac index explained"},{"title":"Currency hedging with forward contracts","query":"hedging currency risk forward contract explained example"}],"flashcards":[{"f":"Direct quote (for a US manager)","b":"Dollars per one unit of foreign currency."},{"f":"Indirect quote","b":"Foreign currency per one dollar; the reciprocal of direct."},{"f":"Cross rate","b":"Exchange rate between two currencies derived from each one's dollar rate."},{"f":"Spot rate","b":"Rate for immediate currency exchange."},{"f":"Appreciation","b":"Currency buys more foreign currency; its value rises."},{"f":"Depreciation","b":"Currency buys less foreign currency; its value falls."},{"f":"Who gains from a weaker domestic currency","b":"Exporters and foreign tourists visiting."},{"f":"Who gains from a stronger domestic currency","b":"Importers and domestic tourists abroad."},{"f":"Effect of higher domestic interest rates","b":"Attracts capital, raises demand for currency, appreciation."},{"f":"Effect of higher relative inflation","b":"Currency depreciates over time."},{"f":"Floating exchange rate","b":"Market supply and demand set the rate."},{"f":"Fixed (pegged) exchange rate","b":"Government defends rate against another currency using reserves."},{"f":"Devaluation","b":"Official cut in a pegged currency's value."},{"f":"Impossible trinity","b":"Cannot have fixed rate, free capital, and independent monetary policy together."},{"f":"Purchasing power parity","b":"Exchange rates adjust to equalize prices of identical goods."},{"f":"Big Mac index","b":"Informal PPP gauge comparing burger prices across countries."},{"f":"Covered interest rate parity","b":"F = S x (1 + i home) / (1 + i foreign)."},{"f":"Forward contract","b":"Agreement to exchange currency at a set rate on a future date."},{"f":"Transaction risk","b":"Exposure on a specific future foreign currency payment or receipt."},{"f":"Foreign direct investment","b":"Lasting controlling ownership of a foreign business."},{"f":"Joint venture","b":"Shared ownership of a foreign operation with a local partner."},{"f":"Political risk","b":"Loss from government action: expropriation, sanctions, capital controls."}],"pairs":[["Direct quote","Home currency per foreign unit"],["Cross rate","Rate derived from two dollar quotes"],["Appreciation","Currency buys more foreign money"],["Peg","Currency fixed to another currency"],["Purchasing power parity","Prices equalize across countries"],["Forward contract","Lock in future exchange rate"],["Foreign direct investment","Controlling stake in foreign business"],["Joint venture","Shared ownership with local partner"]],"traps":["Trap: A stronger domestic currency helps all domestic firms. Truth: It hurts exporters and helps importers and travelers abroad.","Trap: Direct and indirect quotes are interchangeable numbers. Truth: They are reciprocals; check which currency is the 'one unit'.","Trap: Hedging with a forward guarantees a better outcome. Truth: It locks in certainty; you may gain or lose versus unhedged.","Trap: Higher interest rates weaken a currency. Truth: Higher relative rates attract capital and strengthen it.","Trap: A fixed rate lets a country keep independent monetary policy and free capital flows. Truth: Impossible trinity: it can have only two."],"mnemonic":"Strong home money = Exporters Weep, Importers Cheer. Quotes: Direct = Dollars per foreign unit; Hedge a receipt by SELLing forward, a payment by BUYing forward.","cram":["Direct quote: home currency per one foreign unit; indirect is reciprocal.","Cross rate: divide the two dollar quotes.","Appreciation hurts exporters; helps importers and travelers abroad.","Higher relative interest rates raise demand for the currency.","F = S x (1 + i home)/(1 + i foreign).","Big Mac PPP: price in foreign currency divided by US price.","Receivable: sell forward. Payable: buy forward.","Entry modes: exporting, licensing, franchising, joint venture, wholly owned subsidiary."],"ytv":[{"title":"Exchange Rates Explained for Business Studies (Importers & Exporters)","channel":"BizEdMadeSimple","id":"mhBoYf0SWOI","why":"Who wins and loses when currencies move"},{"title":"Explaining Currency Appreciation - A Level and IB Economics","channel":"tutor2u","id":"OHxCDaF7YUA","why":"What causes a currency to appreciate"},{"title":"Currency Appreciation & Depreciation - How it Affects the Economy","channel":"Amit Sengupta","id":"7gMP4HFxRmw","why":"Macro effects of currency moves"}],"questions":[{"q":"For a U.S. manager, which is a DIRECT quote?","o":["$1.10 per euro","0.91 euros per dollar","150 yen per dollar","1.18 euros per pound"],"a":0,"e":"A direct quote is dollars per unit of foreign currency. The other three express foreign currency per unit of another currency, which is the indirect form for a U.S. perspective.","d":1},{"q":"If $1.10 buys one euro, how many euros does one dollar buy?","o":["About 1.10 euros","About 1.21 euros","About 0.91 euros","About 0.10 euros"],"a":2,"e":"Indirect quote = 1 / 1.10 = 0.909. Repeating 1.10 confuses the direct and indirect quotes.","d":1},{"q":"A currency appreciates when it:","o":["Buys fewer units of foreign currency than before","Is devalued by the government","Buys more units of foreign currency than before","Is quoted in a different currency"],"a":2,"e":"Appreciation is a rise in the currency's price in terms of another currency. The 'buys fewer' option describes depreciation.","d":1},{"q":"If the U.S. dollar depreciates, U.S. exports generally become:","o":["More expensive for foreign buyers","Unaffected","Cheaper for foreign buyers","Impossible to sell"],"a":2,"e":"A weaker dollar lets foreigners buy U.S. goods for less of their own currency, which helps exporters.","d":1},{"q":"Under a fixed (pegged) exchange rate system:","o":["Supply and demand alone set the rate every day","The currency can never change in value under any policy","The central bank defends a set rate against another currency","The IMF sets the rate for each member daily"],"a":2,"e":"A peg is maintained by official intervention using reserves. A fixed rate can still be devalued or revalued by policy.","d":1},{"q":"Under a floating exchange rate system, the exchange rate is determined by:","o":["Supply and demand for the currency in the forex market","The central bank's foreign reserves and interest-rate decisions alone","A fixed official formula tied to each country's gold holdings","An international treaty among members"],"a":0,"e":"Floating means the market decides. Reserves matter for a peg, and a gold formula describes the old gold standard.","d":1},{"q":"Foreign direct investment (FDI) is best described as:","o":["A lasting investment giving control of a business in another country","Buying minority shares in a foreign company for short-term trading profit","Exporting goods to a foreign distributor","Borrowing in a foreign currency"],"a":0,"e":"FDI is a controlling stake such as a new factory or an acquisition. Short-term stock buying is portfolio investment, the usual confusion.","d":1},{"q":"A U.S. company allows a foreign firm to produce its patented product for a fee. This entry mode is:","o":["Licensing","Exporting","Wholly owned subsidiary","Portfolio investment"],"a":0,"e":"Licensing grants rights to technology or brand for fees. Exporting means selling goods made at home, and a subsidiary would involve owning operations.","d":1},{"q":"The euro is $1.10 and the pound is $1.30. How many euros does one pound buy?","o":["About 1.18 euros","About 0.85 euros","About 1.43 euros","About 0.77 euros"],"a":0,"e":"Cross rate = 1.30 / 1.10 = 1.18 euros per pound. 0.85 flips the direction, and 1.43 multiplies the two quotes.","d":2},{"q":"A U.S. importer owes EUR 500,000. The euro rises from $1.10 to $1.21. How much more does the bill cost in dollars?","o":["$5,500","$110,000","$55,000","$605,000"],"a":2,"e":"500,000 x 1.21 = $605,000 versus 500,000 x 1.10 = $550,000, a difference of $55,000. $605,000 is the new total, not the increase.","d":2},{"q":"A U.S. firm sells a machine for EUR 220,000. The euro falls from $1.10 to $1.00. The firm's dollar revenue:","o":["Rises by $22,000","Falls by $22,000","Falls by $2,200","Is unchanged"],"a":1,"e":"At 1.10 revenue is $242,000; at 1.00 it is $220,000, a $22,000 drop. A euro-priced sale loses dollar value when the euro weakens.","d":2},{"q":"Which change would most likely cause the U.S. dollar to appreciate?","o":["U.S. interest rates rise relative to foreign rates","U.S. inflation rises relative to foreign inflation","Foreign investors expect the dollar to fall","U.S. imports surge while exports fall"],"a":0,"e":"Higher relative returns attract foreign capital and raise dollar demand. Higher inflation, falling expectations, and a trade deficit all point toward depreciation.","d":2},{"q":"If inflation in Country A is much higher than in Country B for several years, A's currency will tend to:","o":["Appreciate against B's currency","Depreciate against B's currency","Stay fixed","Become the world reserve currency"],"a":1,"e":"Higher inflation erodes purchasing power and competitiveness, so the currency loses value, consistent with PPP.","d":2},{"q":"The U.S. dollar strengthens sharply. Which group is most likely to be hurt?","o":["A U.S. importer that buys Italian furniture for resale","An American family vacationing in Europe","A U.S. retailer buying goods from Asia","A U.S. manufacturer that exports most of its output"],"a":3,"e":"A strong dollar makes U.S. goods pricier abroad. Importers, travelers, and retailers buying foreign goods get cheaper prices.","d":2},{"q":"The law of one price and PPP imply that, in the long run:","o":["Exchange rates are always fixed by law","All countries have the same inflation rate","Interest rates are equal everywhere in the world","Identical goods should cost the same in one currency"],"a":3,"e":"PPP says exchange rates adjust so a basket costs the same across countries. In practice transport costs and barriers keep it from holding exactly.","d":2},{"q":"A forward contract:","o":["Gives the buyer the right but not the obligation to exchange currency later","Locks in an exchange rate today for a currency exchange on a future date","Requires exchange at whatever the spot rate is later","Is a loan in a foreign currency"],"a":1,"e":"A forward is a binding agreement at a set rate. The right-without-obligation description is an option.","d":2},{"q":"A U.S. firm must pay EUR 2,000,000 to a supplier in 60 days and worries the euro will rise. A sensible hedge is to:","o":["Buy euros forward for delivery in 60 days","Sell euros forward for delivery in 60 days","Wait and buy euros at the spot rate","Ask the supplier to invoice in a third currency only"],"a":0,"e":"Buying forward fixes the dollar cost of the future payment. Selling euros forward would double the exposure instead of removing it.","d":2},{"q":"Which sequence lists entry modes from LEAST to MOST commitment and control?","o":["Wholly owned subsidiary, joint venture, licensing, exporting","Licensing, wholly owned subsidiary, exporting, joint venture","Joint venture, exporting, wholly owned subsidiary, licensing","Exporting, licensing, joint venture, wholly owned subsidiary"],"a":3,"e":"Commitment and control rise from exporting to licensing to shared ownership to full ownership. The reversed order lists them from most to least.","d":2},{"q":"A key advantage of a joint venture with a local partner is that it:","o":["Gives the foreign firm total control","Shares costs, risks, and local market knowledge","Eliminates political risk entirely","Avoids any need for investment"],"a":1,"e":"A JV pools resources and local expertise, but control is shared and conflicts can arise. It does not remove political risk.","d":2},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of political risk for a foreign investor?","o":["The local currency depreciates by 3% against the dollar this year","A competitor opens a nearby store","The host government nationalizes the firm's factory","Local interest rates rise slightly after a central bank decision"],"a":2,"e":"Expropriation or nationalization is a government action. Currency moves are financial risk, and competition or rates are ordinary business risk.","d":2},{"q":"Which statement about globalization is most accurate?","o":["It benefits every worker in every industry equally, with no losers at all","It has no effect at all on domestic wages, jobs, or relative prices","It reduces the need for foreign exchange","It cuts prices and widens markets but can displace some workers"],"a":3,"e":"Gains are broad but costs are concentrated in industries facing import competition. Claiming equal benefits to all ignores displacement.","d":2},{"q":"A firm with valuable proprietary technology wants to enter a country with weak intellectual property enforcement while keeping tight control. Which entry mode best fits?","o":["Licensing to a local manufacturer","A wholly owned subsidiary","A joint venture sharing its technology with a local partner","Franchising to independent local operators"],"a":1,"e":"Full ownership keeps the technology inside the firm's control, at the price of higher cost and risk. Licensing, joint ventures, and franchising all hand know-how to outside parties who could copy it.","d":3},{"q":"A Big Mac costs $5.00 in the U.S. and 80 pesos in Mexico; the market rate is 18 pesos per dollar. According to the Big Mac index, the peso is:","o":["Overvalued by about 11%","Undervalued by about 12.5%","Fairly valued","Undervalued by about 11%"],"a":3,"e":"Implied rate 80/5 = 16. Actual 18. (16 - 18)/18 = -11.1%, so the peso is undervalued. 12.5% divides by the wrong base, and overvalued has the sign wrong.","d":3},{"q":"Spot is $1.10 per euro; 1-year U.S. interest is 5% and euro interest is 3%. By covered interest rate parity, the 1-year forward rate is about:","o":["$1.0790 per euro","$1.1000 per euro","$1.1214 per euro","$1.1550 per euro"],"a":2,"e":"F = S x (1 + i_US)/(1 + i_EUR) = 1.10 x 1.05/1.03 = 1.1214. The higher-rate currency (dollar) trades at a forward discount, so the euro is at a premium. 1.0790 inverts the ratio.","d":3},{"q":"A U.S. exporter hedged a EUR 1,000,000 receipt at a forward rate of $1.12. At settlement the spot rate is $1.20. Compared with remaining unhedged, the hedge:","o":["Cost the firm $80,000 of potential gain","Saved the firm $80,000","Saved the firm $70,000","Had no effect on cash received"],"a":0,"e":"Hedged: $1,120,000; unhedged: $1,200,000. The difference is -$80,000. The $70,000 saving applies if spot had instead fallen to $1.05.","d":3},{"q":"A country keeps its currency pegged and allows free capital flows. According to the impossible trinity, it must give up:","o":["Its ability to trade","The peg itself","Its foreign reserves","An independent monetary policy"],"a":3,"e":"With a peg and open capital, domestic interest rates must follow the anchor country's to prevent capital flight or inflow. Reserves and trade are not what the trilemma restricts.","d":3},{"q":"The euro rises from $1.10 to $1.21. By what percentage does the DOLLAR depreciate in euro terms?","o":["10%","About 9.1%","About 11%","About 1.1%"],"a":1,"e":"Dollar value in euros: 1/1.10 = 0.9091 falls to 1/1.21 = 0.8264, a drop of 9.09%. The euro's 10% appreciation is the reciprocal view and is a different percentage.","d":3},{"q":"A U.S. firm's Mexican plant has costs of 1,800 pesos per unit. The peso appreciates from 18 to 15 pesos per dollar. The dollar cost per unit:","o":["Falls from $100 to $83","Stays $100","Rises from $100 to $108","Rises from $100 to $120"],"a":3,"e":"1,800/18 = $100 before and 1,800/15 = $120 after, a 20% increase. The peso's strengthening raises costs for a firm that earns dollars and pays pesos.","d":3},{"q":"A country devalues its peg from 10 to 12 local units per U.S. dollar. The local currency's value in dollars changes by about:","o":["+20%","-16.7%","-20%","+16.7%"],"a":1,"e":"Value in dollars goes from 1/10 = 0.1000 to 1/12 = 0.0833, a 16.7% fall. 20% is the rise in the dollar's price (12/10 - 1), measured from the other side.","d":3},{"q":"Emerging-market currencies often weaken sharply when the Fed raises U.S. rates mainly because:","o":["Emerging markets lose their ability to export","Investors shift capital toward higher U.S. yields","Local inflation always falls when the Fed hikes","Emerging markets must repay all of their debts"],"a":1,"e":"Capital flows toward higher risk-adjusted returns, so emerging-market currencies face outflows and depreciation. Exports typically benefit from a weaker currency, not suffer.","d":3}]}];